//@version=6
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  PRAGATI · प्रगति  ·  CONVICTION × VALUE OSCILLATOR                       v6
//  "Is the push paid for - and at what price?"
//
//  Two readings of one instrument, on one pane, in one colour language:
//
//      HISTOGRAM   FLOW       Pragati's conviction engine - which way the push
//                             is going, and whether it is pressing or fading
//      TRACE       POSITION   Samanvaya's unified value reading - where price
//                             stands against fair value, rich or cheap
//
//      +90  MTF CONVICTION TAPE  who controls across the ladder, and how far
//      −90  MTF VALUE TAPE       rich or cheap across the ladder, and how far
//
//  WHY THESE TWO SHARE A PANE. v5 already ran Samanvaya's engine whole (section
//  4c), and every ▲▼ already began with Samanvaya's value reading crossing back
//  through θ - the ARM - confirmed by the histogram's side. v5 drew the
//  confirmation and hid the cause. v6 draws both, so every layer a declaration
//  is made of is on the pane or its tapes. Flow and position are different
//  questions, which is why they can share one axis without competing.
//
//  WHAT CHANGED FROM v5 - AND WHAT DID NOT
//      · The pane's trace is Samanvaya's value reading, not conviction.
//      · The conviction trace and its signal line left the pane. They are still
//        the engine: the histogram is their difference, R and H are found on
//        conviction's pivots, and ◆ needs conviction above zero. Conviction is
//        in the Data Window and the panel; Nishchaya v3 draws the line itself,
//        bit-identical.
//      · The reference lines belong to the trace that is drawn: θ and ±70.
//        The conviction zones (±30 / ±60) are thresholds now, not lines.
//      · EVERY SIGNAL FIRES ON EXACTLY THE BARS v5 FIRED ON. v6 changes what is
//        drawn, not what fires, so every signal series diffs to zero against
//        v5, and any later change to the logic can be measured on its own.
//      · No new data request. Everything drawn was already computed in v5.
//
//  ═══ WHAT IS ON THE PANE, TOP TO BOTTOM ═══════════════════════════════════
//      +90   MTF conviction tape                 who controls, chart + ladder
//      +70   dashed rail · bearish R H           divergence
//      +θ    solid rail · ▼ SELL · ▽ arm · short ◆
//            θ → +70 shaded: where a ▼ can be armed
//        0   VALUE TRACE in front, HISTOGRAM behind, ⟳ basket rotation,
//            ○ lean (opt-in, on the trace)
//      −θ    solid rail · ▲ BUY · △ arm · long ◆
//            −θ → −70 shaded: where a ▲ can be armed
//      −70   dashed rail · bullish R H           divergence
//      −90   MTF value tape                      rich or cheap on the ladder
//  θ sits at ±43 at the default θ of 1.5, on Samanvaya's scale. Right edge:
//  the θ tags, each tape's name, ladder and reading (↺ = fallback direction),
//  and - only when the stack cannot judge - a gold SIGNALS PAUSED label naming
//  why. Optional panel on the right; on the price chart, the divergence
//  connectors, the volume profile and the open-interest table.
//
//  ═══ ONE CHANNEL, ONE QUESTION ════════════════════════════════════════════
//      column hue         which way conviction is being PUSHED
//      column lightness   pressing, decelerating or turning
//      column saturation  is the push real, or an amplified quiet regime
//      trace hue          WHERE price stands: GREEN rich, RED cheap
//      trace intensity    how far from fair: faint inside θ, a clear step at
//                         θ, solid toward ±70
//      trace saturation   is the value reading coherent: it dims when the RV
//                         and breadth legs point opposite ways, or while the
//                         macro basket is settling
//      MTF conviction     who controls across the ladder - with the conviction
//                         trace off the pane, this is where control is read
//      MTF value          rich or cheap across the ladder
//      △ ▽ hollow         value turned: Samanvaya's own entry, the ARM
//      ▲ ▼ solid          every layer agreed: the DECLARATION
//      pane background    the standing declaration (opt-in)
//
//  GREEN AND RED ARE DIRECTION, EVERYWHERE ON THE PANE. The trace takes the
//  value tape's polarity - rich GREEN, cheap RED - Samanvaya's colouring
//  flipped on purpose: a rally makes price rich AND pushes the columns green,
//  so the two primaries agree in hue while a move runs and SPLIT in hue when it
//  turns. Rails and zones are coloured by the EVENT they carry - sells above,
//  buys below - as in v5.
//
//  VELOCITY IS NOT IN THE TRACE. Samanvaya brightened its trace while it rose
//  because it had no histogram. Here the histogram carries the push, so the
//  trace carries position and nothing else.
//
//  HUE IS NOT CONTROL. A column is conviction minus its own signal line, so a
//  green column can sit under sellers still in control whose grip is easing.
//  Control is the conviction tape's hue, and the panel's conviction row.
//
//  GOLD, everywhere in this script, means one thing: READ WITH CAUTION. It is a
//  qualifier - warming up, a setup without evidence, a capped participation
//  weight, a quiet regime, a basket rotation, split value legs, an idle hedge, a
//  self-limiting flow, a structural OI jump, a crowded book, an unaccepted
//  price. It never means up or down. The tapes use no gold: a tape that is
//  still warming is drawn in neutral grey.
//
//  VIEW: CORE shows the instrument and its tapes. FULL adds WHERE (the volume
//  profile) and WHAT KIND (open interest) on the price chart.
//
//  ═══ THE ONE READING RULE ═════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  SAME HUE   the push is carrying price further from fair.
//  SPLIT HUE  the push is working price back toward fair.
//
//      trace          columns             reading
//      red past −θ    red, pressing       cheap and still being sold
//      red past −θ    pale, turning       selling exhausting in a dislocation
//      red → △        turning green       value crossed back, push flipped: ▲
//      green past +θ  green, pressing     rich and still bought - stretched,
//                                         not early
//      near 0         bright green, ◆     fair, the push resuming
//
//  A hollow △ that never fills is its own reading: value turned and flow did
//  not follow. None of these readings is measured.
//
//  ═══ TWO QUANTITIES, ONE AXIS ═════════════════════════════════════════════
//  The pane's scale belongs to the TRACE: Samanvaya's ±100, softbound(z · 0.5),
//  with θ and ±70 drawn. The histogram is DISPLAYED against it σ-pinned: 3σ of
//  its own distribution lands exactly on θ, so a column that reaches the θ line
//  is a 3σ push. The one line both primaries cross means something for both.
//  Display only - the engine, the triggers and the panel read the native
//  histogram, which the Data Window carries. 'Fixed gain' restores v5's gain.
//
//  The histogram is drawn BEHIND the trace: a line in front of columns stays
//  whole; columns in front of a line cut it into dashes.
//
//  ═══ WHERE SAMANVAYA'S PANE WENT ══════════════════════════════════════════
//      entry triangles     → hollow △ ▽ on the θ rail at the ARM bar
//      divergence circles  → retired. R already requires the value tape on the
//                            stretched side at the pivot, and a second,
//                            unmeasured family would compete with the measured
//                            one on the same rails
//      legs disagree ×     → the trace's saturation, and the panel
//      RV and breadth legs → the panel
//      basket rotation ⟳   → kept, gold, on the zero line: no ▲▼ can arm for
//                            the 21 bars after it
//      exit crossing       → retired. A declaration has no exit
//      zone background     → retired. The pane has one background channel
//      Siddhi × trace ◆    → ○ LEAN, opt-in, off. At the defaults Samanvaya's
//                            embedded Siddhi line is this script's own signal
//                            line, so it is computed from that, not from a
//                            second engine. Its timing depends on two unrelated
//                            scalings - tanh/3σ against softbound · 0.5 - and
//                            one of its two lines is no longer drawn, so the ○
//                            sits on the trace where the crossing happened.
//                            Measured in Samanvaya: +0.0273R, smaller than a
//                            daily round-trip cost. A lean, never an entry.
//      stationarity, cost  → not carried; declarations are not spread trades
//
//  ═══ THE SETTINGS, IN MENU ORDER ══════════════════════════════════════════
//      1 · Conviction engine        the engine behind the histogram: lookback,
//                                   smoothing, signal, normalization,
//                                   participation
//      2 · MTF tapes                which ladder each tape reads
//      3 · Value model · Samanvaya  macro hedge, model clock, leg mix, θ, custom
//                                   driver - everything that moves the trace
//                                   and the value tape
//      4 · Signals · ▲ ▼ ◆          the signal families, their windows, the arm
//                                   marks and the lean
//      5 · Divergence · R H         the pivot engine and its price connectors
//      6 · Conviction thresholds    inner and outer - thresholds, not lines
//      7 · Display                  view, markers, histogram, trace, palette
//      8 · Information panel
//      9 · Volume profile           price-chart overlay, display only
//     10 · Open interest            price-chart readout, display only
//
//  ═══ THE TAPES ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  The tapes ask the two primaries' questions across a ladder of timeframes,
//  in the same colour language: hue is direction, INTENSITY is how far it has
//  run, pale is weak.
//
//  MTF CONVICTION TAPE (+90) - WHO CONTROLS. Pragati's own ladder, the chart's
//      conviction included as one rung. Hue: which side controls across the
//      frames. Intensity: graded on conviction's zones - faint inside the inner
//      zone, a clear step brighter past it, solid past the outer zone. With
//      Ladder down (default) it is the current inside each bar - faster than
//      the chart's conviction; with Ladder up, the tide the signals swim in.
//
//          ◆ needs it past the inner zone on the ◆'s side
//          ▲▼ need it on the signal's side, or turning toward it
//          R needs it to fail to confirm the swing, as chart conviction did
//
//  MTF VALUE TAPE (−90) - WHERE PRICE STANDS, ACROSS HORIZONS. Samanvaya's MTF
//      bias, carried in whole (section 4c): the RV + breadth composite on the
//      chart and on every rung, averaged. The trace IS its chart rung. Hue:
//      GREEN rich, RED cheap, as the trace. Intensity: faint inside θ, a clear
//      step at it, solid toward ±70. The frames must agree for it to brighten -
//      a bright value tape is a dislocation every horizon sees, not one.
//
//          trace past θ, tape pale      a dislocation on this chart alone
//          trace and tape solid,        a dislocation every horizon saw -
//            same hue                   what ▲▼ require
//          both tapes solid, same hue   control already paid for across
//                                       horizons - stretched, not early
//
//  Every signal ENFORCES both tapes (see THE SIGNALS). None of it is measured.
//
//  ═══ TWO DIRECTIONS, ONE SETTING PER TAPE ════════════════════════════════
//  Each tape has its own ladder input, so they can read opposite directions.
//
//  MTF CONVICTION · "MTF conviction ladder" · default LADDER DOWN.
//      Down asks whether the chart's conviction is BUILT from the horizons
//      inside it: every chart bar opened up at each finer resolution. Up asks
//      whether it is HELD by the horizons that contain it.
//  MTF VALUE · "MTF value ladder" · default LADDER UP, Samanvaya's own default.
//      Up carries the WHOLE composite - RV leg and breadth - on every higher
//      frame, so it asks whether the dislocation stands on the horizons that
//      contain the chart. Down is breadth only.
//
//  The defaults therefore read THREE HORIZONS: conviction from INSIDE the bar,
//  the chart itself (the histogram and the trace), and value from ABOVE it.
//  Every lower frame's window is shorter than the chart's and every higher
//  frame's longer, so a ladder direction is also a SPEED: down is faster than
//  the chart, up is slower. The signal rules say what each condition means in
//  both directions (THE SIGNALS).
//
//  FALLBACK. A direction with no frames on this chart - Ladder up on 4h, 45m
//  or monthly, Ladder down on 1m - reads the other direction instead of pausing
//  every signal, and the tape's label shows ↺.
//
//  ═══ THE LADDER ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  A FIXED LADDER OF STANDARD FRAMES, the same on every venue. A frame is used
//  only if the larger of the two holds a whole number of the smaller's bars -
//  a reconstruction that split bars across bars would be inventing data.
//
//      chart   LADDER DOWN                                LADDER UP
//      3m      1m · 3m                                    4h · 1h · 30m · 15m · 3m
//      5m      1m · 5m                                    4h · 1h · 30m · 15m · 5m
//      15m     1m · 3m · 5m · 15m                         4h · 1h · 30m · 15m
//      30m     1m · 3m · 5m · 15m · 30m                   4h · 1h · 30m
//      1h      1m · 3m · 5m · 15m · 30m · 1h              4h · 1h
//      4h      1m · 3m · 5m · 15m · 30m · 1h · 4h         none
//      D       1m · 3m · 5m · 15m · 30m · 1h · 4h · D     W · D
//      W       1h · 4h · D · W                            none
//
//  1m has nothing below it; 4h and weekly nothing above within the ladder's
//  ceiling. A direction with no rungs falls back to the other. Weekly and
//  monthly looking down stop short of the finest frames: TradingView caps the
//  intrabars one request carries, and a 1m rung under a weekly bar would cover
//  only its last few months.
//
//  THE VALUE TAPE KEEPS SAMANVAYA'S OWN RUNGS, on its own input. Up: the same
//  frames as above, plus M · W on a weekly chart. Down: only the THREE frames
//  nearest below the chart (daily reads 30m · 1h · 4h · D), breadth leg only -
//  the RV leg spends 22 data requests and cannot be re-run per rung, and each
//  lower rung is seven full MSF engines running on intrabars.
//
//  SESSION ENDS. 1h and 4h do not divide every session - on NSE the last 1h bar
//  runs 15:15-15:30. Every frame's real bar boundaries are read from that frame
//  itself, and participation weighting mutes a short bar that carried little.
//
//  EQUAL WEIGHT PER RUNG. The standard frames are not evenly spaced, so the tape
//  leans toward wherever the rungs are denser. That is the price of a fixed,
//  familiar ladder, stated rather than hidden. Rungs are not independent
//  evidence: a 5m window sits inside the 4h one. Pramana does the whitened
//  composite; this does structure.
//
//  ═══ HOW A LOWER FRAME IS READ ════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Sampling a lower frame at the chart bar's close would describe only its last
//  few bars - the final twenty minutes of a day, on a 1m rung. So each lower
//  frame runs the full engine on its own history, and the chart bar takes the
//  participation-weighted AVERAGE of that frame's reading across all of its bars
//  inside the chart bar: the bar seen from inside. request.security_lower_tf
//  delivers every intrabar of every closed bar and the intrabars so far on the
//  live one - no repaint. Its depth is capped (about 100,000 intrabars per
//  request on most plans), so the finest rungs reach back less far than the
//  chart; older bars run without them.
//
//  ═══ HOW A HIGHER FRAME IS READ ═══════════════════════════════════════════
//  A higher frame read the usual way is a staircase, or it repaints. So each is
//  RECONSTRUCTED on the chart: from the parent comes only its SETTLED state as
//  of its last closed bar - rolling sums, participation baselines, the sums
//  behind its σ, its last close - and from the chart comes the parent bar now
//  FORMING, aggregated from the chart's own bars. The parent's arithmetic is
//  finished every chart bar, lands on the parent's settled value at its close,
//  and is identical in history and realtime. The panel's 'rungs live' row
//  reports how closely it landed, in points (REC).
//
//  ═══ HOW THE TAPES ARE BUILT ══════════════════════════════════════════════
//      conviction  z = raw / 3σ per rung, σ over its own window
//                  100 · tanh( mean of z over the ladder, the chart included )
//      value       z = Samanvaya's unified z per rung (RV ⊕ breadth, blended
//                      in z-space with the leg correlation measured per rung)
//                  100 · softbound( 0.5 · mean of z over the ladder, chart included )
//
//  Both are fused in linear z-space and compressed once, with no second
//  normalization, so agreement across frames is RARER than any one frame's
//  reading - a bright tape means the ladder is stretched, not one horizon. A
//  rung whose history begins later joins when it can calibrate and moves the
//  level, never the scale. The value tape and the trace also need Samanvaya's
//  model built - ten anchor periods past a twelve-period hold-out - before
//  either is drawn in colour; until then the trace is not drawn at all, because
//  a flat line at zero would read as a neutral statement about the market.
//
//  VALUE ON EVERY TIMEFRAME. Samanvaya's model takes one observation per closed
//  anchor period (a day, by default). Each observation is the SUM of the chart-
//  bar returns inside that period - the period's own return - for the target
//  and every driver alike. Taking the return of the period's LAST chart bar is
//  the period's return only on a daily chart: on a 5m chart the whole macro fit
//  would run on one closing 5-minute return a day, the hedge's measured skill
//  collapses, and value carries no macro information. The intercept is spread
//  over the bars a period actually holds. The panel's 'hedge strength' row
//  shows how much of value is relative value on this chart.
//
//  ═══ THE SIGNALS · THREE FAMILIES ══════════════════════════════════════════
//  INDICATOR SIGNALS, NOT TRADES. A ▲▼ is a DECLARATION about the market at
//  that bar; it stands until the opposite one and has no exit. Every signal
//  needs every one of its layers; nothing is optional. v6 fires every one of
//  them on exactly the bars v5 did:
//
//      CHART        this timeframe: conviction (the histogram, its zones and
//                   pivots, the impulse gate), the TRACE - Samanvaya's value
//                   on this chart - and effort → result
//      CONVICTION   the +90 tape, C
//      VALUE        the −90 tape, V - rich (+) or cheap (−), judged against θ
//
//  1 · DECLARATION  ▲ ▼   Samanvaya's triangle, read through all of Pragati
//      ARM        the TRACE crosses UP through −θ with its basket settled -
//                 drawn as a hollow △ on the −θ rail. Falling back below −θ
//                 cancels the arm.
//      CONFIRM    within the confirmation window, on one closed bar:
//        VALUE · LADDER    V ≤ −θ inside the dislocation window, V < +θ now
//        CONV · CHART      histogram > 0
//        CONV · LADDER     C > 0, or C rising two bars running
//        EFFORT → RESULT   participation absorbed at some point in the
//                          dislocation window (switchable)
//      In one line: cheap across the frames, effort exhausted, and buyers
//      starting to do the work - on this chart and on the ladder. On the pane:
//      a red trace crossing back up through −θ while the columns turn green.
//
//  2 · CONTINUATION  ◆   the pullback reset value without breaking the push
//      CHART        conviction > 0, histogram went < 0 inside the pullback
//                   window, then crosses +k·σ; participation NOT absorbed
//      CONVICTION   C ≥ +inner zone
//      VALUE        V < +θ
//
//  3 · DIVERGENCE  R H   the chart's pivot pattern, confirmed by the tapes
//      R bearish    price higher high, conviction lower high (zone gate on);
//                   C also a lower high; V > 0 at the pivot
//      H bearish    price lower high, conviction higher high; C ≤ −inner zone;
//                   V > −θ
//
//  WHAT C MEANS FOLLOWS ITS DIRECTION - the rules were checked for both:
//      LADDER DOWN (default)  C is the inside of the bars, faster than chart
//          conviction. ▲ 'C rising' = the inside of the bars turning first. ◆
//          'C ≥ inner zone' = the push BUILT at every finer frame, not one
//          print. R 'C a lower high' = the second high carried less conviction
//          inside the bars too - partly a restatement of the chart, since the
//          chart's own reading is one of C's rungs. H = the inside of the bars
//          in control.
//      LADDER UP  C is the frames above, slower than the chart: control HELD by
//          the larger frames, and R the larger frames failing to confirm.
//      With the defaults the view from above comes from V (value ↑), so every
//      signal spans three horizons: inside the bar, the chart, above it.
//
//  BREADTH STAYS INSIDE VALUE, by decision. It is momentum and overlaps C; the
//  panel's 'layer overlap' row measures by how much instead of assuming it
//  away. It stays because the blend is Samanvaya's measured best, because the
//  value ladder below the chart is breadth only, and because the anchor fix
//  (VALUE ON EVERY TIMEFRAME) gives value its macro information back.
//
//  Every bullish rule mirrors its bearish one. A COOLDOWN applies per
//  direction (▲ and long ◆ share one clock); a triangle takes precedence over
//  a diamond on the same bar.
//
//  NOTHING PAUSES SILENTLY. A tape warming or switched Off, no frames on this
//  chart after FALLBACK, or Samanvaya's model still building pauses every
//  signal, and a gold SIGNALS PAUSED label at the pane's right edge names the
//  layer. A ▲▼ also cannot arm while the basket settles after a rotation - the
//  ⟳ on the zero line marks where that clock restarted.
//
//  FREE PARAMETERS - none fitted for this stack: θ (1.5); the inner zone
//  reused as C's threshold (C averages several z's, so it reaches the zone less
//  often than chart conviction); 'rising two bars'; the dislocation window
//  (20); the confirmation window (5); the absorption cut (bottom fifth); V > 0
//  for R; the cooldown (10); the pullback window (6).
//
//  UNMEASURED AS A STACK. Every figure in EVIDENCE below was measured on the
//  chart's conviction layer alone. Samanvaya's figures are the information
//  coefficient of its oscillator LEVEL on weekly data - not of the crossing as
//  an event, and not intraday.
//
//  ═══ HOW TO READ IT ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ZERO LINE - fair value for the trace, the base for the columns. Context,
//      not an event.
//
//  θ - Samanvaya's entry threshold. Beyond it price is dislocated on this
//      chart; the trace crossing BACK through it is what arms a ▲▼.
//
//  CONVICTION ZONES - not drawn. Beyond the inner zone the window's effort is
//      heavily one-sided; beyond the outer it is near-uniform, where trends run
//      and also end. They gate R, set C's thresholds for ◆ and H, and grade the
//      conviction tape. The panel's conviction row reports where it stands.
//
//  DIVERGENCE - the primary edge in this construction, found on conviction.
//      REGULAR BEARISH   price higher high, conviction lower high  → reversal
//      REGULAR BULLISH   price lower low,   conviction higher low  → reversal
//      HIDDEN  BEARISH   price lower high,  conviction higher high → continuation
//      HIDDEN  BULLISH   price higher low,  conviction lower low   → continuation
//
//  THE STRONGEST READING is a regular divergence formed in a zone, confirmed by
//  the ladder, followed by a ▲▼ in the same direction - the chart, the ladder's
//  conviction and value across horizons all turning together. Read, not
//  enforced: the R and the triangle are separate families.
//
//  WITH THE PROFILE ON, read the divergence against the structure too: a high in
//  a low-volume node is distribution into thin air; the same high at the point
//  of control was made where the market trades willingly. A reading only.
//
//  ═══ READING THE RAILS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//      TAPE          +90   MTF conviction - control across the ladder
//      OUTER DASHED  ±70   divergences     R regular        H hidden
//      SOLID θ       ±θ    signals         ▲▼ declaration   △▽ arm   ◆ continuation
//      TAPE          −90   MTF value - rich (green) / cheap (red) across it
//
//  Bearish events on the upper rail, bullish on the lower. Divergence markers
//  sit on the CONFIRMATION bar: nothing is drawn before it is known and nothing
//  is revised afterwards. Their connectors are on the PRICE chart, joining the
//  two swing extremes the divergence compares.
//
//  ═══ WHAT IT MEASURES ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  CONVICTION - how much of a bar's travel became net displacement:
//
//      c = ( C − C[1] ) / TR        bounded −1 … +1
//
//  PARTICIPATION - volume relative to its own recent average, capped:
//
//      w = min( V / EMA(V), cap )    relative TRUE RANGE where there is no volume
//
//  THE RAW SHARE, chosen by the Denominator input:
//
//      AGREEMENT   raw = 100 × Σ ( c · w ) / Σ ( |c| · w )
//      EFFORT      raw = 100 × Σ ( c · w ) / Σ w
//
//  AGREEMENT is Chande's form, the RSI family, on range-normalised closes
//  weighted by relative volume: how CONSISTENTLY bars resolve one way. EFFORT
//  divides by total participation - Wyckoff's law literally - so a heavy bar
//  that goes nowhere dilutes the reading. Every rung of the ladder uses the
//  same form, so the conviction tape and the histogram are the same quantity.
//
//  SCALING. conviction = 100 · tanh( raw / 3σ ), σ over the normalization
//  window: bounded, smooth, calibrated so the inner zone holds about a third of
//  the time and the outer about four percent (measured: 3.2-4.3%). The
//  histogram is conviction minus its signal EMA.
//
//  VALUE - the trace - is section 4c: Y hedged against a selected macro basket
//  in proportion to the hedge's measured skill, scored over a timescale
//  ensemble, blended in z-space with seven price-only breadth views, bounded
//  once: 100 · softbound( 0.5 · z ).
//
//  ═══ WHAT TO KNOW BEFORE LEANING ON IT ════════════════════════════════════
//  · PIVOTS CONFIRM LATE, BY CONSTRUCTION. Not repainting - the cost of knowing
//    a pivot was a pivot.
//  · SCALING IS RELATIVE. A conviction reading is extreme FOR THIS INSTRUMENT
//    over the window. In a dead range small imbalances are amplified - the
//    columns desaturate when that happens, and the panel shows the raw share.
//  · CAPPING PARTICIPATION MATTERS. Expiry days, rebalances and block prints
//    would otherwise dominate a window for its whole length.
//  · THE WINDOW IS THE HORIZON. A 20-bar lookback is a swing instrument on daily
//    bars and a scalping one on 5-minute bars - which is what the tapes are for.
//  · SCALED, NOT CENTRED. Zero keeps meaning "neither side in control", so on a
//    persistently trending instrument one side's zone is visited more often.
//  · TWO SCALES, ONE AXIS. A column touching ±70 or a tape means nothing; read
//    the columns against zero and θ, the trace against zero, θ and ±70.
//  · THE TRACE IS THIS SCRIPT'S PORT OF SAMANVAYA, with its audit: anchor-period
//    returns, the intercept spread over the bars a period holds, hedge skill
//    measured per period, DRIVER TIMING, and Y read as the chart's own close. A
//    standalone Samanvaya without all five will not match the trace intraday,
//    nor on a daily chart where a driver closes after the chart does.
//
//  ═══ EVIDENCE ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Conviction was measured on the single-frame engine, published as Nishchaya
//  v3, under the Agreement denominator, which is the default. The tapes, the
//  signal stack and the pairing of the two primaries on one pane are unmeasured.
//
//  METHOD. Every signal entered at the NEXT bar's open, bracketed at two ATR of
//  target against one ATR of stop, twenty bars maximum, stop assumed first when a
//  bar spans both. Edge in R over a baseline that applies the identical bracket
//  to every bar in both directions. Fitted on the first 60% of each series,
//  tested on the last 40%, re-tested on eight instruments never used in fitting.
//  26 years of daily data, roughly 730 days of hourly, 60 days of 5- and
//  15-minute, on GC, SI, CL, HG, NG, ES, SPY, QQQ, TLT, 6E and BTC.
//
//  WHAT SURVIVED.
//      · Regular divergence RANKED first: the only element positive on both the
//        primary futures and the held-out instruments in nearly every
//        configuration tested. Ranked, not established.
//      · Participation weighting earns its place. Off is the worst setting in
//        all three signal architectures tested.
//      · The adaptive scaling is calibrated: 3.2-4.3% beyond the outer zone.
//
//  WHAT DID NOT.
//      · The chart-only reversal trigger had no edge (+0.0003R, t=0.01) - the
//        reason no ▲▼ fires on the conviction layer alone.
//      · Hidden divergence measured no edge on any universe. Default-off.
//      · Continuation: +0.036R on the primary futures, -0.016R elsewhere.
//      · NOTHING reaches significance once overlapping windows are accounted
//        for. Best of 48 cells was t = 1.9 - what noise alone produces against
//        an expected null maximum near 2.2. The modern bar is t > 3 (Harvey, Liu
//        and Zhu 2016). What the evidence supports is "no demonstrated edge".
//
//  THE TRACE'S EVIDENCE IS SAMANVAYA'S: the information coefficient of its
//  LEVEL at a 4-week horizon on weekly data across 24 targets, +0.0691 for the
//  RV + breadth blend against +0.0637 and +0.0598 for either leg alone - better
//  on both mean and median, none of the differences individually significant.
//  Directional, not decisive. Nothing about the trace crossing θ as an event.
//
//  GLYPH INTENSITY FOLLOWS EVIDENCE WEIGHT. R, the measured element, and the
//  ▲▼ are drawn solid; the instrument-dependent ◆ and the edgeless H lighter;
//  the arm hollow, because it is one layer of a ▲▼, not a verdict.
//
//  ON OPTIMISING THE INPUTS. 900 randomised parameter sets: fitted edge and
//  unseen edge correlate at about zero. Lookback at or above 20 and the inner
//  zone at or below 40 were the only preferences that held. Do not tune this to
//  a backtest. It will not hold.
//
//  COSTS DECIDE THE TIMEFRAME. At one ATR of stop a round trip costs about 0.02R
//  daily, 0.045R hourly, 0.05R on 15m and 0.09R on 5m, against a best measured
//  edge near 0.05R. Daily is the only timeframe with real headroom - and the
//  reason to test whether the tapes help a lower-timeframe trader pick the
//  signals worth paying for.
//
//  ═══ REPAINT POSTURE ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  The histogram, the trace and both tapes are live readings: they move while
//  the chart bar forms and are final at its close. Higher frames enter only as
//  their last CLOSED bar plus chart bars that exist; lower frames only as
//  intrabars that exist. History and realtime compute the same numbers.
//  Triggers, divergence markers and connectors, the ▲▼ arms and declarations,
//  the arm marks, rotation ticks and leans, the declaration background and all
//  alerts are committed on closed bars only. The volume profile's developing
//  session is the one disclosed exception. The trace and the value tape inherit
//  Samanvaya's posture: its macro drivers use lookahead_off with a confirmed-bar
//  fallback, and its anchor samples come from CLOSED periods. DRIVER TIMING: on
//  a daily chart many drivers close hours after the chart does - TVC:US10Y
//  about eleven hours after NSE - so a driver whose bar closes more than a third
//  of a bar late is read at its previous close, the value known at the chart's
//  close. The panel's 'driver timing' row shows what was lagged and what
//  lateness was kept - the most the last bar can revise by.
//
//  ═══ DATA REQUESTS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Pine allows 40 unique requests. v6 adds none. Samanvaya's engine spends 22
//  (the macro symbols; Y is the chart's own close) on every bar; the rest
//  depend on the two ladder settings.
//  Conviction: up to 7 rungs down, 5 up. Value: up to 3 down, 5 up. They never
//  peak together - the most any chart carries across both is 10 (daily with
//  both down: 7 + 3; 1m with both up: 5 + 5; the defaults peak at 8). Plus 1
//  for the profile's intrabars and 2 for open interest (the companion _OI
//  series, read inside one request on its own period). At most 35 execute on
//  any chart and any combination - close to the ceiling, so nothing more should
//  be requested here. FALLBACK swaps a direction for the other, never adds one.
//
//  ═══ DRAWING BUDGETS ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Divergence connectors are drawn on the price chart (force_overlay), capped
//  at sixty and recycled explicitly, so the profile's level lines cannot evict
//  them. The naked-POC ledger is capped by input.
//
//  PLOT COUNT: 64 allowed, and the compiler charges TWO for any colour that is
//  not a literal constant - the theme-aware palette included, not only colours
//  that change bar to bar. So the θ rails and the value action zones are
//  DRAWINGS (lines and boxes extended both ways, redrawn on the last bar), not
//  plots; the ±70 rails and the zero line are hlines; both continuation markers
//  share one plot; the impulse band uses a literal colour; the Data Window
//  carries only the engine's own numbers (the panel carries the rest); and the
//  lean's alerts use alert(), which does not count. The eight signal alerts are
//  alertcondition(), which does. About 55 of 64.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// NAMING. The line runs Siddhi → Nishchaya → Dhṛti → Pragati. Siddhi claimed
// attainment, a result. Nishchaya named what one window measures: resolve.
// Dhṛti named what the ladder tests: whether that resolve holds. Pragati names
// what all of it measures at the root - c = ΔC / TR, how much of a bar's travel
// became progress. v6 asks the other half of that question on the same pane:
// progress, and the price it was made at. Samanvaya - integration - is inside
// it as the value progress is judged against. The engine is unchanged.
// max_bars_back=2500 is Samanvaya's, carried in with its engine (section 4c).
indicator("Pragati · Conviction × Value Oscillator", shorttitle="Pragati", overlay=false,
     precision=1, max_bars_back=2500, max_lines_count=500, max_labels_count=100, max_boxes_count=500)

// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  INPUTS
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
var string G_E  = "1 · Conviction engine"
var string G_M  = "2 · MTF tapes"
var string G_S  = "3 · Value model · Samanvaya"
var string G_T  = "4 · Signals · ▲ ▼ ◆"
var string G_V  = "5 · Divergence · R H"
var string G_Z  = "6 · Conviction thresholds"
var string G_D  = "7 · Display"
var string G_P  = "8 · Information panel"

// ── 1 · conviction engine: the measurement behind the histogram ─────────────
int    inpLen  = input.int(20, "Lookback", minval=3, maxval=200, group=G_E, tooltip="How many bars of effort the conviction engine accounts for. This is the horizon of the histogram and of every conviction signal.\n\nShorter turns faster and produces more signals, most of them noise. Longer produces fewer and better ones but sits at extremes for long stretches during trends.\n\nMatch it to your holding period.", display=display.none)
int    inpSm   = input.int(3, "Smoothing", minval=1, maxval=20, group=G_E, tooltip="Final EMA on conviction. Its real job is the divergence engine: pivots detected on a jagged series produce spurious highs and lows and therefore spurious divergences.\n\n1 disables it. Above about 5 the pivots start arriving materially late.", display=display.none)
int    inpSig  = input.int(9, "Signal EMA", minval=1, maxval=50, group=G_E, tooltip="The reference line. The histogram is the gap between conviction and this.\n\nNeither line is drawn in v6 - the pane's trace is value. Conviction is in the Data Window and the panel; the signal line is conviction minus the native histogram.\n\nA plain crossing of these two is NOT a signal here - two lines near zero cross endlessly. The ◆ needs the gap to cross a magnitude threshold measured in its own standard deviations, after a genuine pullback; the ▲▼ read only which side of zero the gap is on.", display=display.none)
// The "Raw share" scaling option is GONE, and its removal is a fix rather than a
// simplification. Selecting it left the zones at ±30/±60 on a series that lives
// inside roughly ±15, so nothing ever armed, no gated divergence passed, and the
// reversal trigger could not fire - three of the four signal channels went silent
// with nothing on the pane to say why. Its stated purpose was to sanity-check the
// adaptive reading against absolute conviction, and the DETAIL panel's
// "raw share · σ" row already does exactly that without disabling anything. A
// setting whose only other effect is to break the engine is a trap, not a choice.
int    inpNorm = input.int(200, "Normalization window", minval=30, maxval=1000, group=G_E, tooltip="Sample used for the scaling σ and for the trigger's magnitude threshold.\n\nLonger is stabler and slower to acknowledge that the instrument has changed character. Shorter adapts quickly but makes both the zones and the trigger threshold move under your feet.", display=display.none)
string inpPart = input.string("Auto", "Participation", options=["Auto", "Volume", "True range", "Off"], group=G_E, tooltip="What weights each bar's conviction.\n\nAUTO uses volume where the symbol has it and relative true range where it does not, so index spot works without configuration.\n\nVOLUME is the intended input - it is what makes effort-without-result detectable at all.\n\nTRUE RANGE is the fallback: a bar that covered unusual ground counts for more. Weaker, because a wide range is already partly in the conviction term.\n\nOFF weights every bar equally. Useful as a control - if the reading barely changes when you switch participation off, volume is telling you nothing on this instrument.\n\nUNDER THE AGREEMENT DENOMINATOR, participation only re-weights up-bars against down-bars: if every bar in the window closes the same way, the weights cancel and volume drops out entirely. Under EFFORT it also dilutes bars that went nowhere.", display=display.none)
string inpDen  = input.string("Agreement", "Denominator", options=["Agreement", "Effort"], group=G_E, tooltip="What the signed, participation-weighted result is divided by.\n\nAGREEMENT (default, and the only form the published evidence was measured on): Σ|c|·w. Reads how consistently bars resolve one way. A bar that goes nowhere drops out of both sides, so heavy volume with no progress is INVISIBLE to it.\n\nEFFORT: Σw, total participation. Wyckoff's law literally - heavy bars that go nowhere dilute the reading toward zero, the way Twiggs and Chaikin money flow divide by total volume. Untested: measure it on your own universe before relying on it.\n\nThe zones, triggers and divergence engine run unchanged on either; the adaptive scaling recalibrates automatically. The panel's effort → result row shows the absorption reading under both.", display=display.none)
int    inpVN   = input.int(20, "Participation baseline", minval=5, maxval=200, group=G_E, tooltip="Averaging length for the volume or range baseline. Each bar is weighted by its participation relative to this.", display=display.none)
float  inpCap  = input.float(3.0, "Participation cap", minval=1.0, maxval=10.0, step=0.5, group=G_E, tooltip="Ceiling on the participation weight.\n\nExpiry days, index rebalances and block prints generate volume many multiples of normal. Uncapped, a single print would dominate the entire window for its full length and conviction would be reporting that one bar.", display=display.none)

// ── 2 · MTF tapes: which ladder each tape reads ──────────────────────────────
string inpTape = input.string("Ladder down", "MTF conviction ladder", options=["Ladder down", "Ladder up", "Off"], group=G_M, tooltip="Which ladder of timeframes feeds the MTF CONVICTION tape (+90) - Pragati's own. The MTF VALUE tape has its own setting just below.\n\nLADDER DOWN (default) - every standard frame BELOW the chart: 15m reads 1m · 3m · 5m · 15m, daily reads 1m through 4h, then D. Each lower frame is its conviction averaged across its own bars inside the chart bar, weighted by participation. Every lower frame's window is SHORTER than the chart's, so this is a FASTER read than the chart's own conviction: whether it is BUILT from the bars inside it - it sees a turn inside the bars before the chart does.\n\nLADDER UP - the chart and the standard frames ABOVE it, up to 4h: 5m reads 4h · 1h · 30m · 15m · 5m, daily reads W · D. A SLOWER read: whether the conviction is HELD by the frames that contain the chart.\n\nWith the conviction trace off the pane, this tape is where control is read.\n\nThe signal rules are the same in both directions; what each condition MEANS follows the direction, and the header's SIGNALS section spells out both.\n\nFALLBACK - if the chosen direction has no frames on this chart (Ladder up on 4h or weekly, Ladder down on 1m), the other direction is read and the tape's label shows ↺. Nothing pauses silently.\n\nOFF - no conviction tape. Every signal needs both tapes, so with this Off no signal can fire.\n\nUnmeasured.", display=display.none)
string inpVLad = input.string("Ladder up", "MTF value ladder", options=["Ladder up", "Ladder down", "Off"], group=G_M, tooltip="Which ladder of timeframes feeds the MTF VALUE tape (−90) - Samanvaya's MTF bias, with Samanvaya's own rung choice. The trace on the pane is its chart rung.\n\nLADDER UP (default, as in Samanvaya) - the chart and the standard frames above it, 4h the intraday ceiling: 5m reads 4h · 1h · 30m · 15m · 5m, daily reads W · D, weekly reads M · W. Every higher rung is the WHOLE composite: the RV leg exactly and the breadth leg at its last closed bar. A SLOWER read: where the frames that contain the chart say price stands.\n\nLADDER DOWN - the three standard frames nearest below the chart, breadth leg only.\n\nWith the defaults the stack reads THREE HORIZONS: inside the bar (conviction ↓), the chart itself (histogram and trace), and the frames above it (value ↑).\n\nFALLBACK - if the chosen direction has no frames on this chart (Ladder up on 4h, 45m or monthly), the other direction is read and the label shows ↺.\n\nOFF - no value tape; the trace is still drawn. Every signal needs both tapes, so with this Off no signal can fire.\n\nColours as the trace: GREEN = rich, RED = cheap.", display=display.none)

// ── 3 · value model: Samanvaya's own inputs, the ones that move its composite ─
string hedgeMode = input.string("Auto (measured)", "Macro hedge", options=["Auto (measured)", "Off", "Full"], group=G_S, tooltip="How much of the macro hedge to apply in the RV leg.\n\nAUTO scales it by the hedge's own rolling out-of-sample R², so the instrument decides. Across 24 targets this beat a full hedge on both mean and median.\n\nOFF removes the macro layer - the RV leg becomes Y's own cumulative return, and the trace is then a price-only reading.\n\nFULL forces the complete hedge regardless of measured skill. Correct when you know the relationship holds.", display=display.none)
string anchorSel = input.string("Daily (works on every timeframe)", "Model clock", options=["Daily (works on every timeframe)", "Weekly (stronger, needs daily+ charts)"], group=G_S, tooltip="Clock on which the RV leg's driver selection and hedge ratios are estimated, then applied to the chart.\n\nDAILY works everywhere. WEEKLY measured better (mean IC +0.060 vs +0.047) but needs a daily-or-higher chart: on a 5-minute chart there are too few weekly boundaries to build anything.", display=display.none)
float  legMix    = input.float(0.5, "Leg mix · RV ← → breadth", minval=0.0, maxval=1.0, step=0.05, group=G_S, tooltip="Weight on the RELATIVE VALUE leg. 0.5 is the equal integration Samanvaya measured best. 1.0 is the RV leg alone, 0.0 the breadth leg alone. With the value ladder on Ladder down the rungs are breadth only whatever this is set to; the trace always uses the mix.\n\nBREADTH STAYS IN BY DESIGN. The breadth leg is momentum and overlaps conviction - the panel's 'layer overlap' row shows by how much. It stays because the equal blend is Samanvaya's measured best (+0.0691 against +0.0637 for the RV leg alone), the only measured part of the value side; because below the chart the value ladder is breadth only, so removing it would leave Ladder down with no ladder; and because the anchor fix gives the RV leg its macro information back on every timeframe, so value is no longer momentum alone. Set 1.0 to see value without momentum.", display=display.none)
float  entryThresh = input.float(1.5, "θ · value threshold", minval=0.5, maxval=4.0, step=0.1, group=G_S, tooltip="Samanvaya's entry threshold θ, in |z|. DRAWN: the solid ±θ lines are the pane's signal rail.\n\nIt is the level the trace must cross back through to arm a ▲▼, the dislocation level the MTF value tape is judged against by ▲▼, ◆ and H, the knee of the trace's and the value tape's shading - faint inside θ, a clear step brighter at it, solid toward ±70 - and, with the σ-pinned histogram, the height of a 3σ column.\n\nLiterature standard for a mean-reversion entry is 1.5 to 2.5.", display=display.none)
bool   inpCustom = input.bool(false, "Add a custom macro driver", group=G_S, tooltip="Adds your own symbol as a ninth candidate factor in the RV leg. Never the target's own volatility index - it would hedge the target against itself.", display=display.none)
string usy_sym   = input.symbol("", "    Custom driver symbol", group=G_S, tooltip="Leave empty unless you have a specific economic reason.", display=display.none)

// ── 4 · signals ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bool   inpState = input.bool(true, "Declarations  ▲ ▼", group=G_T, tooltip="The ▲▼ DECLARATIONS - Samanvaya's triangle, read through everything Pragati knows. A ▲ declares BUY, a ▼ declares SELL; a declaration stands until the opposite one. It is a statement about the market at that bar, not a position, and it has no exit.\n\nHOW A ▲ IS MADE (▼ mirrors):\n  1 · ARM      the TRACE - Samanvaya's value reading on this chart - crosses back UP through −θ with its basket settled: price leaving a dislocation. Drawn as a hollow △ on the −θ rail. A fall back below −θ cancels the arm; the next crossing re-arms it.\n  2 · CONFIRM  inside the confirmation window, on one closed bar, all of:\n     VALUE · LADDER       the value tape was cheap past θ inside the dislocation window, and is not rich past θ now\n     CONVICTION · CHART   the histogram is above zero\n     CONVICTION · LADDER  the conviction tape is above zero or has risen two bars running\n     EFFORT → RESULT      participation was ABSORBED at some point in the dislocation window - effort that stopped producing result, the exhaustion the turn comes out of (switchable below)\n\nOn the pane: a red trace crossing back up through −θ while the columns turn green.\n\nArm-then-confirm replaced a same-bar rule that lost every ▲ whose layers turned one bar apart. Unmeasured as a stack: neither the crossing as an event nor the confirmations has been tested.", display=display.none)
int    inpConfirm = input.int(5, "Confirmation window", minval=1, maxval=50, group=G_T, tooltip="For ▲▼: how many bars, counting the crossing bar, the other layers have to confirm the trace's crossing.\n\nThe trace and effort usually turn a bar or two apart. Five bars absorbs that stagger while the turn is still the same event. 1 restores the old same-bar rule.\n\nA chosen number, not a fitted one.", display=display.none)
int    inpDisloc = input.int(20, "Dislocation window", minval=3, maxval=200, group=G_T, tooltip="For ▲▼: how far back the MTF value tape may have been past θ for the turn to count as a dislocation every horizon saw.\n\nThe trace turns first; the ladder mean, which includes slower frames, is usually already easing by the time the trace crosses back through θ. This window lets the ladder's dislocation be recent rather than simultaneous.", display=display.none)
bool   inpArm  = input.bool(true, "Arm marks  △ ▽", group=G_T, tooltip="A hollow △ (▽) on the θ rail at the bar the trace crossed back through −θ (+θ) with its basket settled - the ARM of a ▲ (▼), and exactly where Samanvaya on its own would have entered.\n\nWhen every other layer confirms inside the window, the solid ▲ follows on the same rail. A hollow mark that never fills is its own reading: value turned and flow did not follow.\n\nDrawn only while declarations are on and the stack can judge. When the ▲ fires on the arm bar itself, only the ▲ is drawn. A value event, so it does not fade in a quiet conviction regime.", display=display.none)
bool   inpCon  = input.bool(true, "Continuation  ◆", group=G_T, tooltip="CONTINUATION ◆ - control held, value reset, push resumed.\n\n◆ LONG (short mirrors) needs, on one closed bar:\n  CHART                conviction on this chart is above zero, the histogram went below zero inside the pullback window, and now crosses +k·σ\n  CONVICTION · LADDER  the conviction tape is past the inner zone on the buyers' side. With Ladder down (default) the push is BUILT inside the bars at every finer frame, not carried by one print; with Ladder up it is HELD by the frames above\n  VALUE · LADDER       the value tape is not rich past θ - the pullback left price fair or cheap; with the default Ladder up, on the frames above the chart\n  EFFORT → RESULT      participation is NOT absorbed on the bar - the push is producing result (switchable below)\n\nIn one line: the pullback reset value without breaking the push. The chart part alone measured +0.036R on metals and energy and -0.016R elsewhere; the stack is unmeasured.", display=display.none)
float  inpK    = input.float(0.5, "Impulse threshold (σ of histogram)", minval=0.0, maxval=3.0, step=0.1, group=G_T, tooltip="The histogram must cross ± this many standard deviations of its own distribution, rather than merely crossing zero. Used by the ◆, and it sets the brightest histogram tier.\n\nIn the earlier trigger design, 0 - the plain signal-line crossover - was the worst value of this setting out of sample across a 900-configuration search; that is why it is not 0. It has not been re-measured for the ◆ as it now stands.\n\nRaise it for fewer and stronger ◆. Above about 1.5 only violent expansions qualify.", display=display.none)
int    inpPull = input.int(6, "Pullback window", minval=2, maxval=50, group=G_T, tooltip="For continuation signals: how recently the histogram must have crossed to the wrong side for the move to count as a genuine pullback rather than an uninterrupted drift.", display=display.none)
bool   inpEff = input.bool(true, "Effort → result in signals", group=G_T, tooltip="The indicator's own thesis - is the move paid for - as a signal layer.\n\n▲▼ require that participation was ABSORBED (effort → result in the bottom fifth of its history) at some point in the dislocation window: heavy effort that stopped producing result, the exhaustion a turn comes out of.\n◆ requires that participation is NOT absorbed on the signal bar: the resuming push is producing result.\n\nA HYPOTHESIS, not a measured result. Switch it off to see the stack without it; comparing the two is how it should be judged.", display=display.none)
int    inpCool = input.int(10, "Cooldown (bars)", minval=0, maxval=200, group=G_T, tooltip="Minimum bars between signals in the same direction - ▲ and long ◆ share one clock, ▼ and short ◆ the other.\n\nWithout this, one choppy stretch produces a cluster of markers that all describe the same event. Raise it if the rails still look busy on your timeframe.", display=display.none)
bool   inpLean = input.bool(false, "Lean  ○ · signal line × trace", group=G_T, tooltip="OFF BY DEFAULT. Samanvaya's diamond, carried as a lean: conviction's signal line crossing the value trace. A ○ on the trace at the crossing - red when the signal line crosses ABOVE the trace (the short lean), green when it crosses below (the long lean).\n\nAt the defaults Samanvaya's embedded Siddhi line IS this script's signal line, so it is computed from that rather than from a second engine; change the conviction engine's inputs and it is no longer the line that was measured.\n\nMeasured in Samanvaya: +0.0273R against a matched random-entry baseline, positive on 8 of 10 instruments and all three primary futures, holding its sign out of sample - and smaller than a daily round-trip cost, overlap-adjusted t 0.93.\n\nWHY IT IS OFF. Its timing depends on two unrelated scalings - conviction's tanh/3σ against value's softbound · 0.5 - so it is a crossing of two different quantities on an axis they happen to share. And the signal line is no longer drawn, so the event refers to a line you cannot see. Read it as a lean, never as an entry. Its alerts fire only while it is on, through an 'Any alert() function call' alert - the eight signal alerts keep their own named conditions.", display=display.none)

// ── 5 · divergence ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bool   inpDiv  = input.bool(true,  "Regular divergence  R", group=G_V, tooltip="DIVERGENCE FAMILY · REGULAR - the move is not confirmed by the chart OR by the ladder, at a price every horizon calls stretched.\n\nR BEARISH (bullish mirrors), judged at the confirming pivot:\n  CHART        price made a higher high while conviction - the engine behind the histogram, not drawn - made a lower high; with the zone gate on, the earlier conviction high was above the inner zone.\n  CONVICTION   the ladder also made a lower high - its reading at this pivot is below its reading at the previous one. The whole ladder is failing to confirm, not one frame.\n  VALUE        the ladder is on the RICH side (> 0) at the pivot - the high was made above fair value across horizons.\n\nThe connector is drawn on the PRICE chart, joining the two swing highs being compared.\n\nChart-only regular divergence was the one element positive almost everywhere it was measured; the tape conditions are unmeasured.", display=display.none)
bool   inpHid  = input.bool(false, "Hidden divergence  H", group=G_V, tooltip="DIVERGENCE FAMILY · HIDDEN - a pullback where the underlying control never left.\n\nH BEARISH (bullish mirrors), judged at the confirming pivot:\n  CHART        price made a lower high while conviction made a higher high.\n  CONVICTION   the conviction tape is past the inner zone on the sellers' side at the pivot - inside the bars with Ladder down, the frames above with Ladder up.\n  VALUE        the value tape is NOT cheap past θ at the pivot - the bounce restored value inside that control.\n\nThe same thesis as the ◆, found at a pivot. Chart-only hidden divergence measured no edge on any universe; default off.", display=display.none)
int    inpPL   = input.int(5, "Pivot left", minval=1, maxval=30, group=G_V, tooltip="Bars of confirmation on the left of a pivot.", display=display.none)
int    inpPR   = input.int(5, "Pivot right", minval=1, maxval=30, group=G_V, tooltip="Bars of confirmation on the right of a pivot.\n\nTHIS IS THE LAG. A divergence cannot be known until this many bars have passed after the turn. Not repainting - the price of confirmation.", display=display.none)
int    inpGapM = input.int(5,  "Min pivot separation", minval=2, maxval=100, group=G_V, tooltip="Two pivots closer than this are the same swing and their comparison is meaningless.", display=display.none)
int    inpGapX = input.int(60, "Max pivot separation", minval=10, maxval=400, group=G_V, tooltip="Two pivots further apart than this belong to different market episodes. Comparing them produces impressive-looking lines that mean nothing.", display=display.none)
bool   inpGate = input.bool(true, "Zone gate on regular divergence", group=G_V, tooltip="Only accept a regular bearish divergence whose earlier conviction pivot formed above the inner zone, and a regular bullish one whose earlier pivot formed below it.\n\nThe measurement is equivocal: the gate improves divergence quality on gold, silver and crude, and slightly REDUCES it on the eight held-out instruments, where ungated divergence scored better. It is kept on by default because the primary futures are what this indicator is tuned for.", display=display.none)
bool   inpLine = input.bool(true, "Divergence connectors on price", group=G_V, tooltip="The line joining the two swing extremes a divergence compares, drawn on the PRICE chart - the higher high (or lower low) you can actually see. The conviction line it used to join in the pane is no longer drawn.\n\nCapped at sixty and recycled explicitly, so the profile's level lines and these cannot evict each other from a shared drawing budget.", display=display.none)

// ── 6 · conviction thresholds ───────────────────────────────────────────────
float  inpZ1   = input.float(30.0, "Inner zone", minval=5.0,  maxval=95.0, step=5.0, group=G_Z, tooltip="Where the window's effort has become heavily one-sided. Under adaptive scaling conviction sits beyond it roughly a third of the time.\n\nA THRESHOLD, NOT A LINE. It gates regular divergences, sets the conviction tape's control threshold for ◆ and H, and is the tape's first shading step. It is not drawn: the pane's lines belong to the value trace.", display=display.none)
float  inpZ2   = input.float(60.0, "Outer zone", minval=10.0, maxval=99.0, step=5.0, group=G_Z, tooltip="Near-uniform conviction, roughly four percent occupancy under adaptive scaling. Trends live here and so do their endings.\n\nA THRESHOLD, NOT A LINE: where the conviction tape turns solid, and where the panel's conviction row reads EXTREME.", display=display.none)

// ── 7 · display ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
string inpView = input.string("Full", "View", options=["Full", "Core"], group=G_D, tooltip="CORE is the instrument: histogram, value trace, rails, markers and the two ladder tapes. The volume profile, the open-interest table and the gold cast stand down - and the profile stops being COMPUTED, not just hidden.\n\nFULL adds the context layers on the price chart: WHERE participation happened (profile) and WHAT KIND it was (open interest).\n\nFull is the default. Core is the lighter view when you want the pane alone. Nothing in the engine or the signals changes either way.", display=display.none)
string inpPlace = input.string("Threshold rails", "Marker placement", options=["Threshold rails", "On the trace"], group=G_D, tooltip="THRESHOLD RAILS stamps every event on a fixed level - divergences on the outer dashed lines at ±70, declarations, arms and continuations on the θ lines - with bearish above and bullish below, so the markers form readable rows. A ▲ then sits on the very line whose crossing armed it.\n\nON THE TRACE places each marker at the value trace's reading when the event fired.", display=display.none)
bool   inpHist = input.bool(true, "Histogram", group=G_D, display=display.none)
string inpHScale = input.string("σ-pinned · 3σ = θ", "    Histogram scale", options=["σ-pinned · 3σ = θ", "Fixed gain"], group=G_D, tooltip="DISPLAY ONLY. How the columns are sized against a pane whose scale belongs to the value trace.\n\nσ-PINNED (default) - 3σ of the histogram's own distribution lands exactly on θ. The θ line is the one line both primaries cross, so pinning makes it mean something for both: a column that reaches it is a 3σ push. The scale follows the histogram's σ over the normalization window, so it is the same statement on every instrument. Until that σ exists the fixed gain below is used.\n\nFIXED GAIN - v5's scaling: the native histogram multiplied by the gain below.\n\nNothing in the engine reads the displayed value. Triggers, the impulse gate, the shading and the panel all work on the native histogram, which the Data Window carries.\n\nRead the columns against zero and θ only. A column touching ±70 or a tape means nothing.", display=display.none)
float  inpHGain = input.float(2.0, "    Fixed gain", minval=1.0, maxval=10.0, step=0.5, group=G_D, tooltip="DISPLAY ONLY. Used by the 'Fixed gain' scale, and by the σ-pinned scale until the histogram's σ has a full window. The histogram's native range is roughly ±20.", display=display.none)
string inpHLogic = input.string("Expansion + slope", "    Histogram shading", options=["Expansion + slope", "Expansion only"], group=G_D, tooltip="DISPLAY ONLY. How each column's shade is decided.\n\nHUE is the direction of the PUSH - which side of its own signal line conviction is on. It is NOT who is in control; that is conviction's side of zero, which the conviction tape and the panel carry. A green column while conviction sits at -45 means sellers still control but their grip is easing.\n\nEXPANSION + SLOPE (default) - three tiers per side:\n  PRESSING      column growing away from zero. Moderate below the impulse threshold, near-solid past it - bright means the trigger would accept it.\n  DECELERATING  column shrinking, but conviction is still moving with it. Mid shade - a pause in the push, not a reversal of it.\n  TURNING       column shrinking and conviction has flattened or turned against it. Pale - conviction itself is giving ground, often a few bars before the column crosses zero.\n\nWHY THE SLOPE ONLY SPLITS SHRINKING COLUMNS. With the signal an EMA of conviction, Δhist = (1−α)·Δosc − α·hist[1]. A growing column above zero therefore REQUIRES rising conviction, so slope adds nothing there. A shrinking column is ambiguous - conviction may be rising slower than the signal catches up, or it may have turned - and the slope is what tells the two apart.\n\nA conviction move smaller than 0.1σ of its own bar-to-bar change counts as flat, so a wobble reads as turning rather than flickering between tiers.\n\nEXPANSION ONLY - two tiers: growing is saturated, shrinking is pale.\n\nNone of this has been measured for edge. It is a reading aid.", display=display.none)
bool   inpQuiet = input.bool(true, "    Quiet-regime desaturation", group=G_D, tooltip="DISPLAY ONLY. Adaptive scaling divides by 3σ of the raw share, so when that σ collapses a small imbalance scales into a large reading - the columns look dramatic and the chart does not.\n\nWhen the raw share's σ sits in the bottom fifth of its own history (4× the normalization window), the columns lose about half their colour and the panel's raw-share row reads QUIET. The tiers still read; the hue just stops shouting. Conviction-triggered markers fade with them.\n\nThis is the check the panel has always asked you to make by hand, moved onto the chart.", display=display.none)
bool   inpTraceZone = input.bool(true, "Trace intensity by distance", group=G_D, tooltip="DISPLAY ONLY. The value trace grades with distance from fair value exactly as the value tape does: faint inside θ, a clear step brighter at θ, solid toward ±70. How far price is dislocated reads off the line itself.\n\nIntensity, not a new colour. Velocity is deliberately NOT in the trace - Samanvaya brightened its trace while it rose because it had no histogram; here the histogram carries the push.\n\nSeparately, the trace's hue dims toward grey when the RV and breadth legs point opposite ways or while the macro basket is settling: the value reading is then less coherent than its level suggests.", display=display.none)
bool   inpThr  = input.bool(false, "Show impulse threshold", group=G_D, tooltip="Draws the ± k·σ band the histogram must cross for a trigger to fire, on the same display scale as the columns. Useful once, while calibrating the threshold to an instrument; clutter afterwards.", display=display.none)
bool   inpFill = input.bool(true, "Value action zones", group=G_D, tooltip="Shades θ → ±70: where the trace is dislocated and a ▼ (above) or ▲ (below) can be armed. Coloured by the event, as the rails are - the upper zone is red because it carries sells, even though a trace in it is green (rich).", display=display.none)
bool   inpRot  = input.bool(true, "Basket rotation  ⟳", group=G_D, tooltip="A gold ⟳ on the zero line when the RV leg's macro basket changes membership. The trace is a different series afterwards - prior history describes the old basket - and no ▲▼ can arm for the next 21 bars while it settles; the trace dims for the same stretch.", display=display.none)
bool   inpStateBg = input.bool(false, "Declaration background", group=G_D, tooltip="DISPLAY ONLY. A faint pane tint for the standing declaration: green after a ▲ until the next ▼, red after a ▼ until the next ▲. Off by default - the triangles already mark each declaration, and a declaration has no end, so the tint can stand for a long time.\n\nNo tint while the stack is paused.", display=display.none)
string inpTheme = input.string("Auto", "Palette", options=["Auto", "Light", "Dark"], group=G_D, display=display.none)

// ── 8 · information panel ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
bool   inpPanel = input.bool(false, "Information panel", group=G_P, tooltip="The oscillator's own panel, in the indicator pane. Off removes it entirely.\n\nThe open-interest readout is a separate table with its own switch, in the '10 · Open interest' group — it sits on the PRICE chart rather than in this pane, so the two never collide and either can be run without the other.", display=display.none)
string inpTbl  = input.string("Detailed", "    Detail", options=["Compact", "Detailed"], group=G_P, tooltip="COMPACT is the headline, the signal state, value and its legs, both tapes and the impulse. DETAILED adds the value model - hedge strength, the driver basket, driver timing - then conviction's own reading, the raw share and its σ, this bar's conviction, the participation weight, effort → result, layer overlap and live rungs, and — when the profile is on — its data mode and where price sits in it.", display=display.none)

int         DIV_LINES = 60      // divergence connectors retained, recycled explicitly
const float RAIL_OUT  = 70.0    // the trace's outer rail - Samanvaya's ±70

// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  PALETTE
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
float bgLum    = color.r(chart.bg_color) * 0.299 + color.g(chart.bg_color) * 0.587 + color.b(chart.bg_color) * 0.114
bool  darkMode = inpTheme == "Dark" or (inpTheme == "Auto" and bgLum < 128)

color C_BULL  = darkMode ? #3FB980 : #08724A
color C_BEAR  = darkMode ? #E4776A : #A8443A
color C_NEUT  = darkMode ? #8A97A0 : #69757E
color C_GOLD  = darkMode ? #CFC257 : #7A6B15      // READ WITH CAUTION - never a direction
color C_SOFT  = darkMode ? #A8B3B9 : #525C64
color C_INK   = darkMode ? #0D1215 : #FFFFFF      // glyph ink on filled labels - never panel text
color C_PANEL = darkMode ? color.new(#0D1215, 6) : color.new(#FFFFFF, 4)
color C_GRID  = darkMode ? color.new(#8A97A0, 70) : color.new(#7A868F, 74)

// profile palette — PVOI's, unchanged
color C_POC    = darkMode ? #FFB020 : #B26A00      // point of control — warm anchor
color C_VA     = darkMode ? #5B8FD6 : #2C5C99      // value-area boundaries + band, cool
color C_VWAP   = darkMode ? #F06BA8 : #A32468      // volume-weighted average, rose
color C_NODE   = darkMode ? #6E7C88 : #8A949B      // monochrome out-of-value tails · neutral grey
color C_HVN    = darkMode ? #8F7BD8 : #5E4B9E      // High-Volume Node · dense violet
color C_LVN    = darkMode ? #C6BBEA : #8F7DBB      // Low-Volume Node  · pale  violet
color C_VIOLET = darkMode ? #A87ABF : #6A3F84      // Initial Balance

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  1 · CONVICTION AND PARTICIPATION
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float tr   = ta.tr(true)
float disp = close - nz(close[1], close)
float conv = tr > 1e-12 ? disp / tr : 0.0          // bounded −1 … +1

bool  volOK  = not na(volume) and volume > 0
// A hollow bar (holiday, half session, thin overnight print) used to feed na into
// this EMA, which killed it for at least inpVN bars and silently dropped the whole
// series back to range weighting. Measured on real data, volume weighting was only
// live on 73% of bars under "Auto" - 31% on 1h futures, 50% on silver daily, where
// the resulting oscillator correlated only 0.58 with the intended one. Carrying the
// last good print keeps the baseline alive; a genuinely volume-less symbol still
// leaves volLast na and still falls through to true range, as documented.
var float volLast = na
if volOK
    volLast := volume
float volAvg = ta.ema(volLast, inpVN)
float trAvg  = ta.ema(tr, inpVN)

float wVol = volOK and not na(volAvg) and volAvg > 1e-12 ? volume / volAvg : na
float wRng = not na(trAvg) and trAvg > 1e-12 ? tr / trAvg : 1.0

float wRaw = inpPart == "Off"        ? 1.0 :
             inpPart == "True range" ? wRng :
             inpPart == "Volume"     ? nz(wVol, 1.0) :
                                       nz(wVol, wRng)
float w = math.min(math.max(nz(wRaw, 1.0), 0.0), inpCap)

// Which branch ACTUALLY produced w on this bar - reported by the panel. It used
// to be inferred from volOK alone, which read "volume" in True-range mode and
// "true range" in Volume mode on a hollow bar, where the weight is really a flat
// 1.0. A panel that guesses at the branch is an echo, not a report.
string wSrc = inpPart == "Off"        ? "off · equal weight" :
              inpPart == "True range" ? "true range" :
              inpPart == "Volume"     ? (not na(wVol) ? "volume" : "no print · flat") :
              not na(wVol)            ? "volume" : "true range"

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  2 · THE CHART'S OWN CONVICTION           the fastest rung of the ladder
//
//  Ratio of sums, so a dead bar contributes nothing to either side rather than
//  an undefined ratio; then scaled against its own dispersion. This is the
//  published Nishchaya v3 oscillator exactly. In v6 it is not drawn: it is the
//  engine behind the histogram, the pivots R and H are found on, and the
//  chart rung of the conviction tape.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_tanh(float x) =>
    float xc = math.max(-10.0, math.min(10.0, x))
    float e  = math.exp(2.0 * xc)
    (e - 1.0) / (e + 1.0)

float num  = ta.sma(conv * w, inpLen)
float denA = ta.sma(math.abs(conv) * w, inpLen)          // agreement: Σ|c|·w
float denE = ta.sma(w, inpLen)                           // effort:    Σw
float den  = inpDen == "Effort" ? denE : denA
float raw  = den > 1e-12 ? 100.0 * num / den : 0.0

// EFFORT → RESULT (display only). The share of the window's participation that
// became directional displacement at all, and where that share sits in its own
// history. Low = effort being absorbed. Computed under both denominators.
float effShare = denE > 1e-12 ? denA / denE : na
float effPct   = ta.percentrank(effShare, inpNorm)

float rawSd  = ta.stdev(raw, inpNorm)
float zChart = na(rawSd) or rawSd < 1e-9 ? 0.0 : raw / (3.0 * rawSd)     // the tanh argument
float scaled = 100.0 * f_tanh(zChart)
float fast   = inpSm > 1 ? ta.ema(scaled, inpSm) : scaled            // v3's oscillator

// Warm-up, stage one: raw is pinned to 0 until its window and the participation
// baseline fill; zChart until rawSd has a window free of those zeros.
var int nRaw = 0
if bar_index >= inpLen + inpVN
    nRaw += 1
bool sdOK = nRaw > inpNorm

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  3 · THE LADDER                          fixed, standard, the same everywhere
//
//  UP    every frame of 4h · 1h · 30m · 15m · 5m ABOVE the chart that holds a
//        whole number of chart bars, then the chart. Daily: W · D. Up to five.
//  DOWN  every frame of 1m · 3m · 5m · 15m · 30m · 1h · 4h BELOW the chart that
//        the chart holds a whole number of, then the chart. Daily: all seven.
//        Weekly: D · 4h · 1h. Monthly: W · D. Up to seven.
//  FALLBACK  a direction with no frames on this chart reads the other; the
//        tape's label shows ↺.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var array<int> LAD_UP = array.from(240, 60, 30, 15, 5)
var array<int> LAD_DN = array.from(1, 3, 5, 15, 30, 60, 240)

int  ladSec   = timeframe.in_seconds()
int  ladTm    = int(math.round(ladSec / 60.0))
bool ladMin   = timeframe.isintraday and ladSec >= 60 and ladSec % 60 == 0
bool ladDaily = timeframe.isdaily and timeframe.multiplier == 1
// FALLBACK. A direction with no frames on this chart uses the other instead of
// pausing every signal; the tape's label shows ↺. Availability mirrors the rung
// loops below exactly: every frame above an intraday chart divides 240, and 1m
// divides every chart above it.
bool ladUpAv  = ladDaily or (ladMin and ladTm < 240 and 240 % ladTm == 0)
bool ladDnAv  = (timeframe.ismonthly and timeframe.multiplier == 1) or (timeframe.isweekly and timeframe.multiplier == 1) or ladDaily or (ladMin and ladTm > 1)
bool cWantUp  = inpTape == "Ladder up"
bool cWantDn  = inpTape == "Ladder down"
bool ladUp    = (cWantUp and ladUpAv) or (cWantDn and not ladDnAv and ladUpAv)
bool ladDn    = (cWantDn and ladDnAv) or (cWantUp and not ladUpAv and ladDnAv)
bool ladFallback = (cWantUp and ladDn) or (cWantDn and ladUp)

// Up: parents, top → down.
string lad1 = ""
string lad2 = ""
string lad3 = ""
string lad4 = ""
string lad5 = ""
// Down: children, finest → coarsest.
string low1 = ""
string low2 = ""
string low3 = ""
string low4 = ""
string low5 = ""
string low6 = ""
string low7 = ""
if ladUp
    if ladDaily
        lad1 := "W"
    else if ladMin and ladTm < 240
        int n = 0
        for f in LAD_UP
            if f > ladTm and f % ladTm == 0
                n += 1
                string sf = str.tostring(f)
                if n == 1
                    lad1 := sf
                else if n == 2
                    lad2 := sf
                else if n == 3
                    lad3 := sf
                else if n == 4
                    lad4 := sf
                else
                    lad5 := sf
if ladDn
    if timeframe.ismonthly and timeframe.multiplier == 1
        low1 := "D"
        low2 := "W"
    else if timeframe.isweekly and timeframe.multiplier == 1
        low1 := "60"
        low2 := "240"
        low3 := "D"
    else if ladDaily or ladMin
        int n = 0
        for f in LAD_DN
            if ladDaily or (f < ladTm and ladTm % f == 0)
                n += 1
                string sf = str.tostring(f)
                if n == 1
                    low1 := sf
                else if n == 2
                    low2 := sf
                else if n == 3
                    low3 := sf
                else if n == 4
                    low4 := sf
                else if n == 5
                    low5 := sf
                else if n == 6
                    low6 := sf
                else
                    low7 := sf
bool multiUp = lad1 != ""
bool multiDn = low1 != ""
bool multi   = multiUp or multiDn

f_tfLbl(string tf) =>
    string out = tf
    if tf != "D" and tf != "W" and tf != "M" and tf != ""
        int n = int(str.tonumber(tf))
        out := n % 60 == 0 ? str.tostring(n / 60) + "h" : tf + "m"
    out

f_join(string acc, string tf) => tf == "" ? acc : acc + f_tfLbl(tf) + "·"
string ladChartLbl = timeframe.isintraday ? f_tfLbl(str.tostring(ladTm)) : timeframe.isdaily ? "D" : timeframe.isweekly ? "W" : timeframe.period
string ladLbl = not multi ? "chart only" :
     multiUp ? f_join(f_join(f_join(f_join(f_join("", lad1), lad2), lad3), lad4), lad5) + ladChartLbl :
     f_join(f_join(f_join(f_join(f_join(f_join(f_join("", low1), low2), low3), low4), low5), low6), low7) + ladChartLbl

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  4 · PARENTS, RECONSTRUCTED ON THE CHART              (ladder up)
//
//  The parent supplies only what it knows for certain: its state as of its last
//  CLOSED bar, plus the open time of the bar now forming. Every sum is over one
//  bar fewer than its window, so the chart can complete it with the forming bar.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
type Parent
    float cPrev      // last settled close
    float vLast      // last good volume print, carried
    float vAvg       // EMA of volume at the settled bar
    float trAvg      // EMA of true range at the settled bar
    float sNum       // Σ c·w     over the last (lookback − 1) settled bars
    float sDa        // Σ |c|·w
    float sDe        // Σ w
    float sR         // Σ raw     over the last (normalization − 1) settled bars
    float sR2        // Σ raw²
    float zS         // the settled bar's own z, for the reconstruction check
    bool  rdy        // the parent's own normalization window is clean
    int   t          // open time of the parent bar now forming

f_parentSettled() =>
    float pTr  = ta.tr(true)
    float pCv  = pTr > 1e-12 ? (close - nz(close[1], close)) / pTr : 0.0
    bool  pVok = not na(volume) and volume > 0
    var float pVl = na
    if pVok
        pVl := volume
    float pVa  = ta.ema(pVl, inpVN)
    float pTa  = ta.ema(pTr, inpVN)
    float pWv  = pVok and not na(pVa) and pVa > 1e-12 ? volume / pVa : na
    float pWr  = not na(pTa) and pTa > 1e-12 ? pTr / pTa : 1.0
    float pWx  = inpPart == "Off" ? 1.0 : inpPart == "True range" ? pWr : inpPart == "Volume" ? nz(pWv, 1.0) : nz(pWv, pWr)
    float pW   = math.min(math.max(nz(pWx, 1.0), 0.0), inpCap)
    float pNum = ta.sma(pCv * pW, inpLen)
    float pDa  = ta.sma(math.abs(pCv) * pW, inpLen)
    float pDe  = ta.sma(pW, inpLen)
    float pDen = inpDen == "Effort" ? pDe : pDa
    float pRaw = pDen > 1e-12 ? 100.0 * pNum / pDen : 0.0
    float pSd  = ta.stdev(pRaw, inpNorm)
    float pZ   = na(pSd) or pSd < 1e-9 ? 0.0 : pRaw / (3.0 * pSd)
    var int pN = 0
    if bar_index >= inpLen + inpVN
        pN += 1
    float sNum = math.sum(pCv * pW, inpLen - 1)
    float sDa  = math.sum(math.abs(pCv) * pW, inpLen - 1)
    float sDe  = math.sum(pW, inpLen - 1)
    float sR   = math.sum(pRaw, inpNorm - 1)
    float sR2  = math.sum(pRaw * pRaw, inpNorm - 1)
    bool  rdy  = pN > inpNorm and not na(sNum) and not na(sR) and not na(pVa) and not na(pTa)
    Parent.new(close, pVl, pVa, pTa, sNum, sDa, sDe, sR, sR2, pZ, rdy, time)

// Evaluated on parent bar i with lookahead_on, this hands every chart bar inside
// bar i the state of bar i−1 - closed, final, known - and the open time of bar
// i, which is known the moment it opens. Nothing from bar i's future leaks.
f_parent() =>
    Parent x = f_parentSettled()
    Parent p = x[1]
    if not na(p)
        p := p.copy()
        p.t := time
    p

// The forming parent bar, aggregated from chart bars since it opened. Each call
// site keeps its own running bar.
f_agg(int t) =>
    var int   at = na
    var float ah = na
    var float al = na
    var float av = 0.0
    if na(at) or t != at
        at := t
        ah := high
        al := low
        av := nz(volume)
    else
        ah := math.max(ah, high)
        al := math.min(al, low)
        av += nz(volume)
    [ah, al, close, av]

// The parent's arithmetic, finished with the forming bar included. Mirrors
// section 1-2 line for line; returns the parent's live z, or na until the
// parent can calibrate.
f_develop(Parent p, float aH, float aL, float aC, float aV) =>
    float z = na
    if not na(p) and p.rdy
        float cp  = p.cPrev
        float trD = math.max(aH, cp) - math.min(aL, cp)
        float cD  = trD > 1e-12 ? (aC - cp) / trD : 0.0
        bool  vOk = aV > 0
        float vl  = vOk ? aV : p.vLast
        float al  = 2.0 / (inpVN + 1)
        float vA  = na(vl) ? p.vAvg : al * vl + (1.0 - al) * p.vAvg
        float tA  = al * trD + (1.0 - al) * p.trAvg
        float wv  = vOk and not na(vA) and vA > 1e-12 ? aV / vA : na
        float wr  = not na(tA) and tA > 1e-12 ? trD / tA : 1.0
        float wx  = inpPart == "Off" ? 1.0 : inpPart == "True range" ? wr : inpPart == "Volume" ? nz(wv, 1.0) : nz(wv, wr)
        float wD  = math.min(math.max(nz(wx, 1.0), 0.0), inpCap)
        float nm  = (p.sNum + cD * wD) / inpLen
        float dA  = (p.sDa + math.abs(cD) * wD) / inpLen
        float dE  = (p.sDe + wD) / inpLen
        float dn  = inpDen == "Effort" ? dE : dA
        float rw  = dn > 1e-12 ? 100.0 * nm / dn : 0.0
        float mu  = (p.sR + rw) / inpNorm
        float vr  = (p.sR2 + rw * rw) / inpNorm - mu * mu
        float sd  = math.sqrt(math.max(vr, 0.0))
        z := sd < 1e-9 ? 0.0 : rw / (3.0 * sd)
    z

Parent p1 = na
Parent p2 = na
Parent p3 = na
Parent p4 = na
Parent p5 = na
if multiUp
    p1 := request.security(syminfo.tickerid, lad1, f_parent(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
    if lad2 != ""
        p2 := request.security(syminfo.tickerid, lad2, f_parent(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
    if lad3 != ""
        p3 := request.security(syminfo.tickerid, lad3, f_parent(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
    if lad4 != ""
        p4 := request.security(syminfo.tickerid, lad4, f_parent(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
    if lad5 != ""
        p5 := request.security(syminfo.tickerid, lad5, f_parent(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)

// Fields are read only inside a guard: a field of an na object is a runtime
// error, and a ternary is not a guaranteed guard.
f_pt(Parent p) =>
    int t = na
    if not na(p)
        t := p.t
    t
int t1 = f_pt(p1)
int t2 = f_pt(p2)
int t3 = f_pt(p3)
int t4 = f_pt(p4)
int t5 = f_pt(p5)
[aH1, aL1, aC1, aV1] = f_agg(t1)
[aH2, aL2, aC2, aV2] = f_agg(t2)
[aH3, aL3, aC3, aV3] = f_agg(t3)
[aH4, aL4, aC4, aV4] = f_agg(t4)
[aH5, aL5, aC5, aV5] = f_agg(t5)
float z1 = f_develop(p1, aH1, aL1, aC1, aV1)
float z2 = f_develop(p2, aH2, aL2, aC2, aV2)
float z3 = f_develop(p3, aH3, aL3, aC3, aV3)
float z4 = f_develop(p4, aH4, aL4, aC4, aV4)
float z5 = f_develop(p5, aH5, aL5, aC5, aV5)

// RECONSTRUCTION CHECK (display only). On the first chart bar of each new parent
// bar, the last live reading of the bar that just closed is compared with the
// value the parent itself settled on, in conviction points. Carried between closes.
f_recErr(Parent p, int t, float zLive) =>
    float e = na
    if not na(p) and not na(t) and t != nz(t[1], t) and not na(zLive[1]) and not na(p.zS)
        e := 100.0 * math.abs(f_tanh(zLive[1]) - f_tanh(p.zS))
    e
float e1 = f_recErr(p1, t1, z1)
float e2 = f_recErr(p2, t2, z2)
float e3 = f_recErr(p3, t3, z3)
float e4 = f_recErr(p4, t4, z4)
float e5 = f_recErr(p5, t5, z5)
var float recErr = na
if not na(e1) or not na(e2) or not na(e3) or not na(e4) or not na(e5)
    recErr := math.max(nz(e1), nz(e2), nz(e3), nz(e4), nz(e5))

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  4b · CHILDREN, READ FROM INSIDE THE CHART BAR           (ladder down)
//
//  Sampling a lower frame at the chart bar's close would describe only its last
//  few bars - the final twenty minutes of a day, on a 1m rung. So each lower
//  frame runs the full engine on its own continuous history, and the chart bar
//  takes the participation-weighted AVERAGE of that frame's z across all of its
//  bars inside the chart bar: the bar seen from inside, at that resolution. A
//  heavy intrabar counts for more than a quiet one, as it should.
//
//  request.security_lower_tf delivers every intrabar of every closed chart bar
//  in history and the intrabars so far on the live one, so the reading moves
//  while the chart bar forms and is final at its close - no repaint.
//
//  DEPTH. TradingView caps intrabars per request (about 100,000 on most plans).
//  A 1m rung on a daily chart is 375 intrabars a day on NSE, so it reaches back
//  roughly a year; older bars run without it. A frame joins the ladder when its
//  own normalization is clean and shifts the tape's level, not its scale.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_child() =>
    float qTr  = ta.tr(true)
    float qCv  = qTr > 1e-12 ? (close - nz(close[1], close)) / qTr : 0.0
    bool  qVok = not na(volume) and volume > 0
    var float qVl = na
    if qVok
        qVl := volume
    float qVa  = ta.ema(qVl, inpVN)
    float qTa  = ta.ema(qTr, inpVN)
    float qWv  = qVok and not na(qVa) and qVa > 1e-12 ? volume / qVa : na
    float qWr  = not na(qTa) and qTa > 1e-12 ? qTr / qTa : 1.0
    float qWx  = inpPart == "Off" ? 1.0 : inpPart == "True range" ? qWr : inpPart == "Volume" ? nz(qWv, 1.0) : nz(qWv, qWr)
    float qW   = math.min(math.max(nz(qWx, 1.0), 0.0), inpCap)
    float qNum = ta.sma(qCv * qW, inpLen)
    float qDa  = ta.sma(math.abs(qCv) * qW, inpLen)
    float qDe  = ta.sma(qW, inpLen)
    float qDen = inpDen == "Effort" ? qDe : qDa
    float qRaw = qDen > 1e-12 ? 100.0 * qNum / qDen : 0.0
    float qSd  = ta.stdev(qRaw, inpNorm)
    var int qN = 0
    if bar_index >= inpLen + inpVN
        qN += 1
    float qZ   = qN > inpNorm ? (na(qSd) or qSd < 1e-9 ? 0.0 : qRaw / (3.0 * qSd)) : na
    [qZ, qW]

// Participation-weighted mean of a child's z over the intrabars of this chart
// bar; na when the frame has no calibrated intrabars here.
f_inside(array<float> zs, array<float> ws) =>
    float sz = 0.0
    float sw = 0.0
    if not na(zs) and not na(ws)
        int m = math.min(array.size(zs), array.size(ws))
        if m > 0
            for i = 0 to m - 1
                float zi = array.get(zs, i)
                if not na(zi)
                    float wi = math.max(nz(array.get(ws, i), 1.0), 1e-6)
                    sz += zi * wi
                    sw += wi
    sw > 0.0 ? sz / sw : na

float y1 = na
float y2 = na
float y3 = na
float y4 = na
float y5 = na
float y6 = na
float y7 = na
if multiDn
    [qz1, qw1] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low1, f_child())
    y1 := f_inside(qz1, qw1)
    if low2 != ""
        [qz2, qw2] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low2, f_child())
        y2 := f_inside(qz2, qw2)
    if low3 != ""
        [qz3, qw3] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low3, f_child())
        y3 := f_inside(qz3, qw3)
    if low4 != ""
        [qz4, qw4] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low4, f_child())
        y4 := f_inside(qz4, qw4)
    if low5 != ""
        [qz5, qw5] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low5, f_child())
        y5 := f_inside(qz5, qw5)
    if low6 != ""
        [qz6, qw6] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low6, f_child())
        y6 := f_inside(qz6, qw6)
    if low7 != ""
        [qz7, qw7] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, low7, f_child())
        y7 := f_inside(qz7, qw7)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  4c · THE VALUE ENGINE · Samanvaya's composite, carried whole
//
//  Everything from here to section 5 is Samanvaya's composite: the RV leg (Y
//  hedged against a stepwise-selected macro basket on an anchored clock, the
//  hedge applied in proportion to its measured skill, the spread scored over a
//  timescale ensemble) and the breadth leg (seven MSF views of Y's own price),
//  blended in z-space. In v6 its chart reading IS the pane's trace, and the
//  same composite on every rung is the MTF value tape; a lighter copy would be
//  a different reading wearing Samanvaya's name.
//
//  Ported line for line. Left behind: Samanvaya's table, its OU / ADF
//  diagnostics, its exits, its own markers, and its embedded copy of the
//  conviction engine - this script IS that engine. Names that collided with
//  Pragati's were renamed (confirmed → svConfirmed, f_tanh → f_svTanh,
//  b1..b3 → gb1..gb3). "The pane" in the comments below means Samanvaya's
//  unified reading - in v6, the trace.
//
//  Nothing here touches the histogram. The signals read three things from it:
//  the value tape (tpBias), the trace (unified_osc) and its basket gate
//  (basketWarm, enoughData). The panel reads the legs and the basket.
//
//  THE AUDIT THIS PORT CARRIES - a standalone samanvaya.pine must carry the
//  same, or it will not match the trace:
//    · the anchor sample is each period's OWN return (section 4 below), the
//      intercept is spread over the bars a period holds (section 9), and hedge
//      skill is measured one period at a time (section 10);
//    · DRIVER TIMING - a driver whose bar closes more than a third of a chart
//      bar after the chart's is read at its previous close (section 1);
//    · Y is the chart's own close, not a request; the custom driver faces the
//      same self-screen as every constituent; breadth is defined once.
//  The value rungs keep rolling window sums instead of re-summing (same
//  numbers).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  STUDY-FIXED CONSTANTS.  Each was an input; each was swept over its full range
//  across 24 real targets. The bracket is how far the WHOLE range moved mean
//  information coefficient, in standard errors across targets (SE = 0.0135).
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
string anchorTF   = anchorSel == "Weekly (stronger, needs daily+ charts)" ? "W" : "D"
bool   selStep    = true   // stepwise vs absolute-ρ selection        [0.1 SE]
int    corrLen    = 250    // estimation sample, anchor bars          [0.4 SE, best]
int    holdOut    = 12     // selection hold-out, anchor bars         [0.4 SE, best]
float  hystMarg   = 0.10   // selection hysteresis                    [0.1 SE, best]
float  ridgeLam   = 0.05   // ridge shrinkage                         [0.1 SE]
float  minRho     = 0.0    // economic floor; Fisher does the work    [0.0 SE]
bool   sigCorrect = true   // Šidák over 9 candidates                 [0.0 SE]
int    inpNumVars = 3      // maximum model order                     [0.1 SE]
int    minObs     = 30     // minimum usable anchor sample
float  gain       = 0.5    // display scaling; monotone, cannot move rank IC [0.0 SE]
bool   useDrift   = true   // high-pass. neutral in the sweep [0.5 SE]; kept
int    driftLen   = 400    // because with the hedge off the RV leg is a raw
                           // cumulative return and would otherwise drift [0.7 SE]
int    skillWin   = 104    // anchor bars over which hedge skill is measured

// RV leg timescale ensemble. No window dominates — best and worst member are
// 0.0053 IC apart — so the grid is averaged rather than chosen. The members are
// ~0.73 correlated, so their mean has sd √((1+4ρ)/5) = 0.887 and is divided by
// it to keep θ meaning what it means at a single window.
int   Z1 = 8
int   Z2 = 13
int   Z3 = 21
int   Z4 = 34
int   Z5 = 55
                           // (ENS_SCALE is declared with the ensemble below,
                           //  where its derivation is documented)

// Breadth leg grid — Tattva's module default, which also measured best of the
// six configurations swept (+0.0691; whole spread 0.0060 IC, inside the SE).
int   B1 = 10
int   B2 = 14
int   B3 = 20
int   B4 = 28
int   B5 = 40
float rocFrac    = 0.70
float regimeSens = 8.0     // Tattva SWAYAM_REGIME_SENSITIVITY
float baseWeight = 0.0     // Tattva SWAYAM_BASE_WEIGHT — sets the (-5,5) bound
float clipZ      = 3.0
int   varCorrLen = 200     // window for measuring ρ between the two legs

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  1. DATA  ·  strict non-repainting fetch of Y and the 21-instrument universe
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
bool svConfirmed = barstate.isconfirmed

// DRIVER TIMING. A driver's bar is matched to the chart bar by date, and on a
// daily chart many drivers close hours AFTER Y - TVC:US10Y about eleven hours
// after NSE. In history such a bar carries information from after Y's close;
// live, at Y's close, it is still forming - so history would read better than
// realtime and the last bar would revise. A driver whose bar closes more than a
// third of a chart bar after the chart's is read at its PREVIOUS close, the
// value known when Y closed. The third is derived: lagging a driver L of a bar
// late keeps L of genuine overlap with Y's return; keeping it keeps 1 − L, of
// which L is future; keeping wins exactly while (1 − L) − L > L. Measured, not
// assumed, so a driver already aligned to an earlier bar is never lagged twice.
// The tuple costs no extra request.
float DRIVER_LATE = 1.0 / 3.0

f_req(simple string sym) =>
    [cNow, cPrev, tClose] = request.security(sym, "", [close, close[1], time_close], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
    float span = timeframe.in_seconds() * 1000.0
    float lf   = na(tClose) or na(time_close) or span <= 0 ? 0.0 : math.max(0.0, (tClose - time_close) / span)
    float v    = lf > DRIVER_LATE ? cPrev : cNow
    [barstate.isconfirmed ? v : nz(v[1], v), lf]

// Y is the chart's own close under the same confirmed-bar discipline - it used
// to be fetched through a request of the chart's own symbol, which bought
// nothing and spent one of the forty.
float src_y = svConfirmed ? close : nz(close[1], close)

[d_JP10Y, lf_JP10Y]   = f_req('TVC:JP10Y')
[d_JP02Y, lf_JP02Y]   = f_req('TVC:JP02Y')
[d_CN10Y, lf_CN10Y]   = f_req('TVC:CN10Y')
[d_CN02Y, lf_CN02Y]   = f_req('TVC:CN02Y')
[d_EU10Y, lf_EU10Y]   = f_req('TVC:EU10Y')
[d_EU02Y, lf_EU02Y]   = f_req('TVC:EU02Y')
[d_GB10Y, lf_GB10Y]   = f_req('TVC:GB10Y')
[d_GB02Y, lf_GB02Y]   = f_req('TVC:GB02Y')
[d_US10Y, lf_US10Y]   = f_req('TVC:US10Y')
[d_US02Y, lf_US02Y]   = f_req('TVC:US02Y')
[d_US30Y, lf_US30Y]   = f_req('TVC:US30Y')
[d_IN10Y, lf_IN10Y]   = f_req('TVC:IN10Y')
[d_IN02Y, lf_IN02Y]   = f_req('TVC:IN02Y')
[d_USDINR, lf_USDINR] = f_req('FX_IDC:USDINR')
[d_EURINR, lf_EURINR] = f_req('FX_IDC:EURINR')
[d_GBPINR, lf_GBPINR] = f_req('FX_IDC:GBPINR')
[d_JPYINR, lf_JPYINR] = f_req('FX_IDC:JPYINR')
[d_GOLD, lf_GOLD]     = f_req('TVC:GOLD')
[d_SILVER, lf_SILVER] = f_req('TVC:SILVER')
[d_USOIL, lf_USOIL]   = f_req('TVC:USOIL')
[d_DXY, lf_DXY]       = f_req('TVC:DXY')
// An empty symbol is not a valid request.security argument and the custom slot
// now ships empty. Substituting the chart symbol keeps the call legal; the name
// screen then drops it, so the column is constant and can never be selected.
string custSym = usy_sym == "" ? syminfo.tickerid : usy_sym
[d_CUSTOM, lf_CUSTOM] = f_req(custSym)

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  2. NATURAL UNITS  ·  the reason the model can travel between timeframes
//
//  A yield and a price do not move in the same unit. Yields are measured in
//  PERCENTAGE POINTS — log(4.28/4.20) is meaningless the moment a yield
//  approaches or crosses zero, which JP and EU spent years doing. Prices are
//  measured in LOGARITHMS, whose differences are returns.
//
//  Crucially there is NO rolling standardization here. A rolling z-score is a
//  window-dependent transform, so a beta fitted against it is only valid on the
//  window that produced it and cannot be moved from the anchor clock to the
//  chart clock. Natural units are frequency-stable, which is what makes the
//  whole anchored design work. It also keeps every value O(1)–O(10), which is
//  better conditioned than the raw levels ever were.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_lg(series float x) => na(x) or x <= 0 ? na : math.log(x)
f_df(series float x) => na(x) or na(x[1]) ? na : x - x[1]

// levels representation: yields as-is (%), prices as logs
float L_JP10Y  = d_JP10Y
float L_JP02Y  = d_JP02Y
float L_CN10Y  = d_CN10Y
float L_CN02Y  = d_CN02Y
float L_EU10Y  = d_EU10Y
float L_EU02Y  = d_EU02Y
float L_GB10Y  = d_GB10Y
float L_GB02Y  = d_GB02Y
float L_US10Y  = d_US10Y
float L_US02Y  = d_US02Y
float L_US30Y  = d_US30Y
float L_IN10Y  = d_IN10Y
float L_IN02Y  = d_IN02Y
float L_USDINR = f_lg(d_USDINR)
float L_EURINR = f_lg(d_EURINR)
float L_GBPINR = f_lg(d_GBPINR)
float L_JPYINR = f_lg(d_JPYINR)
float L_GOLD   = f_lg(d_GOLD)
float L_SILVER = f_lg(d_SILVER)
float L_USOIL  = f_lg(d_USOIL)
float L_DXY    = f_lg(d_DXY)
float L_CUSTOM = f_lg(d_CUSTOM)
float L_Y      = f_lg(src_y)

// Differenced once: the model runs on returns. (A levels engine existed and was
// removed - it passed ADF 2% of the time - and with it the switch between them.)
float v_JP10Y  = f_df(L_JP10Y)
float v_JP02Y  = f_df(L_JP02Y)
float v_CN10Y  = f_df(L_CN10Y)
float v_CN02Y  = f_df(L_CN02Y)
float v_EU10Y  = f_df(L_EU10Y)
float v_EU02Y  = f_df(L_EU02Y)
float v_GB10Y  = f_df(L_GB10Y)
float v_GB02Y  = f_df(L_GB02Y)
float v_US10Y  = f_df(L_US10Y)
float v_US02Y  = f_df(L_US02Y)
float v_US30Y  = f_df(L_US30Y)
float v_IN10Y  = f_df(L_IN10Y)
float v_IN02Y  = f_df(L_IN02Y)
float v_USDINR = f_df(L_USDINR)
float v_EURINR = f_df(L_EURINR)
float v_GBPINR = f_df(L_GBPINR)
float v_JPYINR = f_df(L_JPYINR)
float v_GOLD   = f_df(L_GOLD)
float v_SILVER = f_df(L_SILVER)
float v_USOIL  = f_df(L_USOIL)
float v_DXY    = f_df(L_DXY)
float v_CUSTOM = f_df(L_CUSTOM)
float yFit     = f_df(L_Y)

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  3. FACTOR CONSTRUCTION
//
//  Pooling is na-safe: a symbol your data plan does not carry drops out of its
//  factor instead of poisoning it. Because differencing is linear, the pool of
//  the constituents' returns is exactly the return of the pooled level.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_pool(float a, float b, float c, float d, float e, float f) =>
    float s = 0.0
    int   n = 0
    if not na(a)
        s := s + a
        n := n + 1
    if not na(b)
        s := s + b
        n := n + 1
    if not na(c)
        s := s + c
        n := n + 1
    if not na(d)
        s := s + d
        n := n + 1
    if not na(e)
        s := s + e
        n := n + 1
    if not na(f)
        s := s + f
        n := n + 1
    n > 0 ? s / n : na

f_sub(float a, float b) => na(a) or na(b) ? na : a - b

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  SELF-CONTAINMENT SCREEN  ·  do not hedge the target against itself
//
//  Chart TVC:GOLD and the "Precious metals" factor IS half gold; chart TVC:DXY
//  and the "Dollar" factor IS the chart symbol exactly. Measured on weekly
//  returns, the hedge then removes the target's own variance rather than a
//  macro exposure:
//        GOLD   vs Precious   ρ 0.91  →  82% of gold's own variance removed
//        SILVER vs Precious   ρ 0.98  →  96%
//        WTI    vs Energy     ρ 1.00  →  100%   (residual identically zero)
//        DXY    vs Dollar     ρ 1.00  →  100%
//        USDINR vs INR carry  ρ 0.72  →  51%
//
//  THE SCREEN RUNS ON CONSTITUENTS, NOT FACTORS, and that is the whole point:
//  at FACTOR level the two cases are provably inseparable. Charting gold gives
//  ρ = 0.91 against Precious, and charting TLT gives ρ = 0.91 against US rates
//  — identical numbers, opposite meanings, one self-hedging and one a genuine
//  duration relationship. No factor-level threshold can tell them apart.
//  At CONSTITUENT level they separate: self and alias pairs measure 1.000 while
//  the highest genuine constituent pair in the study was TLT against the US 30Y
//  at 0.986, so 0.99 sits in the gap.
//
//  Two screens, because neither alone suffices:
//    NAME  exact ticker match. Deterministic, no false positives, but blind to
//          aliases — most people chart gold as OANDA:XAUUSD or GC1!, not
//          TVC:GOLD.
//    |ρ|   catches those aliases at 0.99, measured over 200 CHART bars.
//          It is dormant until that window fills, so on a chart with fewer than
//          200 bars only the NAME screen is active. That is the deterministic
//          one, so the exact-ticker case is still covered; an ALIAS on a very
//          short chart is the residual gap. The window is deliberately long
//          rather than short: the verdict latches, so a false positive is
//          permanent, and TLT against the US 30Y already measures 0.986 — a
//          shorter window could push a genuine pair over 0.99 in a quiet
//          stretch and strip a real driver for good.
//  The verdict LATCHES: once a constituent is identified as the target it stays
//  excluded, so the basket cannot flicker as a rolling correlation wanders.
//
//  A dropped constituent leaves its pool via the na-safe path, so "Precious"
//  on a gold chart becomes silver alone — ρ 0.80 to gold, a real driver rather
//  than a mirror. A factor whose every constituent is dropped goes constant,
//  and a constant column has zero variance and can never be selected.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var string TK_SELF = ticker.standard(syminfo.tickerid)

f_isSelf(series float v, simple string sym) =>
    float c = ta.correlation(yFit, v, 200)
    bool nameHit = sym == TK_SELF or sym == syminfo.tickerid or sym == syminfo.ticker
    var bool latch = false
    latch := latch or nameHit or (not na(c) and math.abs(c) > 0.99)
    latch

// isSelf is SERIES, not simple: f_isSelf carries a `var bool` latch, and a
// latched value is by definition series-qualified. Declaring the parameter
// simple is what the compiler rejects.
f_drop(series float v, series bool isSelf) => isSelf ? na : v

bool s_JP10Y  = f_isSelf(v_JP10Y,  'TVC:JP10Y')
bool s_JP02Y  = f_isSelf(v_JP02Y,  'TVC:JP02Y')
bool s_CN10Y  = f_isSelf(v_CN10Y,  'TVC:CN10Y')
bool s_CN02Y  = f_isSelf(v_CN02Y,  'TVC:CN02Y')
bool s_EU10Y  = f_isSelf(v_EU10Y,  'TVC:EU10Y')
bool s_EU02Y  = f_isSelf(v_EU02Y,  'TVC:EU02Y')
bool s_GB10Y  = f_isSelf(v_GB10Y,  'TVC:GB10Y')
bool s_GB02Y  = f_isSelf(v_GB02Y,  'TVC:GB02Y')
bool s_US10Y  = f_isSelf(v_US10Y,  'TVC:US10Y')
bool s_US02Y  = f_isSelf(v_US02Y,  'TVC:US02Y')
bool s_US30Y  = f_isSelf(v_US30Y,  'TVC:US30Y')
bool s_IN10Y  = f_isSelf(v_IN10Y,  'TVC:IN10Y')
bool s_IN02Y  = f_isSelf(v_IN02Y,  'TVC:IN02Y')
bool s_USDINR = f_isSelf(v_USDINR, 'FX_IDC:USDINR')
bool s_EURINR = f_isSelf(v_EURINR, 'FX_IDC:EURINR')
bool s_GBPINR = f_isSelf(v_GBPINR, 'FX_IDC:GBPINR')
bool s_JPYINR = f_isSelf(v_JPYINR, 'FX_IDC:JPYINR')
bool s_GOLD   = f_isSelf(v_GOLD,   'TVC:GOLD')
bool s_SILVER = f_isSelf(v_SILVER, 'TVC:SILVER')
bool s_USOIL  = f_isSelf(v_USOIL,  'TVC:USOIL')
bool s_DXY    = f_isSelf(v_DXY,    'TVC:DXY')
// The custom driver faces the same two screens as every built-in constituent.
bool s_CUSTOM = f_isSelf(v_CUSTOM, custSym)

float k_JP10Y  = f_drop(v_JP10Y,  s_JP10Y)
float k_JP02Y  = f_drop(v_JP02Y,  s_JP02Y)
float k_CN10Y  = f_drop(v_CN10Y,  s_CN10Y)
float k_CN02Y  = f_drop(v_CN02Y,  s_CN02Y)
float k_EU10Y  = f_drop(v_EU10Y,  s_EU10Y)
float k_EU02Y  = f_drop(v_EU02Y,  s_EU02Y)
float k_GB10Y  = f_drop(v_GB10Y,  s_GB10Y)
float k_GB02Y  = f_drop(v_GB02Y,  s_GB02Y)
float k_US10Y  = f_drop(v_US10Y,  s_US10Y)
float k_US02Y  = f_drop(v_US02Y,  s_US02Y)
float k_US30Y  = f_drop(v_US30Y,  s_US30Y)
float k_IN10Y  = f_drop(v_IN10Y,  s_IN10Y)
float k_IN02Y  = f_drop(v_IN02Y,  s_IN02Y)
float k_USDINR = f_drop(v_USDINR, s_USDINR)
float k_EURINR = f_drop(v_EURINR, s_EURINR)
float k_GBPINR = f_drop(v_GBPINR, s_GBPINR)
float k_JPYINR = f_drop(v_JPYINR, s_JPYINR)
float k_GOLD   = f_drop(v_GOLD,   s_GOLD)
float k_SILVER = f_drop(v_SILVER, s_SILVER)
float k_USOIL  = f_drop(v_USOIL,  s_USOIL)
float k_DXY    = f_drop(v_DXY,    s_DXY)


float fac1 = f_pool(k_JP10Y, k_CN10Y, k_EU10Y, k_GB10Y, k_US10Y, k_IN10Y)
float fac2 = f_pool(f_sub(k_JP10Y, k_JP02Y), f_sub(k_CN10Y, k_CN02Y), f_sub(k_EU10Y, k_EU02Y),
     f_sub(k_GB10Y, k_GB02Y), f_sub(k_US10Y, k_US02Y), f_sub(k_IN10Y, k_IN02Y))
float fac3 = f_pool(k_US02Y, k_US10Y, k_US30Y, na, na, na)
float fac4 = f_sub(k_US30Y, k_US02Y)
float fac5 = k_DXY
float fac6 = k_USOIL
float fac7 = f_pool(k_GOLD, k_SILVER, na, na, na, na)
float fac8 = f_pool(k_USDINR, k_EURINR, k_GBPINR, k_JPYINR, na, na)
float fac9 = f_drop(v_CUSTOM, s_CUSTOM)

bool customOk = inpCustom and usy_sym != "" and usy_sym != ticker.standard(syminfo.tickerid) and usy_sym != syminfo.tickerid and usy_sym != syminfo.ticker

// The nine factor names, in column order - for the panel's driver basket. The
// same names Samanvaya's table carried; strings only, no request.
var array<string> DRV_NAMES = array.from("Global rates · level", "Global rates · slope", "US rates · level",
     "US curve · 2s30s", "Dollar · DXY", "Energy · WTI", "Precious metals", "INR carry basket",
     usy_sym == "" ? "Custom driver" : usy_sym)

// What the driver-timing rule did on this bar, for the panel: how many drivers
// were read a bar late, and the largest lateness KEPT, in hours - the most the
// last bar can still revise by.
f_lag(float lf) => lf > DRIVER_LATE ? 1 : 0
f_kept(float lf) => lf > DRIVER_LATE ? 0.0 : lf
int   drvLagged = f_lag(lf_JP10Y) + f_lag(lf_JP02Y) + f_lag(lf_CN10Y) + f_lag(lf_CN02Y) + f_lag(lf_EU10Y) +
     f_lag(lf_EU02Y) + f_lag(lf_GB10Y) + f_lag(lf_GB02Y) + f_lag(lf_US10Y) + f_lag(lf_US02Y) + f_lag(lf_US30Y) +
     f_lag(lf_IN10Y) + f_lag(lf_IN02Y) + f_lag(lf_USDINR) + f_lag(lf_EURINR) + f_lag(lf_GBPINR) + f_lag(lf_JPYINR) +
     f_lag(lf_GOLD) + f_lag(lf_SILVER) + f_lag(lf_USOIL) + f_lag(lf_DXY) + (customOk ? f_lag(lf_CUSTOM) : 0)
float drvKeptH  = math.max(f_kept(lf_JP10Y), f_kept(lf_JP02Y), f_kept(lf_CN10Y), f_kept(lf_CN02Y), f_kept(lf_EU10Y),
     f_kept(lf_EU02Y), f_kept(lf_GB10Y), f_kept(lf_GB02Y), f_kept(lf_US10Y), f_kept(lf_US02Y), f_kept(lf_US30Y),
     f_kept(lf_IN10Y), f_kept(lf_IN02Y), f_kept(lf_USDINR), f_kept(lf_EURINR), f_kept(lf_GBPINR), f_kept(lf_JPYINR),
     f_kept(lf_GOLD), f_kept(lf_SILVER), f_kept(lf_USOIL), f_kept(lf_DXY), customOk ? f_kept(lf_CUSTOM) : 0.0) *
     timeframe.in_seconds() / 3600.0

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  4. THE ANCHOR SAMPLE
//
//  One observation is taken per CLOSED anchor bar and pushed into a circular
//  buffer of length corrLen, carrying Y in column 0 and the nine factors in
//  columns 1-9. Incremental sums maintain the full 10x10 cross-product matrix in
//  O(1) per anchor bar, so the entire correlation matrix is available every bar
//  without a single ta.* call and without re-scanning the window.
//
//  Values are stored as deviations from a fixed per-series base captured at the
//  first insertion. Correlations and regression slopes are shift-invariant, so
//  this costs nothing — but it turns Σx² ≈ 21.2 with a variance of 0.0004 (log
//  DXY over a decade) into a well-conditioned sum, recovering roughly five
//  significant digits that naive centring throws away.
//
//  The sample is read `holdOut` anchor periods in arrears via a small FIFO, so
//  the coefficients applied to any period were fitted on data that ended holdOut
//  periods earlier - which is what makes the skill measure in section 10
//  genuinely out-of-sample. A queue rather than a history offset because holdOut
//  is counted in ANCHOR periods: 12 daily periods is 900 chart bars on a
//  5-minute chart, far past any sane max_bars_back.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
int  anchorT   = time(anchorTF)
bool newAnchor = not na(anchorT) and not na(anchorT[1]) and anchorT != anchorT[1]

var matrix<float> BUF  = matrix.new<float>(corrLen, 10, 0.0)
var matrix<float> MS   = matrix.new<float>(10, 10, 0.0)
var array<float>  VS   = array.new_float(10, 0.0)
var array<float>  BASE = array.new_float(10, na)
var array<float>  CUR  = array.new_float(10, na)
var array<float>  INS  = array.new_float(10, na)
var array<float>  QQ   = array.new_float(0)
var int nObs = 0
var int wptr = 0

// ANCHOR-PERIOD RETURNS. An observation is the anchor period's OWN return: the
// sum of the chart-bar returns inside it, for the target and every factor. The
// port used to store the return of the period's LAST chart bar, which is the
// period's return only on a daily chart - on a 5m chart the whole macro fit ran
// on one closing 5-minute return a day, the hedge's measured skill collapsed,
// and value carried no macro information. Accumulated on every bar below.
var array<float> ACC = array.new_float(10, 0.0)
var int accYn    = 0      // bars with a target return in the open period
var int accBars  = 0      // chart bars in the open period
var int anchN    = 0      // closed periods seen
var int anchBars = 0      // chart bars across them

if newAnchor
    // the period that just CLOSED ended on the previous chart bar - never the
    // forming one, so nothing here can repaint
    array.set(CUR, 0, accYn > 0 ? array.get(ACC, 0) : na)
    for i = 1 to 8
        array.set(CUR, i, array.get(ACC, i))
    array.set(CUR, 9, customOk ? array.get(ACC, 9) : na)
    anchN    += 1
    anchBars += accBars

    // MISSING DATA IS PER-COLUMN, NEVER PER-ROW. Discarding a whole observation
    // because one factor is absent would empty the sample entirely whenever a
    // single symbol is outside the user's data plan — or simply whenever the
    // custom driver is switched off, which blanks column 9 by design. A missing
    // return is a zero: there was no change to observe. A factor that is NEVER
    // available stays constant, so its variance is zero, its correlation is zero
    // by the guard in f_corr, and it can never be selected. The column
    // disqualifies itself instead of taking the sample down.
    for i = 1 to 9
        if na(array.get(CUR, i))
            array.set(CUR, i, 0.0)

    // Y is the one column with no substitute: no dependent variable, no
    // observation.
    if not na(array.get(CUR, 0))
        for i = 0 to 9
            array.push(QQ, array.get(CUR, i))

        if array.size(QQ) >= (holdOut + 1) * 10
            for i = 0 to 9
                array.set(INS, i, array.shift(QQ))

            // base-shift against the first value each column ever showed
            for i = 0 to 9
                float vv = array.get(INS, i)
                if na(array.get(BASE, i)) and not na(vv)
                    array.set(BASE, i, vv)
                float bb = array.get(BASE, i)
                array.set(INS, i, na(vv) or na(bb) ? 0.0 : vv - bb)

            if nObs >= corrLen
                for i = 0 to 9
                    float oi = matrix.get(BUF, wptr, i)
                    array.set(VS, i, array.get(VS, i) - oi)
                    for j = 0 to 9
                        matrix.set(MS, i, j, matrix.get(MS, i, j) - oi * matrix.get(BUF, wptr, j))
                nObs := nObs - 1

            for i = 0 to 9
                float ci = array.get(INS, i)
                matrix.set(BUF, wptr, i, ci)
                array.set(VS, i, array.get(VS, i) + ci)
            for i = 0 to 9
                for j = 0 to 9
                    matrix.set(MS, i, j, matrix.get(MS, i, j) + matrix.get(BUF, wptr, i) * matrix.get(BUF, wptr, j))

            nObs := nObs + 1
            wptr := (wptr + 1) % corrLen

// open a new period on its first bar, then add THIS bar to the open period
if newAnchor
    for i = 0 to 9
        array.set(ACC, i, 0.0)
    accYn   := 0
    accBars := 0
array.set(ACC, 0, array.get(ACC, 0) + nz(yFit))
array.set(ACC, 1, array.get(ACC, 1) + nz(fac1))
array.set(ACC, 2, array.get(ACC, 2) + nz(fac2))
array.set(ACC, 3, array.get(ACC, 3) + nz(fac3))
array.set(ACC, 4, array.get(ACC, 4) + nz(fac4))
array.set(ACC, 5, array.get(ACC, 5) + nz(fac5))
array.set(ACC, 6, array.get(ACC, 6) + nz(fac6))
array.set(ACC, 7, array.get(ACC, 7) + nz(fac7))
array.set(ACC, 8, array.get(ACC, 8) + nz(fac8))
array.set(ACC, 9, array.get(ACC, 9) + nz(fac9))
if not na(yFit)
    accYn += 1
accBars += 1

float nF = nObs

// ── moment accessors ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
f_mean(i, n, vs, base) => n > 0 ? array.get(vs, i) / n + nz(array.get(base, i)) : na

f_sd(i, n, vs, ms) =>
    float s = array.get(vs, i)
    float v = n > 1 ? (matrix.get(ms, i, i) - s * s / n) / n : na
    na(v) ? na : math.sqrt(math.max(v, 0.0))

f_corr(i, j, n, vs, ms) =>
    float sx  = array.get(vs, i)
    float sy  = array.get(vs, j)
    float cNum = n * matrix.get(ms, i, j) - sx * sy
    float dx  = n * matrix.get(ms, i, i) - sx * sx
    float dy  = n * matrix.get(ms, j, j) - sy * sy
    float cDen = math.sqrt(math.max(dx, 0.0) * math.max(dy, 0.0))
    n > 2 and cDen > 1e-12 ? math.max(-1.0, math.min(1.0, cNum / cDen)) : 0.0

// partial correlation of x and y controlling for z, from pairwise correlations
f_part(rxy, rxz, ryz) =>
    float d = math.sqrt(math.max(1e-12, (1.0 - rxz * rxz) * (1.0 - ryz * ryz)))
    math.max(-1.0, math.min(1.0, (rxy - rxz * ryz) / d))

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  5. SIGNIFICANCE FLOOR SIZED TO THE SAMPLE ACTUALLY AVAILABLE
//
//  Fisher (1921): z = artanh(r) is approximately normal with SE = 1/√(n−3−k)
//  for a partial correlation with k controls. Inverting gives the smallest |ρ|
//  distinguishable from zero at level α:  |ρ|* = tanh(z_α / √(n−3−k)).
//
//  Šidák over the 9 candidates because we keep the best of nine, not a
//  pre-specified one: α′ = 1 − (1−0.05)^(1/9) ≈ 0.005683, two-sided z = 2.7655.
//
//     n = 200 → 0.195     n = 60 → 0.354     n = 40 → 0.431     n = 30 → 0.495
//
//  This is the whole answer to a short-history instrument. A fixed 0.10 floor
//  on 40 observations admits pure noise and calls it a macro driver.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_svTanh(float x) =>
    float e = math.exp(math.min(20.0, 2.0 * math.abs(x)))
    float t = (e - 1.0) / (e + 1.0)
    x < 0 ? -t : t

float zCrit = sigCorrect ? 2.7655 : 1.9600

f_critR(n, k, z) =>
    float df = n - 3.0 - k
    df > 0.0 ? f_svTanh(z / math.sqrt(df)) : 1.0

// critR1 / floor1 serve the panel only: driver ① is always applied (a spread
// needs an anchor), and the panel says when its |ρ| does not clear the floor.
float critR1 = f_critR(nF, 0, zCrit)
float critR2 = f_critR(nF, 1, zCrit)
float critR3 = f_critR(nF, 2, zCrit)
float floor1 = math.max(minRho, critR1)
float floor2 = math.max(minRho, critR2)
float floor3 = math.max(minRho, critR3)
bool  enoughData = nObs >= minObs

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  6. SELECTION  ·  closed-form stepwise partial correlation
//
//  Every score comes off the anchor correlation matrix, so this is pure
//  arithmetic — safe inside loops, and identical on every chart timeframe.
//  A near-duplicate of an already-chosen driver has partial correlation ≈0 by
//  construction and cannot be picked.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_argmax(scores, exA, exB) =>
    int   best  = 0
    float bestv = -1.0
    for i = 0 to array.size(scores) - 1
        if i != exA and i != exB
            float v = math.abs(array.get(scores, i))
            if v > bestv
                bestv := v
                best  := i
    best

// HYSTERESIS. Candidates inside the margin are a statistical tie; swapping
// between them on noise rotates the basket for no informational gain.
f_hold(prev, rawIdx, scores, marg, exA, exB) =>
    int out = rawIdx
    if not na(prev) and prev != rawIdx and prev != exA and prev != exB
        float sInc = math.abs(array.get(scores, prev))
        float sCha = math.abs(array.get(scores, rawIdx))
        out := sCha > sInc * (1.0 + marg) ? rawIdx : prev
    out

// step 1 · marginal correlation with Y
var array<float> S1 = array.new_float(9, 0.0)
for i = 0 to 8
    array.set(S1, i, f_corr(0, i + 1, nF, VS, MS))

var int hIdx1 = na
int raw1 = f_argmax(S1, -1, -1)
hIdx1 := f_hold(hIdx1, raw1, S1, hystMarg, -1, -1)
int idx1 = hIdx1

// step 2 · partial correlation given driver 1
var array<float> S2 = array.new_float(9, 0.0)
float ry1 = array.get(S1, idx1)
for i = 0 to 8
    float rk1 = f_corr(i + 1, idx1 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
    array.set(S2, i, i == idx1 ? 0.0 : f_part(array.get(S1, i), ry1, rk1))

var int hIdx2 = na
int raw2 = selStep ? f_argmax(S2, idx1, -1) : f_argmax(S1, idx1, -1)
hIdx2 := f_hold(hIdx2, raw2, selStep ? S2 : S1, hystMarg, idx1, -1)
int idx2 = hIdx2

// step 3 · partial correlation given drivers 1 and 2
var array<float> S3 = array.new_float(9, 0.0)
float r12  = f_corr(idx1 + 1, idx2 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
float ry2  = array.get(S1, idx2)
float ry21 = f_part(ry2, ry1, r12)
for i = 0 to 8
    float rk1  = f_corr(i + 1, idx1 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
    float rk2  = f_corr(i + 1, idx2 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
    float rk21 = f_part(rk2, rk1, r12)
    float ryk1 = f_part(array.get(S1, i), ry1, rk1)
    array.set(S3, i, i == idx1 or i == idx2 ? 0.0 : f_part(ryk1, ry21, rk21))

var int hIdx3 = na
int raw3 = selStep ? f_argmax(S3, idx1, idx2) : f_argmax(S1, idx1, idx2)
hIdx3 := f_hold(hIdx3, raw3, selStep ? S3 : S1, hystMarg, idx1, idx2)
int idx3 = hIdx3

// ── admission ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
float q1 = math.abs(array.get(S1, idx1))      // panel only - see floor1
float q2 = math.abs(selStep ? array.get(S2, idx2) : array.get(S1, idx2))
float q3 = math.abs(selStep ? array.get(S3, idx3) : array.get(S1, idx3))

int kEff = 1
kEff := inpNumVars >= 2 and q2 >= floor2 ? 2 : 1
kEff := inpNumVars >= 3 and q2 >= floor2 and q3 >= floor3 ? 3 : kEff

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  7. ROTATION DETECTION
//  Set membership, not order: swapping ranks 2 and 3 reorders Gram-Schmidt but
//  spans the same subspace, so the fit is unchanged and it is not a rotation.
//  Carried in because Samanvaya's ▲▼ are only tradable once the basket has
//  been stable for a representative window - and drawn in v6 as the ⟳.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_inSet(v, a, b, c) => v == a or v == b or v == c

int pv1 = nz(idx1[1], idx1)
int pv2 = nz(idx2[1], idx2)
int pv3 = nz(idx3[1], idx3)
bool sameSet = f_inSet(idx1, pv1, pv2, pv3) and f_inSet(idx2, pv1, pv2, pv3) and f_inSet(idx3, pv1, pv2, pv3)
bool rotated = not barstate.isfirst and (not sameSet or kEff != nz(kEff[1], kEff))

var int barsSinceRot = 0
barsSinceRot := rotated ? 0 : barsSinceRot + 1
bool basketWarm = barsSinceRot >= Z3

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  8. REGRESSION  ·  Gram-Schmidt in correlation space, with ridge
//
//  Working in correlation space means var(u₁) = 1 exactly, so the ridge penalty
//  reads directly as a fraction and the orthogonalization needs nothing but the
//  correlation matrix. Coefficients are then converted back to natural units so
//  they can be applied to chart-timeframe data.
//
//  λ SCALES WITH 1/n. The optimal ridge penalty grows as the noise-to-signal
//  ratio of the estimate grows, and that ratio is ~1/n. A 50-observation
//  history therefore gets four times the shrinkage of a 200-observation one.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float lamEff = ridgeLam * math.max(1.0, 200.0 / math.max(nF, 1.0))

float r13 = f_corr(idx1 + 1, idx3 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
float r23 = f_corr(idx2 + 1, idx3 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
float ry3 = array.get(S1, idx3)

float a21 = r12
float vu2 = math.max(0.0, 1.0 - r12 * r12)
float a31 = r13
float a32 = vu2 > 1e-10 ? (r23 - r13 * r12) / vu2 : 0.0
float vu3 = math.max(0.0, 1.0 - r13 * r13 - a32 * a32 * vu2)

float c1 = ry1
float c2 = ry2 - a21 * ry1
float c3 = ry3 - a31 * ry1 - a32 * c2

float gb1 = kEff >= 1 ? c1 / (1.0 + lamEff) : 0.0
float gb2 = kEff >= 2 ? c2 / (vu2 + lamEff) : 0.0
float gb3 = kEff >= 3 ? c3 / (vu3 + lamEff) : 0.0

// back-substitution to per-driver weights on the standardized axes:
//   u₃ = z₃ − a₃₂z₂ − (a₃₁ − a₃₂a₂₁)z₁ ,  u₂ = z₂ − a₂₁z₁
float w3 = gb3
float w2 = gb2 - a32 * gb3
float w1 = gb1 - a21 * gb2 - (a31 - a21 * a32) * gb3

float sdY = f_sd(0, nF, VS, MS)
float sd1 = f_sd(idx1 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
float sd2 = f_sd(idx2 + 1, nF, VS, MS)
float sd3 = f_sd(idx3 + 1, nF, VS, MS)

// natural-unit hedge ratios: move in Y per one unit of the driver
float h1 = not na(sd1) and sd1 > 1e-12 and not na(sdY) ? w1 * sdY / sd1 : 0.0
float h2 = not na(sd2) and sd2 > 1e-12 and not na(sdY) ? w2 * sdY / sd2 : 0.0
float h3 = not na(sd3) and sd3 > 1e-12 and not na(sdY) ? w3 * sdY / sd3 : 0.0

float mY  = f_mean(0, nF, VS, BASE)
float mX1 = f_mean(idx1 + 1, nF, VS, BASE)
float mX2 = f_mean(idx2 + 1, nF, VS, BASE)
float mX3 = f_mean(idx3 + 1, nF, VS, BASE)
float icept = na(mY) ? na : mY - h1 * mX1 - h2 * mX2 - h3 * mX3

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  9. APPLYING THE ANCHORED MODEL TO CHART-TIMEFRAME DATA
//
//  The intercept is the one quantity that does not transfer unchanged: it is a
//  drift per anchor bar, so it is scaled by the ratio of chart bar to anchor
//  bar — a weekly alpha spread across the bars that make up the week.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float secChart  = timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period)
float secAnchor = timeframe.in_seconds(anchorTF)
float tfRatio   = secAnchor > 0 ? math.min(1.0, secChart / secAnchor) : 1.0
// The intercept is a drift per anchor PERIOD. It is spread over the chart bars a
// period actually holds - measured, so a 6¼-hour session is not treated as 24
// hours - with the clock ratio only until the first period has closed.
float barsPerAnchor = anchN > 0 ? float(anchBars) / anchN : (tfRatio > 0 ? 1.0 / tfRatio : 1.0)
float iceptUse  = na(icept) ? na : icept / math.max(1.0, barsPerAnchor)

float f1c = idx1 == 0 ? fac1 : idx1 == 1 ? fac2 : idx1 == 2 ? fac3 : idx1 == 3 ? fac4 : idx1 == 4 ? fac5 : idx1 == 5 ? fac6 : idx1 == 6 ? fac7 : idx1 == 7 ? fac8 : fac9
float f2c = idx2 == 0 ? fac1 : idx2 == 1 ? fac2 : idx2 == 2 ? fac3 : idx2 == 3 ? fac4 : idx2 == 4 ? fac5 : idx2 == 5 ? fac6 : idx2 == 6 ? fac7 : idx2 == 7 ? fac8 : fac9
float f3c = idx3 == 0 ? fac1 : idx3 == 1 ? fac2 : idx3 == 2 ? fac3 : idx3 == 3 ? fac4 : idx3 == 4 ? fac5 : idx3 == 5 ? fac6 : idx3 == 6 ? fac7 : idx3 == 7 ? fac8 : fac9

float y_hat    = na(iceptUse) or na(yFit) ? na : iceptUse + h1 * nz(f1c) + h2 * nz(f2c) + h3 * nz(f3c)
float residFul = na(y_hat) or na(yFit) ? na : yFit - y_hat

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  10. THE HEDGE WEIGHS ITSELF
//
//  This is the one genuinely new mechanism, and it exists because the study
//  rejected the alternative. A full macro hedge predicted WORSE than no hedge
//  at every frequency tested, and the correlation between how much the basket
//  explained and whether hedging helped was +0.11 — nothing. So the hedge is
//  applied in proportion to how well it has actually been working:
//
//      s = clip( 1 − Σε² / Σr² , 0 , 1 )     over the last skillWin anchor bars
//      ε_used = (1−s)·r_Y + s·(r_Y − ŷ)
//
//  s = 0 collapses the spread to Y's own cumulative return, which is the
//  benchmark that won the comparison. s = 1 is the full hedge, which is right
//  when the basket has earned it. TLT measured 0.97, INFY 0.04.
//
//  Both sums run on the ANCHOR clock and use the residual as it stood at each
//  closed anchor bar — coefficients that were genuinely in force at the time —
//  so this is measured skill, never a look-back-fitted number.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var array<float> SKQ = array.new_float(0)
var float SK_e = 0.0
var float SK_y = 0.0
var int   SK_n = 0

// Each skill observation is one anchor PERIOD: the residual and the target
// return summed over its chart bars, each with the coefficients in force then.
var float skRes = 0.0
var float skY   = 0.0
var int   skN   = 0

if newAnchor and skN > 0
    float ee = skRes * skRes
    float yy = skY * skY
    array.push(SKQ, ee)
    array.push(SKQ, yy)
    SK_e := SK_e + ee
    SK_y := SK_y + yy
    SK_n := SK_n + 1
    if SK_n > skillWin
        SK_e := SK_e - array.shift(SKQ)
        SK_y := SK_y - array.shift(SKQ)
        SK_n := SK_n - 1
if newAnchor
    skRes := 0.0
    skY   := 0.0
    skN   := 0
if not na(residFul) and not na(yFit)
    skRes += residFul
    skY   += yFit
    skN   += 1

float skillR2 = SK_n >= 20 and SK_y > 1e-18 ? 1.0 - SK_e / SK_y : na
float sAuto   = na(skillR2) ? 0.0 : math.max(0.0, math.min(1.0, skillR2))
float hedgeW  = hedgeMode == "Off" ? 0.0 : hedgeMode == "Full" ? 1.0 : sAuto

// ε_used interpolates between the raw return and the fully hedged residual.
float resid = na(yFit) ? na : na(residFul) ? yFit : yFit - hedgeW * (yFit - residFul)

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  11. THE SPREAD  ·  running sum of the residual return, i.e. the log P&L of
//  the book as actually hedged. At hedgeW = 0 this is simply Y's own path.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
bool  modelBuilt = nObs >= 10
bool  modelReady = modelBuilt and not na(resid)
float spread     = ta.cum(modelReady ? resid : 0.0)

// ── ENSEMBLE MEMBERS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// One member per timescale: sample z of the spread over that window, then the
// high-pass. The drift correction is applied PER MEMBER before averaging, not
// after: each window has its own drift to remove, and averaging first would
// leave the fast members carrying the slow members' trend.
f_member(series float sp, simple int zl, simple int dl) =>
    float sd = ta.stdev(sp, zl)
    float m  = ta.sma(sp, zl)
    float z  = sd > 1e-14 ? (sp - m) / sd : 0.0
    float e  = ta.ema(z, dl)          // evaluated unconditionally, masked after
    useDrift ? z - e : z

float m1 = f_member(spread, Z1, driftLen)
float m2 = f_member(spread, Z2, driftLen)
float m3 = f_member(spread, Z3, driftLen)
float m4 = f_member(spread, Z4, driftLen)
float m5 = f_member(spread, Z5, driftLen)

// AVERAGING SHRINKS THE SCALE, AND θ HAS TO KEEP MEANING THE SAME THING.
// The five members are views of ONE spread, so they are highly correlated —
// measured mean pairwise ρ = 0.734 — and the mean of k correlated unit-variance
// series has sd √((1+(k−1)ρ)/k) = √((1+4·0.734)/5) = 0.887, which is exactly the
// ratio measured on real data (0.887 mean; 0.862-0.900 across ten instruments
// as different as TLT, INFY, COPPER and FTSE — a 4% spread, so a constant is
// safe here). Left uncorrected the ensemble would sit inside θ far more often:
// bars beyond |z|>2 fell from 10.2% to 5.8%, halving the entry rate silently
// while looking like a pure refactor. Dividing restores single-window
// semantics, and being a monotone rescale it cannot change rank information.
float ENS_SCALE = 0.887
float z_hp    = (m1 + m2 + m3 + m4 + m5) / 5.0 / ENS_SCALE
float z_score = z_hp

// MEMBER AGREEMENT (panel only) - Samanvaya's breadth question asked of the RV
// leg: how many of the five timescales independently sit beyond θ, and on which
// side. Five of five and three of five can share an average and mean very
// different things; the average alone cannot tell them apart.
int agreeRich  = (m1 >  entryThresh ? 1 : 0) + (m2 >  entryThresh ? 1 : 0) + (m3 >  entryThresh ? 1 : 0) + (m4 >  entryThresh ? 1 : 0) + (m5 >  entryThresh ? 1 : 0)
int agreeCheap = (m1 < -entryThresh ? 1 : 0) + (m2 < -entryThresh ? 1 : 0) + (m3 < -entryThresh ? 1 : 0) + (m4 < -entryThresh ? 1 : 0) + (m5 < -entryThresh ? 1 : 0)

f_softBound(float x) =>
    x / (1.0 + math.abs(x))

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  LEG 2 · BREADTH  ·  the price-only half of Tattva's Swayam self-ensemble
//
//  Seven causal views of Y's OWN price: five timescales running the full Market
//  Strength Factor, plus the fastest as momentum-only and the slowest as
//  flow-only. Ported line-for-line from engines/swayam/kernel.py with the
//  primitives byte-matched to pandas — zscore_clipped's shift(1) and ddof=1,
//  ATR as ewm(alpha=1/14) ≡ ta.atr, close.diff(5).diff(5) expanded to
//  close − 2·close[5] + close[10], and the √n that cancels in the MSF combine.
//
//  The eight MACRO-anchored members of the full 15-member ensemble are absent
//  by necessity, not choice: they select from ~100 external series per bar,
//  which no Pine script can fetch. This is the price-action half, exactly.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_sig(float x, float s) => 2.0 / (1.0 + math.exp(-x / s)) - 1.0
// Locals renamed from m1/sd1 to zcM/zcSd: in the standalone Swayam port those
// names were free, but this file defines a top-level ensemble member m1 and a
// driver deviation sd1, and a function local silently shadowing a global is a
// hazard whether or not the compiler allows it.
f_zc(float src, int win, float clp) =>
    float zcM  = ta.sma(src, win)[1]
    float zcSd = ta.stdev(src, win, false)[1]
    float mm = na(zcM) ? 0.0 : zcM
    float z = (na(zcSd) or zcSd == 0.0) ? na : (src - mm) / zcSd
    na(z) ? 0.0 : math.max(-clp, math.min(clp, z))
f_msf(int _len, int _roc, int _mask) =>
    float roc_raw = (close - close[_roc]) / close[_roc]
    float momentum_norm = f_sig(f_zc(roc_raw, _len, clipZ), 1.5)
    float intrabar_dir = (high + low) / 2.0 - open
    float vol_ma = ta.sma(volume, _len)
    float vol_ratio = (na(vol_ma) or vol_ma == 0.0) ? 1.0 : volume / vol_ma
    float vw_direction = ta.sma(intrabar_dir * vol_ratio, _len)
    float vw_impact = ta.sma((close - close[5]) * vol_ratio, _len)
    float micro_norm = f_sig(f_zc(vw_direction - vw_impact, _len, clipZ), 1.5)
    float trend_slow = ta.sma(close, _len)
    float trend_diff_z = f_zc(ta.sma(close, 5) - trend_slow, _len, clipZ)
    float mom_accel_z = f_zc(close - 2.0 * close[5] + close[10], _len, clipZ)
    float atrv = ta.atr(14)
    float vol_adj_mom_z = f_zc((close - close[5]) / atrv, _len, clipZ)
    float mean_rev_z = f_zc(close - trend_slow, _len, clipZ)
    float composite_trend_z = (trend_diff_z + mom_accel_z + vol_adj_mom_z + mean_rev_z) / 2.0 
    float composite_trend_norm = f_sig(composite_trend_z, 1.5)
    float typical_price = (high + low + close) / 3.0
    float mf = typical_price * volume
    float mf_pos = close > close[1] ? mf : 0.0
    float mf_neg = close < close[1] ? mf : 0.0
    float mf_pos_smooth = ta.sma(mf_pos, _len)
    float mf_neg_smooth = ta.sma(mf_neg, _len)
    float mf_total = mf_pos_smooth + mf_neg_smooth
    float accum_ratio = (na(mf_total) or mf_total == 0.0) ? 0.5 : mf_pos_smooth / mf_total
    float accum_norm = 2.0 * (accum_ratio - 0.5)
    float pctc = (close - close[1]) / close[1]
    float regime_signal = pctc > 0.0033 ? 1.0 : pctc < -0.0033 ? -1.0 : 0.0
    float regime_count = ta.cum(regime_signal)
    float regime_norm = f_sig(f_zc(regime_count - ta.sma(regime_count, _len), _len, clipZ), 1.5)
    float structure = (micro_norm + composite_trend_norm) / math.sqrt(2.0)
    float flow = (accum_norm + regime_norm) / math.sqrt(2.0)
    float msum = _mask == 0 ? (momentum_norm + structure + flow) : _mask == 1 ? momentum_norm : _mask == 2 ? structure : flow
    f_sig(msum, 1.0)

f_unified_msf(float _msf, float _rs, float _bw) =>
    float msf_cl = math.pow(math.abs(_msf), _rs)
    float msf_w_ad = msf_cl / (msf_cl + 0.001) 
    float msf_w_final = 0.5 * _bw + 0.5 * msf_w_ad
    float mmr_w_final = 0.5 * (1.0 - _bw)
    float msf_w_norm = msf_w_final / (msf_w_final + mmr_w_final)
    math.max(-1.0, math.min(1.0, msf_w_norm * _msf))

int rb1 = int(math.max(5.0, math.round(rocFrac * B1)))
int rb2 = int(math.max(5.0, math.round(rocFrac * B2)))
int rb3 = int(math.max(5.0, math.round(rocFrac * B3)))
int rb4 = int(math.max(5.0, math.round(rocFrac * B4)))
int rb5 = int(math.max(5.0, math.round(rocFrac * B5)))

// ONE DEFINITION of the breadth leg, run by the chart and by every value rung,
// so a rung's breadth is the same quantity the chart blends: five timescales at
// full MSF, the fastest as momentum only and the slowest as flow only.
//
// VOLUME DEGENERACY. Flow is the only MSF component that needs real volume, so
// a flow-ONLY member is dropped on instruments that do not report it (index
// levels, several FX and futures feeds). Streaming approximation of Tattva's
// full-sample (volume>0).mean() >= 0.5 test.
f_breadthRaw() =>
    float br0 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B1, rb1, 0), regimeSens, baseWeight)
    float br1 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B2, rb2, 0), regimeSens, baseWeight)
    float br2 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B3, rb3, 0), regimeSens, baseWeight)
    float br3 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B4, rb4, 0), regimeSens, baseWeight)
    float br4 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B5, rb5, 0), regimeSens, baseWeight)
    float br5 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B1, rb1, 1), regimeSens, baseWeight)   // fastest · momentum
    float br6 = f_unified_msf(f_msf(B5, rb5, 3), regimeSens, baseWeight)   // slowest · flow
    float bVp = ta.cum((na(volume) or volume == 0.0) ? 0.0 : 1.0)
    bool  bVo = (bVp / (bar_index + 1)) >= 0.5
    [(br0 + br1 + br2 + br3 + br4 + br5 + (bVo ? br6 : 0.0)) * 10.0 / (bVo ? 7.0 : 6.0), bVo]

// A value rung's breadth, held na until the slowest member has a full window.
f_breadth() =>
    [bZ, bVo] = f_breadthRaw()
    bar_index > 2 * B5 + 20 ? bZ : na

[breadth_z, volOk] = f_breadthRaw()

// Each member is bounded by (baseWeight+1)/2, so at baseWeight = 0 every one
// lies in (-0.5, 0.5) and their mean ×10 in (-5, 5) — measured range over
// 145,411 member-bars was -4.375 .. +4.481. In these units the mean has
// sd ≈ 0.97, i.e. already a unit-variance score, so it blends against the RV
// z directly with no rescale.
float rv_z      = z_score

// LEG DISAGREEMENT. The legs correlate ~0.7, so the times they point opposite
// ways are uncommon and therefore informative. In v6 it dims the trace - the
// value reading is less coherent than its level suggests - and turns the
// panel's legs row gold, instead of Samanvaya's × on the zero line.
bool legsSplit = (rv_z > 0) != (breadth_z > 0) and math.abs(rv_z) > 0.5 and math.abs(breadth_z) > 0.5

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  INTEGRATION  ·  blend in z-space, restore variance by measurement, bound once
//
//  For legs with weights w and (1−w), unit variance and correlation ρ, the mix
//  has sd √(w² + (1−w)² + 2w(1−w)ρ). Dividing by that restores amplitude for ANY
//  mix and degenerates correctly at the endpoints: at w = 0.5, ρ = 0 it is the
//  familiar √2 = 1.414, at w = 0.5, ρ = 0.71 (measured here) it is 1.081, and at
//  w = 0 or 1 it is exactly 1 because a single leg needs no restoration.
//  The soft-bound is applied ONCE, after the blend — never to a leg first.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float rho_legs = ta.correlation(rv_z, breadth_z, varCorrLen)
float rho_c    = math.max(-0.90, math.min(0.99, nz(rho_legs, 0.71)))
float wRV      = legMix
float wBR      = 1.0 - legMix
float mixVar   = wRV * wRV + wBR * wBR + 2.0 * wRV * wBR * rho_c
float varCorr  = mixVar > 1e-9 ? 1.0 / math.sqrt(mixVar) : 1.0

float unified_z   = (wRV * rv_z + wBR * breadth_z) * varCorr
// Samanvaya's unified reading on its ±100 scale - THE TRACE in v6, and the value
// its crossing of θ arms a ▲▼ with.
float unified_osc = math.max(-100.0, math.min(100.0, f_softBound(unified_z * gain) * 100.0))

// θ on the trace's ±100 scale: the signal rail, the shading knee of the trace
// and the value tape, and the height of a 3σ column.
float osc_entry = f_softBound(entryThresh * gain) * 100.0

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  MTF LADDER · the engine behind the value tape
//
//  Driven by its own "MTF value ladder" input, default Ladder up as in
//  Samanvaya, independent of the conviction ladder. Rung choice is Samanvaya's:
//  up to five frames above the chart (weekly adds M), or the THREE nearest below
//  it, breadth only. The chart rung is the trace itself. The one addition to
//  Samanvaya's ladder is FALLBACK - a direction with no frames here reads the
//  other.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var array<int> TP_UP = array.from(240, 60, 30, 15, 5)
var array<int> TP_DN = array.from(240, 60, 30, 15, 5, 3, 1)      // nearest first

int  tpSec   = timeframe.in_seconds()
int  tpTm    = int(math.round(tpSec / 60.0))
bool tpMin   = timeframe.isintraday and tpSec >= 60 and tpSec % 60 == 0
bool tpDay   = timeframe.isdaily   and timeframe.multiplier == 1
bool tpWeek  = timeframe.isweekly  and timeframe.multiplier == 1
bool tpMonth = timeframe.ismonthly and timeframe.multiplier == 1
bool tpUpAv  = tpDay or tpWeek or (tpMin and tpTm < 240 and 240 % tpTm == 0)
bool tpDnAv  = tpMonth or tpWeek or tpDay or (tpMin and tpTm > 1)
bool vWantUp = inpVLad == "Ladder up"
bool vWantDn = inpVLad == "Ladder down"
bool tpUp    = (vWantUp and tpUpAv) or (vWantDn and not tpDnAv and tpUpAv)
bool tpDn    = (vWantDn and tpDnAv) or (vWantUp and not tpUpAv and tpDnAv)
bool tpFallback = (vWantUp and tpDn) or (vWantDn and tpUp)

// Up: the frames above the chart, top → down. Down: the three nearest below.
string up1 = ""
string up2 = ""
string up3 = ""
string up4 = ""
string up5 = ""
string dn1 = ""
string dn2 = ""
string dn3 = ""
if tpUp
    if tpDay
        up1 := "W"
    else if tpWeek
        up1 := "M"
    else if tpMin and tpTm < 240
        int nU = 0
        for f in TP_UP
            if f > tpTm and f % tpTm == 0
                nU += 1
                string sf = str.tostring(f)
                if nU == 1
                    up1 := sf
                else if nU == 2
                    up2 := sf
                else if nU == 3
                    up3 := sf
                else if nU == 4
                    up4 := sf
                else
                    up5 := sf
if tpDn
    if tpMonth
        dn1 := "W"
        dn2 := "D"
    else if tpWeek
        dn1 := "D"
        dn2 := "240"
        dn3 := "60"
    else if tpDay
        dn1 := "240"
        dn2 := "60"
        dn3 := "30"
    else if tpMin
        int nD = 0
        for f in TP_DN
            if nD < 3 and f < tpTm and tpTm % f == 0
                nD += 1
                string sf = str.tostring(f)
                if nD == 1
                    dn1 := sf
                else if nD == 2
                    dn2 := sf
                else
                    dn3 := sf
bool tpMultiUp = up1 != ""
bool tpMultiDn = dn1 != ""
bool tpOn      = tpMultiUp or tpMultiDn

f_tpLbl(string t) =>
    string o = t
    if t != "" and t != "D" and t != "W" and t != "M"
        int n = int(str.tonumber(t))
        o := n % 60 == 0 ? str.tostring(n / 60) + "h" : t + "m"
    o
f_tpJoin(string acc, string t) => t == "" ? acc : acc + f_tpLbl(t) + "·"
string tpChartLbl = tpMin ? f_tpLbl(str.tostring(tpTm)) : tpDay ? "D" : tpWeek ? "W" : tpMonth ? "M" : timeframe.period
string tpLadLbl = tpMultiUp ? f_tpJoin(f_tpJoin(f_tpJoin(f_tpJoin(f_tpJoin("", up1), up2), up3), up4), up5) + tpChartLbl :
     f_tpJoin(f_tpJoin(f_tpJoin("", dn3), dn2), dn1) + tpChartLbl

// ── the breadth leg on a rung: f_breadth(), defined with the chart's leg ─────

// A higher frame hands over breadth at its last CLOSED bar, plus the open time
// of the bar now forming - known the moment it opens, so nothing leaks.
f_tpUpReq() =>
    float bZ = f_breadth()
    [bZ[1], time]

// A lower frame's breadth, averaged across its bars inside this chart bar.
f_tpInside(array<float> a) =>
    float s = 0.0
    int   k = 0
    if not na(a)
        for v in a
            if not na(v)
                s += v
                k += 1
    k > 0 ? s / k : na

// ── the RV leg on a higher frame, rebuilt from this chart's spread ───────────
// A higher frame's spread is this spread sampled at that frame's closes. Each
// rung keeps its settled samples and the settled drift EMA of each of the five
// ensemble members; every chart bar it finishes the ensemble with the current
// spread as the forming bar's close - the same arithmetic as section 12, in
// that frame's bars.
type TpRung
    array<float> smp      // settled samples, newest last
    array<float> ema      // per member: settled drift EMA
    array<float> s1       // per member: Σ of its last (window − 1) settled samples
    array<float> s2       // per member: Σ of their squares

f_tpZl(int k) => k == 0 ? Z1 : k == 1 ? Z2 : k == 2 ? Z3 : k == 3 ? Z4 : Z5

// A member's z with the forming value as the window's last observation. The
// window sums are kept rolling, so this is O(1) rather than a loop over up to
// 55 samples per member, per rung, per bar - the same numbers.
f_tpZ(TpRung r, int k, float cur) =>
    int   zl  = f_tpZl(k)
    float out = na
    if not na(cur) and array.size(r.smp) >= zl - 1
        float a1  = array.get(r.s1, k) + cur
        float a2  = array.get(r.s2, k) + cur * cur
        float mu  = a1 / zl
        float sdv = math.sqrt(math.max(a2 / zl - mu * mu, 0.0))
        out := sdv > 1e-14 ? (cur - mu) / sdv : 0.0
    out

// Freeze a closed parent bar: each member's final z enters its drift EMA, then
// the close enters the sample.
f_tpSettle(TpRung r, float cClosed, float al) =>
    for k = 0 to 4
        float zk = f_tpZ(r, k, cClosed)
        if not na(zk)
            float e0 = array.get(r.ema, k)
            array.set(r.ema, k, na(e0) ? zk : al * zk + (1.0 - al) * e0)
    array.push(r.smp, cClosed)
    int ns = array.size(r.smp)
    for k = 0 to 4
        int wn = f_tpZl(k) - 1
        array.set(r.s1, k, array.get(r.s1, k) + cClosed)
        array.set(r.s2, k, array.get(r.s2, k) + cClosed * cClosed)
        if ns > wn
            float vOut = array.get(r.smp, ns - 1 - wn)
            array.set(r.s1, k, array.get(r.s1, k) - vOut)
            array.set(r.s2, k, array.get(r.s2, k) - vOut * vOut)
    if array.size(r.smp) > Z5
        array.shift(r.smp)

// The forming parent bar: members finished with the current spread, the drift
// EMA advanced one step in arrears, exactly as ta.ema would at that close.
f_tpLive(TpRung r, float cur, float al) =>
    float acc = 0.0
    bool  okk = true
    for k = 0 to 4
        float zk = f_tpZ(r, k, cur)
        if na(zk)
            okk := false
        else
            float e0 = array.get(r.ema, k)
            float ek = na(e0) ? zk : al * zk + (1.0 - al) * e0
            acc += useDrift ? zk - ek : zk
    okk ? acc / 5.0 / ENS_SCALE : na

float tpAl = 2.0 / (driftLen + 1)

// A new parent bar opened on this chart bar, so the previous chart bar closed
// the last one: settle its spread, then read the forming bar live.
f_tpStep(TpRung r, int t) =>
    if not na(t) and not na(t[1]) and t != t[1] and modelBuilt[1]
        f_tpSettle(r, spread[1], tpAl)
    modelBuilt ? f_tpLive(r, spread, tpAl) : na

// Each rung blended as the trace blends: z-space, measured ρ, variance restored.
f_tpBlend(float rz, float bz, float rho) =>
    float out = na
    if not na(rz) and not na(bz)
        float rc = math.max(-0.90, math.min(0.99, nz(rho, 0.71)))
        float mv = wRV * wRV + wBR * wBR + 2.0 * wRV * wBR * rc
        out := (wRV * rz + wBR * bz) * (mv > 1e-9 ? 1.0 / math.sqrt(mv) : 1.0)
    else if not na(rz) and wRV > 0.0
        out := rz
    else if not na(bz) and wBR > 0.0
        out := bz
    out

// ── requests: up to five parents, up to three children ───────────────────────
float tpB1 = na
float tpB2 = na
float tpB3 = na
float tpB4 = na
float tpB5 = na
int   tpT1 = na
int   tpT2 = na
int   tpT3 = na
int   tpT4 = na
int   tpT5 = na
if tpMultiUp
    [ub1, ut1] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, up1, f_tpUpReq(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
    tpB1 := ub1
    tpT1 := ut1
    if up2 != ""
        [ub2, ut2] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, up2, f_tpUpReq(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
        tpB2 := ub2
        tpT2 := ut2
    if up3 != ""
        [ub3, ut3] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, up3, f_tpUpReq(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
        tpB3 := ub3
        tpT3 := ut3
    if up4 != ""
        [ub4, ut4] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, up4, f_tpUpReq(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
        tpB4 := ub4
        tpT4 := ut4
    if up5 != ""
        [ub5, ut5] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, up5, f_tpUpReq(), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
        tpB5 := ub5
        tpT5 := ut5

float tpD1 = na
float tpD2 = na
float tpD3 = na
if tpMultiDn
    tpD1 := f_tpInside(request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, dn1, f_breadth()))
    if dn2 != ""
        tpD2 := f_tpInside(request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, dn2, f_breadth()))
    if dn3 != ""
        tpD3 := f_tpInside(request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, dn3, f_breadth()))

// ── higher rungs: the whole composite at each frame ──────────────────────────
var TpRung tpR1 = TpRung.new(array.new_float(0), array.new_float(5, na), array.new_float(5, 0.0), array.new_float(5, 0.0))
var TpRung tpR2 = TpRung.new(array.new_float(0), array.new_float(5, na), array.new_float(5, 0.0), array.new_float(5, 0.0))
var TpRung tpR3 = TpRung.new(array.new_float(0), array.new_float(5, na), array.new_float(5, 0.0), array.new_float(5, 0.0))
var TpRung tpR4 = TpRung.new(array.new_float(0), array.new_float(5, na), array.new_float(5, 0.0), array.new_float(5, 0.0))
var TpRung tpR5 = TpRung.new(array.new_float(0), array.new_float(5, na), array.new_float(5, 0.0), array.new_float(5, 0.0))
float tpRv1 = f_tpStep(tpR1, tpT1)
float tpRv2 = f_tpStep(tpR2, tpT2)
float tpRv3 = f_tpStep(tpR3, tpT3)
float tpRv4 = f_tpStep(tpR4, tpT4)
float tpRv5 = f_tpStep(tpR5, tpT5)
float tpU1 = f_tpBlend(tpRv1, tpB1, ta.correlation(tpRv1, tpB1, varCorrLen))
float tpU2 = f_tpBlend(tpRv2, tpB2, ta.correlation(tpRv2, tpB2, varCorrLen))
float tpU3 = f_tpBlend(tpRv3, tpB3, ta.correlation(tpRv3, tpB3, varCorrLen))
float tpU4 = f_tpBlend(tpRv4, tpB4, ta.correlation(tpRv4, tpB4, varCorrLen))
float tpU5 = f_tpBlend(tpRv5, tpB5, ta.correlation(tpRv5, tpB5, varCorrLen))

// ── the value bias ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Samanvaya's MTF bias exactly: the ladder mean of z, NOT variance-restored -
// frames that disagree pull it toward zero, so a bright tape means the
// dislocation stands across horizons, not on one. Positive = RICH, negative =
// CHEAP, in Samanvaya's polarity. Only the colours are flipped (see the PANE).
f_tpA(float z) => na(z) ? 0.0 : z
f_tpC(float z) => na(z) ? 0 : 1
float tpSum = 0.0
int   tpCnt = 0
if tpOn and modelBuilt
    tpSum := unified_z + f_tpA(tpU1) + f_tpA(tpU2) + f_tpA(tpU3) + f_tpA(tpU4) + f_tpA(tpU5) +
         f_tpA(tpD1) + f_tpA(tpD2) + f_tpA(tpD3)
    tpCnt := 1 + f_tpC(tpU1) + f_tpC(tpU2) + f_tpC(tpU3) + f_tpC(tpU4) + f_tpC(tpU5) +
         f_tpC(tpD1) + f_tpC(tpD2) + f_tpC(tpD3)
bool  tpReady = tpOn and modelBuilt and tpCnt > 1
float tpBiasZ = tpCnt > 0 ? tpSum / tpCnt : 0.0
float tpBias  = math.max(-100.0, math.min(100.0, f_softBound(tpBiasZ * gain) * 100.0))


// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  5 · THE PRIMARIES AND THE TAPES
//
//  HISTOGRAM   the single-frame engine (Nishchaya v3), bit-identical:
//              conviction minus its signal EMA. Conviction itself is not drawn.
//  TRACE       Samanvaya's unified reading on this chart (unified_osc, 4c).
//  MTF CONV.   every rung's z - the chart's included - averaged in linear space
//              and compressed ONCE, with no second normalization. Each rung is
//              calibrated on its own, so agreement across frames is RARER than
//              any one frame's reading. A rung whose history starts later joins
//              when it can calibrate and moves the level, never the scale.
//  MTF VALUE   Samanvaya's MTF bias, computed in section 4c (tpBias).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_acc(float z) => na(z) ? 0.0 : z
f_cnt(float z) => na(z) ? 0 : 1
float zSum = 0.0
int   zCnt = 0
if multi and sdOK
    zSum := zChart + f_acc(z1) + f_acc(z2) + f_acc(z3) + f_acc(z4) + f_acc(z5) +
         f_acc(y1) + f_acc(y2) + f_acc(y3) + f_acc(y4) + f_acc(y5) + f_acc(y6) + f_acc(y7)
    zCnt := 1 + f_cnt(z1) + f_cnt(z2) + f_cnt(z3) + f_cnt(z4) + f_cnt(z5) +
         f_cnt(y1) + f_cnt(y2) + f_cnt(y3) + f_cnt(y4) + f_cnt(y5) + f_cnt(y6) + f_cnt(y7)
float zLad      = zCnt > 0 ? zSum / zCnt : 0.0
float ladScaled = 100.0 * f_tanh(zLad)
float ladAgree  = inpSm > 1 ? ta.ema(ladScaled, inpSm) : ladScaled

// The conviction tape is ready once the chart rung is calibrated and a second
// rung has held for a full normalization window - the same warm-up the
// histogram itself needs.
var int nLad = 0
if multi and sdOK and zCnt > 1
    nLad += 1
bool  ladReady  = nLad > inpNorm + inpSm

// ── conviction and the histogram: the single-frame engine ───────────────────
float osc  = fast
float sig  = ta.ema(osc, inpSig)
float hist = osc - sig

var int nOsc = 0
if sdOK
    nOsc += 1
bool ready = nOsc > inpNorm + inpSm + inpSig       // histSd's window is clean

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  6 · THE MAGNITUDE GATE
//
//  The histogram must cross ± k·σ of its OWN distribution. ta.crossover(hist, 0)
//  fires whenever two lines touch, which near zero is constantly.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float histSd = ta.stdev(hist, inpNorm)
float thr    = inpK * nz(histSd, 0.0)

bool confirmed = barstate.isconfirmed

bool impUp = ta.crossover (hist,  thr)
bool impDn = ta.crossunder(hist, -thr)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  6b · THE SIGNAL STACK · what every signal reads
//
//  Every signal in the pane - ▲▼ declaration, ◆ continuation, R H divergence - is ONE
//  thesis stated on several layers, and needs all of them on the same closed bar:
//
//      CHART        this timeframe: conviction (histogram, zones, pivots,
//                   impulse gate) and the trace (Samanvaya's value, below)
//      CONVICTION   the +90 tape, C: who controls across its ladder
//      VALUE        the −90 tape, V: rich (+) or cheap (−) across its ladder,
//                   knee at θ (osc_entry)
//
//  No tape is optional and none is a filter laid on afterwards: each family
//  asks each tape the question its thesis depends on, and a signal that cannot
//  ask - a tape warming, off, or with no rungs on this chart - does not fire.
//  The panel says which layer is missing. The thresholds are the tapes' own:
//  conviction's zones for conviction, θ for value. No new number is introduced.
//
//  "Rising two bars running" is spelled out rather than ta.rising(x, 2), which
//  is "above both of the last two values" and is satisfied by one jump - the
//  one-tick noise it is meant to exclude.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
bool  mtfCOk  = multi and ladReady
bool  mtfVOk  = tpReady
bool  stackOk = ready and mtfCOk and mtfVOk
float mC      = ladAgree
float mV      = tpBias
bool  mCUp    = ladAgree > nz(ladAgree[1], ladAgree) and nz(ladAgree[1]) > nz(ladAgree[2], nz(ladAgree[1]))
bool  mCDn    = ladAgree < nz(ladAgree[1], ladAgree) and nz(ladAgree[1]) < nz(ladAgree[2], nz(ladAgree[1]))
float mVLoW   = nz(ta.lowest (tpBias, inpDisloc), tpBias)  // cheapest the ladder got in the dislocation window
float mVHiW   = nz(ta.highest(tpBias, inpDisloc), tpBias)  // richest

// EFFORT → RESULT - the indicator's own thesis, as a signal layer. ABSORBED is
// the share of participation that became displacement sitting in the bottom
// fifth of its own history: heavy effort, little result.
bool  effAbs  = not na(effPct) and effPct <= 20.0
bool  absSeen = nz(ta.highest(effAbs ? 1.0 : 0.0, inpDisloc)) > 0.0

// LAYER OVERLAP - how much of each tape chart conviction already knows. Shown,
// not used: the signals require every layer, and this says how independent
// they are.
float rhoC = ta.correlation(ladAgree, osc, inpNorm)
float rhoV = ta.correlation(tpBias,   osc, inpNorm)

// WHY THE STACK CANNOT JUDGE, when it cannot - drawn on the pane and in the panel.
int    svNeed   = holdOut + 10
string stackWhy = not ready ? "chart warming" :
     not mtfCOk ? (inpTape == "Off" ? "conviction tape off" : multi ? "conviction warming" : "no conviction frames on this chart") :
     not mtfVOk ? (inpVLad == "Off" ? "value tape off" : not tpOn ? "no value frames on this chart" :
                  not modelBuilt ? "value model building · " + str.tostring(math.min(anchN, svNeed)) + "/" + str.tostring(svNeed) + " periods" : "value warming") : ""

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  7 · DIVERGENCE ENGINE
//
//  Found on CONVICTION's pivots - the series the evidence was measured on - not
//  on the trace. The trace's own divergence (Samanvaya's circles) was never
//  measured and is not carried.
//
//  Price is sampled at the true extreme of the swing, not at whatever bar
//  conviction happened to peak on. Measured over GC/SI/CL on four timeframes
//  this raised divergence edge from +0.035R to +0.060R and lifted the number of
//  datasets it helped from 7/12 to 9/12; on a held-out set of eight other
//  instruments it improved 17/32 to 20/32.
//
//  THE CONNECTOR IS ON THE PRICE CHART, joining those two swing extremes at the
//  bars they printed on. The pane end had no line left to join once conviction
//  left the pane - and the price leg is the half of a divergence you can see.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float pvH = ta.pivothigh(osc, inpPL, inpPR)
float pvL = ta.pivotlow (osc, inpPL, inpPR)

// the swing's own extreme, over the same window the pivot was confirmed across,
// and the bar it printed on
float swingHi  = ta.highest(high, inpPL + inpPR + 1)
float swingLo  = ta.lowest (low,  inpPL + inpPR + 1)
int   swingHiX = bar_index + ta.highestbars(high, inpPL + inpPR + 1)
int   swingLoX = bar_index + ta.lowestbars (low,  inpPL + inpPR + 1)

var float lastHV = na
var int   lastHB = na
var float lastHP = na
var int   lastHX = na          // bar of the last swing high's price extreme
var float lastHC = na          // MTF conviction at the last conviction high
var float lastLV = na
var int   lastLB = na
var float lastLP = na
var int   lastLX = na          // bar of the last swing low's price extreme
var float lastLC = na          // MTF conviction at the last conviction low

// Both tapes as they stood AT the pivot, not at the confirmation bar.
float cPiv = ladAgree[inpPR]
float vPiv = tpBias[inpPR]

// THE DIVERGENCE FAMILY - the chart's pivot pattern, confirmed by both tapes.
//   REGULAR  the ladder fails to confirm too (conviction makes the same lower
//            high / higher low as the chart) at a price on the stretched side
//            of fair value (value > 0 at a top, < 0 at a bottom).
//   HIDDEN   the ladder is in control on the continuation side past the inner
//            zone, and value is not stretched past θ against it - the ◆ thesis
//            found at a pivot.
bool rBearT = stackOk and not na(lastHC) and cPiv < lastHC and vPiv > 0.0
bool rBullT = stackOk and not na(lastLC) and cPiv > lastLC and vPiv < 0.0
bool hBearT = stackOk and cPiv <= -inpZ1 and vPiv > -osc_entry
bool hBullT = stackOk and cPiv >=  inpZ1 and vPiv <  osc_entry

bool divBear = false
bool divBull = false
bool hidBear = false
bool hidBull = false

// Capped and recycled explicitly: the profile recreates its level lines on every
// last bar, so an uncapped connector pool would be what Pine evicted under
// pressure — silently, with nothing to say the chart is showing fewer
// divergences than it found.
var array<line> divLn = array.new<line>(0)
f_connect(int x1, float v1, int x2, float v2, color c, int wd) =>
    if inpLine
        array.push(divLn, line.new(x1, v1, x2, v2, xloc.bar_index, extend.none, c, line.style_solid, wd, force_overlay = true))
        if array.size(divLn) > DIV_LINES
            line.delete(array.shift(divLn))

if not na(pvH) and ready and confirmed
    int   b   = bar_index - inpPR
    float p   = swingHi
    int   px  = swingHiX
    int   gap = na(lastHB) ? 0 : b - lastHB
    if not na(lastHV) and gap >= inpGapM and gap <= inpGapX
        if inpDiv and pvH < lastHV and p > lastHP and (not inpGate or lastHV >= inpZ1) and rBearT
            divBear := true
            f_connect(lastHX, lastHP, px, p, color.new(C_BEAR, 10), 2)
        if inpHid and pvH > lastHV and p < lastHP and hBearT
            hidBear := true
            f_connect(lastHX, lastHP, px, p, color.new(C_BEAR, 50), 1)
    lastHV := pvH
    lastHB := b
    lastHP := p
    lastHX := px
    lastHC := cPiv

if not na(pvL) and ready and confirmed
    int   b   = bar_index - inpPR
    float p   = swingLo
    int   px  = swingLoX
    int   gap = na(lastLB) ? 0 : b - lastLB
    if not na(lastLV) and gap >= inpGapM and gap <= inpGapX
        if inpDiv and pvL > lastLV and p < lastLP and (not inpGate or lastLV <= -inpZ1) and rBullT
            divBull := true
            f_connect(lastLX, lastLP, px, p, color.new(C_BULL, 10), 2)
        if inpHid and pvL < lastLV and p > lastLP and hBullT
            hidBull := true
            f_connect(lastLX, lastLP, px, p, color.new(C_BULL, 50), 1)
    lastLV := pvL
    lastLB := b
    lastLP := p
    lastLX := px
    lastLC := cPiv

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  8 · SIGNALS · ▲▼ DECLARATIONS AND ◆ CONTINUATION
//
//  Indicator signals, not trades. A ▲ DECLARES buy and a ▼ declares sell; a
//  declaration stands until the opposite one and has no exit. The ◆ marks a
//  push resuming after a pullback. Nothing here opens, sizes or closes a
//  position. The header's SIGNALS section has every rule and its reasoning.
//
//  UNCHANGED FROM v5, BAR FOR BAR. The only additions below are the two ARM
//  EVENTS given names so the pane can draw them, and the opt-in lean - neither
//  feeds a signal.
//
//  ARM, THEN CONFIRM. The trace's crossing is a one-bar event; the other layers
//  are states that turn a bar or two either side of it. Requiring all of them
//  on the crossing bar lost every ▲▼ whose layers were one bar apart, so the
//  crossing now ARMS a setup for the confirmation window and the ▲▼ fires on
//  the first closed bar inside it where every other layer agrees. A return into
//  the dislocation cancels the arm; the next crossing re-arms it.
//
//  Cooldown per direction: ▲ and long ◆ share one clock, ▼ and short ◆ the
//  other. A triangle takes precedence over a diamond on the same bar.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var int lastLongBar  = na
var int lastShortBar = na
bool coolLong  = na(lastLongBar)  or (bar_index - lastLongBar)  >= inpCool
bool coolShort = na(lastShortBar) or (bar_index - lastShortBar) >= inpCool

// a genuine pullback: the histogram actually crossed to the other side
bool pulledUp = ta.lowest (hist, inpPull) <  0.0
bool pulledDn = ta.highest(hist, inpPull) >  0.0

// ── ARM · the trace: Samanvaya's own trigger and its own gate ────────────────
bool svTradable = basketWarm and enoughData
bool svXLong    = ta.crossover (unified_osc, -osc_entry)
bool svXShort   = ta.crossunder(unified_osc,  osc_entry)

// The arm events - exactly where Samanvaya on its own would have entered, with
// its stationarity gate at its default (off). Drawn as △ ▽.
bool armBuyNow  = confirmed and svTradable and svXLong
bool armSellNow = confirmed and svTradable and svXShort

var int armBuyBar  = na
var int armSellBar = na
if armBuyNow
    armBuyBar  := bar_index
    armSellBar := na
if armSellNow
    armSellBar := bar_index
    armBuyBar  := na
// back inside the dislocation: the turn failed - wait for the next crossing
if confirmed and unified_osc <= -osc_entry
    armBuyBar  := na
if confirmed and unified_osc >=  osc_entry
    armSellBar := na
bool armedBuy  = not na(armBuyBar)  and bar_index - armBuyBar  <= inpConfirm - 1
bool armedSell = not na(armSellBar) and bar_index - armSellBar <= inpConfirm - 1

// ── CONFIRM · the value ladder, this chart's effort, the conviction ladder ────
bool tBuy  = mVLoW <= -osc_entry and mV <  osc_entry and hist > 0.0 and (mC > 0.0 or mCUp) and (not inpEff or absSeen)
bool tSell = mVHiW >=  osc_entry and mV > -osc_entry and hist < 0.0 and (mC < 0.0 or mCDn) and (not inpEff or absSeen)

bool buySig  = inpState and confirmed and stackOk and svTradable and armedBuy  and tBuy  and coolLong
bool sellSig = inpState and confirmed and stackOk and svTradable and armedSell and tSell and coolShort
if buySig
    armBuyBar  := na
if sellSig
    armSellBar := na

// ── ◆ · the push resumes inside conviction, with value room and real result ───
bool tConL = mC >=  inpZ1 and mV <  osc_entry and (not inpEff or not effAbs)
bool tConS = mC <= -inpZ1 and mV > -osc_entry and (not inpEff or not effAbs)

bool conLong  = inpCon and confirmed and stackOk and not buySig  and
     osc > 0.0 and pulledUp and impUp and coolLong and tConL
bool conShort = inpCon and confirmed and stackOk and not sellSig and
     osc < 0.0 and pulledDn and impDn and coolShort and tConS

if buySig or conLong
    lastLongBar := bar_index
if sellSig or conShort
    lastShortBar := bar_index

// ── the standing declaration ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
// +1 after a ▲, −1 after a ▼, standing until the opposite one: a declaration
// about the market at that bar, deliberately without an exit.
var int decl    = 0
var int declBar = na
if buySig
    decl    := 1
    declBar := bar_index
if sellSig
    decl    := -1
    declBar := bar_index

// ── ○ · the lean: conviction's signal line crossing the trace ────────────────
// Display and alerts only; it feeds nothing. Crossing ABOVE the trace is the
// SHORT lean, below the LONG lean - the polarity Samanvaya measured.
bool leanCrossUp = ta.crossover (sig, unified_osc)
bool leanCrossDn = ta.crossunder(sig, unified_osc)
bool leanShort   = confirmed and ready and modelBuilt and leanCrossUp
bool leanLong    = confirmed and ready and modelBuilt and leanCrossDn

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  VOLUME PROFILE · MARKET-STRUCTURE OVERLAY   (ported from PVOI v2.1)
//  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  A PRICE-CHART overlay: session volume profile (histogram + POC/VAH/VAL value
//  area), plus session VWAP, Initial Balance, naked POCs, HVN/LVN nodes,
//  prior-session levels and developing-level trails. Pragati is an oscillator
//  pane, so every element is drawn with force_overlay = true.
//
//  DISPLAY-ONLY AND FULLY DETACHED. It touches NOTHING — not the histogram,
//  the trace, the divergence engine, the triggers, or the signals. Everything
//  is namespaced vp*/C_* so it cannot collide with the oscillator's own state.
//  Removing this whole block leaves the oscillator intact.
//
//  Sorted-key bins, O(1) POC, value-area walk outward from the POC, optional
//  intrabar distribution and inferred buy/sell delta. Session anchor = D intraday,
//  W on daily, M above. request.security_lower_tf history depth is plan-dependent;
//  the profile falls back to a uniform bar-range spread when it is unavailable.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var string G_VP = "9 · Volume profile · price chart"
bool   inpVpMaster    = input.bool(true,  "▸ Volume profile overlay (master)", group=G_VP, tooltip="ON by default. Off removes every chart-overlay element below AND stops the profile being computed — the accumulation loop, the row inserts, the value-area walk and the naked-POC ledger all stand down, so this is a real off switch rather than a curtain over work that keeps running.\n\nThe oscillator pane is unaffected either way; nothing in the engine reads the profile.\n\nONE COST CANNOT BE SWITCHED OFF: Pine requires request.security_lower_tf() at global scope, so the lower-timeframe fetch happens whether or not the profile uses it. Nothing else does.", display=display.none)
bool   inpVpProfile   = input.bool(true,  "Enable profile compute (POC/VAH/VAL)", group=G_VP, tooltip="Builds the session volume profile. Off: histogram, value area, nodes, naked POCs and prior-session levels go dark (VWAP and Initial Balance are independent of it).", display=display.none)
bool   inpVpUseVol    = input.bool(true,  "Volume-weight the profile", group=G_VP, tooltip="Off degrades the profile to a time-at-price (TPO) distribution — for spot FX / synthetic volume.", display=display.none)
float  inpVpBinsPerAtr= input.float(4.0,  "Profile bins per ATR", minval=1.0, maxval=20.0, step=0.5, group=G_VP, tooltip="Bin width = ATR / this. Frozen at session start so keys stay consistent within a profile.", display=display.none)
string inpVpBinMode   = input.string("ATR adaptive", "Profile bin grid", options=["ATR adaptive", "Fixed ticks"], group=G_VP, display=display.none)
int    inpVpBinTicks  = input.int(20,     "    Bin size (ticks) · Fixed mode", minval=1, maxval=5000, group=G_VP, display=display.none)
float  inpVpVaPct     = input.float(0.70, "Value area fraction", minval=0.50, maxval=0.95, step=0.01, group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpIntrabar  = input.bool(true,  "Build profile from intrabar data", group=G_VP, tooltip="request.security_lower_tf() distributes volume by ACTUAL traded price. More accurate, slower, plan-dependent history. Falls back automatically.", display=display.none)
bool   inpVpDelta     = input.bool(true,  "Split profile into inferred buy/sell", group=G_VP, tooltip="Inferred, not measured order flow — display only.", display=display.none)
bool   inpVpHistg     = input.bool(true,  "    Histogram", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
string inpVpStyle     = input.string("Monochrome", "    Histogram style", options=["Monochrome", "Delta split"], group=G_VP, tooltip="MONOCHROME (default): one box per row, amber POC row inside blue value-area rows, grey out-of-value tails. Hue marks identity only, never direction.\n\nDELTA SPLIT: each row divides into green (inferred buy) and red (inferred sell) volume; the POC row is an amber outline.", display=display.none)
bool   vpMono         = inpVpStyle == "Monochrome"
int    inpVpWidth     = input.int(30,     "    Profile width (bars)", minval=8, maxval=150, group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpNodes     = input.bool(true,  "    HVN / LVN markers", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpNaked     = input.bool(true,  "    Naked POC (untouched)", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
int    inpVpNakedMax  = input.int(5,      "        Naked POC · max kept", minval=1, maxval=20, group=G_VP, tooltip="How many untouched POCs the ledger carries. The oldest is dropped when the ledger is full.\n\nThe original ceiling was twelve, and on a long chart twelve amber dotted lines stop being a reference and become the chart. A level you cannot count is not a level. Five is enough to see the unfinished business above and below without burying the price action; raise it only if you are actually trading to the older ones.", display=display.none)
int    inpVpNakedAge  = input.int(250,    "        Naked POC · max age (bars)", minval=50, maxval=1000, step=25, group=G_VP, tooltip="A naked POC older than this is dropped whether or not price ever traded through it, and no line starts further back than this. A point of control from four hundred bars ago describes a market that no longer exists.", display=display.none)
bool   inpVpPrev      = input.bool(true,  "    Prior session value", group=G_VP, tooltip="Last session's pPOC, pVAH and pVAL — three dotted lines, drawn only across the session in front of you. These are the reference the market is currently accepting or rejecting, which is why they are not stacked back through history.", display=display.none)
bool   inpVpVwap      = input.bool(true,  "    Session VWAP", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpIb        = input.bool(true,  "    Initial Balance (IBH/IBL)", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
int    inpVpIbBars    = input.int(12,     "    Initial Balance span (bars)", minval=3, maxval=60, group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpVa        = input.bool(true,  "    Value area (VAH/VAL)", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpPoc       = input.bool(true,  "    POC line", group=G_VP, display=display.none)
bool   inpVpTrail     = input.bool(true,  "    Developing level trails", group=G_VP, display=display.none)

// ── gates, resolved once ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bool   coreView   = inpView == "Core"
bool   vpChartOn  = inpVpMaster and not coreView
// THE MASTER NOW GATES COMPUTE AS WELL AS DRAWING. Before this it only silenced
// the chart objects while the accumulation loop, the row inserts and the value-
// area walk all still ran on every bar. A switch that turns the picture off and
// leaves the work on is not an off switch.
bool   vpCtxOn    = inpVpProfile and vpChartOn
bool   vpIsIntra  = timeframe.isintraday
int    vpTfSec    = timeframe.in_seconds()
// AUTO-TPO FALLBACK. On a symbol reporting no volume every row received zero, so
// pocVol never exceeded zero and the `pocVol > 0` guard suppressed the histogram,
// the POC and the value area entirely - the profile simply did not appear, with
// nothing to explain why, and the panel still read "Volume" because it reported the
// user's INTENT rather than what happened. Index spot and most TVC symbols are
// exactly that case. The share of bars carrying real volume decides it now.
float  vpVolShare = ta.cum((na(volume) or volume == 0.0) ? 0.0 : 1.0) / (bar_index + 1)
bool   vpVolUsable= vpVolShare >= 0.5
bool   vpVolOff   = not inpVpUseVol or not vpVolUsable
bool   vpVolOk    = not vpVolOff          // what ACTUALLY happened, not what was asked
bool   vpTpoAuto  = inpVpUseVol and not vpVolUsable
string vpAnchorTf = vpIsIntra ? "D" : timeframe.isdaily ? "W" : "M"
// The first bar opens a session too. Without it the first partial session had no
// frozen bin width and no session start, so it drew no profile and its Initial
// Balance stretched across the whole of it. The flip is evaluated on its own line
// first so ta.change runs on every bar under v6's lazy `or`.
bool   vpSessFlip = ta.change(time(vpAnchorTf)) != 0
bool   vpNewSess  = barstate.isfirst or vpSessFlip
bool   vpHistOn   = vpChartOn and inpVpHistg and vpCtxOn
bool   vpVaOn     = vpChartOn and inpVpVa    and vpCtxOn
bool   vpPocOn    = vpChartOn and inpVpPoc   and vpCtxOn
bool   vpNodesOn  = vpHistOn  and inpVpNodes
bool   vpNakedOn  = vpChartOn and inpVpNaked and vpCtxOn
bool   vpPrevOn   = vpChartOn and inpVpPrev  and vpCtxOn
bool   vpTrailOn  = vpChartOn and inpVpTrail and vpCtxOn
bool   vpVwapOn   = vpChartOn and inpVpVwap
bool   vpIbOn     = vpChartOn and inpVpIb

// ATR floor. Floored to mintick so the session-start bin freeze never freezes to
// na and blanks the first session. ta.* must be evaluated on EVERY bar, never
// inside the session branch that consumes it.
float vpATR = math.max(nz(ta.atr(20), syminfo.mintick), syminfo.mintick)

// ── helpers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
f_vpLocate(array<int> keys, int k) =>
    int  lo    = 0
    int  hi    = array.size(keys) - 1
    int  pos   = 0
    bool found = false
    while lo <= hi
        int mid = (lo + hi) / 2
        int mk  = array.get(keys, mid)
        if mk == k
            pos   := mid
            found := true
            break
        else if mk < k
            lo := mid + 1
        else
            hi := mid - 1
    if not found
        pos := lo
    [pos, found]

f_vpAdd(array<int> keys, array<float> vols, array<float> buys, array<float> sells, int k, float v, float bv, float sv) =>
    [pos, found] = f_vpLocate(keys, k)
    float nv = v
    if found
        nv := array.get(vols, pos) + v
        array.set(vols,  pos, nv)
        array.set(buys,  pos, array.get(buys,  pos) + bv)
        array.set(sells, pos, array.get(sells, pos) + sv)
    else
        array.insert(keys,  pos, k)
        array.insert(vols,  pos, v)
        array.insert(buys,  pos, bv)
        array.insert(sells, pos, sv)
    nv

// ── profile state ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
var array<int>   vpKey  = array.new<int>()
var array<float> vpVol  = array.new<float>()
var array<float> vpBuy  = array.new<float>()
var array<float> vpSell = array.new<float>()
var float vpBin   = na
var float vpTotal = 0.0
var int   pocKey  = na
var float pocVol  = 0.0
var float poc     = na
var float vah     = na
var float vaLow   = na
var float prevPoc = na
var float prevVah = na
var float prevVal = na
var bool  vpIntraOk = false

// intrabar distribution (falls back automatically when unavailable)
// On a chart at or below one minute there is no lower timeframe worth spreading
// by, and on a SECONDS chart "1" is HIGHER than the chart - a runtime error. The
// request then asks for the chart's own timeframe (harmless) and intrabar
// distribution stands down.
string vpLtf      = vpTfSec >= 3600 ? "5" : vpTfSec > 60 ? "1" : timeframe.period
bool   vpIntraUse = inpVpIntrabar and vpTfSec > 60
[vpIbHi, vpIbLo, vpIbVol, vpIbClose] = request.security_lower_tf(syminfo.tickerid, vpLtf, [high, low, volume, close])

// inferred delta (DISPLAY ONLY) — gap-aware close-location share to buyers
float vpTrHi  = math.max(high, nz(close[1], high))
float vpTrLo  = math.min(low,  nz(close[1], low))
float vpTrRng = math.max(vpTrHi - vpTrLo, syminfo.mintick)
float vpShare = inpVpDelta ? math.min(1.0, math.max(0.0, (close - vpTrLo) / vpTrRng)) : 0.5
float vpBarV  = vpVolOff ? 1.0 : math.max(nz(volume), 0.0)
float vpBarB  = vpBarV * vpShare
float vpBarS  = vpBarV - vpBarB

if vpCtxOn
    if vpNewSess
        prevPoc := poc
        prevVah := vah
        prevVal := vaLow
        array.clear(vpKey)
        array.clear(vpVol)
        array.clear(vpBuy)
        array.clear(vpSell)
        vpTotal := 0.0
        pocKey  := na
        pocVol  := 0.0
        vpBin   := inpVpBinMode == "Fixed ticks" ? math.max(syminfo.mintick, inpVpBinTicks * syminfo.mintick) : math.max(syminfo.mintick, vpATR / inpVpBinsPerAtr)

    if not na(vpBin) and vpBin > 0
        bool usedIntrabar = false
        if vpIntraUse and not na(vpIbHi) and not na(vpIbLo) and not na(vpIbVol) and not na(vpIbClose)
            int m = array.size(vpIbHi)
            if m > 0 and m == array.size(vpIbLo) and m == array.size(vpIbVol) and m == array.size(vpIbClose)
                usedIntrabar := true
                float prevC = nz(close[1], na)
                for j = 0 to m - 1
                    float ih = array.get(vpIbHi, j)
                    float il = array.get(vpIbLo,  j)
                    // TPO: each CHART bar is one unit of time, split across its
                    // intrabars - otherwise an intrabar-built bar counted m times
                    // what a fallback bar counted in the same session.
                    float iv = vpVolOff ? 1.0 / m : math.max(nz(array.get(vpIbVol, j)), 0.0)
                    float ic = array.get(vpIbClose, j)
                    float bvn = iv * 0.5
                    float svn = iv * 0.5
                    if inpVpDelta and not na(ic) and not na(prevC)
                        if ic > prevC
                            bvn := iv
                            svn := 0.0
                        else if ic < prevC
                            bvn := 0.0
                            svn := iv
                    if not na(ic)
                        prevC := ic
                    if not na(ih) and not na(il)
                        int kl = int(math.floor(il / vpBin))
                        int kh = int(math.floor(ih / vpBin))
                        int nb = math.max(1, math.min(kh - kl + 1, 200))
                        float per  = iv  / nb
                        float perB = bvn / nb
                        float perS = svn / nb
                        for k = kl to kl + nb - 1
                            float tot = f_vpAdd(vpKey, vpVol, vpBuy, vpSell, k, per, perB, perS)
                            vpTotal += per
                            if tot > pocVol
                                pocVol := tot
                                pocKey := k
        if not usedIntrabar
            int kl = int(math.floor(low  / vpBin))
            int kh = int(math.floor(high / vpBin))
            int nb = math.max(1, math.min(kh - kl + 1, 200))
            float per  = vpBarV / nb
            float perB = vpBarB / nb
            float perS = vpBarS / nb
            for k = kl to kl + nb - 1
                float tot = f_vpAdd(vpKey, vpVol, vpBuy, vpSell, k, per, perB, perS)
                vpTotal += per
                if tot > pocVol
                    pocVol := tot
                    pocKey := k

        vpIntraOk := usedIntrabar

    int nLev = array.size(vpKey)
    if nLev > 0 and not na(pocKey) and not na(vpBin)
        [pi, pfound] = f_vpLocate(vpKey, pocKey)
        if pfound
            float target = vpTotal * inpVpVaPct
            int   lo    = pi
            int   hi    = pi
            float acc   = array.get(vpVol, pi)
            int   guard = 0
            while acc < target and (lo > 0 or hi < nLev - 1) and guard < nLev + 2
                guard += 1
                float vd = lo > 0        ? array.get(vpVol, lo - 1) : -1.0
                float vu = hi < nLev - 1 ? array.get(vpVol, hi + 1) : -1.0
                if vu >= vd
                    hi  += 1
                    acc += vu
                else
                    lo  -= 1
                    acc += vd
            poc   := (pocKey + 0.5) * vpBin
            vah   := (array.get(vpKey, hi) + 1.0) * vpBin
            vaLow := array.get(vpKey, lo) * vpBin

// ── naked POC ledger (measured) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Capped by input rather than at a hard twelve: twelve amber dotted lines on a
//  long chart stop being a reference and become the chart.
var array<float> vpNpY = array.new<float>()
var array<int>   vpNpX = array.new<int>()
if barstate.isconfirmed and array.size(vpNpY) > 0
    int kn = array.size(vpNpY) - 1
    while kn >= 0
        if high >= array.get(vpNpY, kn) and low <= array.get(vpNpY, kn)
            array.remove(vpNpY, kn)
            array.remove(vpNpX, kn)
        kn -= 1
if vpCtxOn and vpNewSess and not na(prevPoc)
    bool dupN = false
    if array.size(vpNpY) > 0
        for qn = 0 to array.size(vpNpY) - 1
            if math.abs(array.get(vpNpY, qn) - prevPoc) <= vpATR * 0.1
                dupN := true
                break
    if not dupN
        array.push(vpNpY, prevPoc)
        array.push(vpNpX, bar_index)
        while array.size(vpNpY) > inpVpNakedMax
            array.shift(vpNpY)
            array.shift(vpNpX)
while array.size(vpNpX) > 0
    if bar_index - array.get(vpNpX, 0) > inpVpNakedAge
        array.shift(vpNpY)
        array.shift(vpNpX)
    else
        break

// ── session VWAP + Initial Balance (measured) ─────────────────────────────────
var float vpSessPv = 0.0
var float vpSessVv = 0.0
var float vpIbh    = na
var float vpIbl    = na
var int   vpSessStart = na
if vpChartOn
    if vpNewSess
        vpSessPv    := 0.0
        vpSessVv    := 0.0
        vpIbh       := high
        vpIbl       := low
        vpSessStart := bar_index
    float wV = vpVolOff ? 1.0 : math.max(nz(volume), 1e-10)
    vpSessPv += hlc3 * wV
    vpSessVv += wV
    if bar_index - nz(vpSessStart, bar_index) < inpVpIbBars       // bars 0 … n-1 = n bars
        vpIbh := math.max(nz(vpIbh, high), high)
        vpIbl := math.min(nz(vpIbl, low), low)
float vpVwap = vpSessVv > 0 ? vpSessPv / vpSessVv : na

// ── right-edge tag column + collision control ─────────────────────────────────
int VP_GUTTER = 4
int VP_XBACK  = 5000          // furthest-back x any profile drawing may take
int VP_OFFSET = 3             // extra bars of air between the last candle and the profile
int vpLeft    = bar_index + 3 + VP_OFFSET
int vpRight   = vpLeft + inpVpWidth
var int tagX  = 0
var array<float> tagRows = array.new<float>()

f_rowFree(float y) =>
    float minGap = vpATR * 0.30
    bool  free   = true
    if array.size(tagRows) > 0
        for yy in tagRows
            if math.abs(yy - y) < minGap
                free := false
                break
    free

f_vpTag(label lb, float y, string txt, color col, bool on) =>
    label.delete(lb)
    bool draw = on and not na(y) and f_rowFree(y)
    if draw
        array.push(tagRows, y)
    draw ? label.new(tagX, y, txt + "  " + str.tostring(y, format.mintick),
              xloc.bar_index, yloc.price, color.new(col, 88), label.style_label_left,
              col, size.tiny, force_overlay = true) : label(na)

// ── developing-level trails + VWAP curve (force_overlay) ──────────────────────
// Blank on the session-open bar so yesterday's finals never connect to today.
// force_overlay relocates these to the MAIN chart pane, so they render as price-
// level curves and never touch the oscillator's ±100 autoscale.
plot(vpTrailOn and vpVaOn  and not vpNewSess ? vah   : na, "VP · VAH trail", color.new(C_VA, 75), 1, plot.style_linebr, force_overlay = true, display = display.pane + display.price_scale)
plot(vpTrailOn and vpVaOn  and not vpNewSess ? vaLow : na, "VP · VAL trail", color.new(C_VA, 75), 1, plot.style_linebr, force_overlay = true, display = display.pane + display.price_scale)
plot(vpTrailOn and vpPocOn and not vpNewSess ? poc   : na, "VP · POC trail", color.new(C_POC, 65), 1, plot.style_linebr, force_overlay = true, display = display.pane + display.price_scale)
plot(vpVwapOn ? vpVwap : na, "VP · Session VWAP", color.new(C_VWAP, 0), 1, plot.style_linebr, force_overlay = true, display = display.pane + display.price_scale)

// ── histogram (render aggregation into ≤110 rows) ─────────────────────────────
var array<box> vpBoxes = array.new<box>()
if barstate.islast
    for b in vpBoxes
        box.delete(b)
    array.clear(vpBoxes)
    tagX := (vpHistOn ? vpRight : bar_index) + VP_GUTTER
    if vpHistOn and not na(vpBin) and vpBin > 0 and pocVol > 0
        int maxRight = vpLeft
        int nB = array.size(vpKey)
        if nB > 0
            int ROWS_MAX = 110
            int step = math.max(1, int(math.ceil(nB / float(ROWS_MAX))))
            int nR   = int(math.ceil(nB / float(step)))
            array<float> rV  = array.new<float>(nR, 0.0)
            array<float> rB  = array.new<float>(nR, 0.0)
            array<float> rS  = array.new<float>(nR, 0.0)
            array<float> rLo = array.new<float>(nR, 0.0)
            array<float> rHi = array.new<float>(nR, 0.0)
            array<bool>  rP  = array.new<bool>(nR, false)
            [pIdx, pFound] = f_vpLocate(vpKey, pocKey)
            for i = 0 to nB - 1
                int rr = int(math.floor(i / float(step)))
                array.set(rV, rr, array.get(rV, rr) + array.get(vpVol, i))
                array.set(rB, rr, array.get(rB, rr) + array.get(vpBuy, i))
                array.set(rS, rr, array.get(rS, rr) + array.get(vpSell, i))
                if i % step == 0
                    array.set(rLo, rr, array.get(vpKey, i) * vpBin)
                if i % step == step - 1 or i == nB - 1
                    array.set(rHi, rr, (array.get(vpKey, i) + 1.0) * vpBin)
                if pFound and i == pIdx
                    array.set(rP, rr, true)
            // Row width is normalised by the WIDEST RENDERED ROW, not by the raw
            // POC bin. Above ROWS_MAX bins the rows are aggregated in groups of
            // `step`, so an aggregated row holds several bins' worth and can
            // exceed pocVol — which made the histogram overshoot its declared
            // width by up to `step` times and drag the tag column off the screen
            // with it. Any session finer than 110 bins hit this.
            float rMax   = 0.0
            float hvMean = 0.0
            int   hvCnt  = 0
            for r0 = 0 to nR - 1
                float tv0 = array.get(rV, r0)
                rMax := math.max(rMax, tv0)
                if tv0 > 0
                    hvMean += tv0
                    hvCnt  += 1
            hvMean := hvCnt > 0 ? hvMean / hvCnt : 0.0
            float hvVar = 0.0
            for r0 = 0 to nR - 1
                float tv0 = array.get(rV, r0)
                if tv0 > 0
                    hvVar += math.pow(tv0 - hvMean, 2)
            float hvSd  = hvCnt > 0 ? math.sqrt(hvVar / hvCnt) : 0.0
            float hvnTh = hvMean + 1.2 * hvSd
            float lvnTh = hvMean - 0.6 * hvSd
            array<float> srtV = array.new<float>()
            for r0 = 0 to nR - 1
                if array.get(rV, r0) > 0
                    array.push(srtV, array.get(rV, r0))
            if array.size(srtV) > 10
                array.sort(srtV, order.ascending)
                lvnTh := array.get(srtV, math.max(0, int(math.floor(array.size(srtV) * 0.10))))
            for r = 0 to nR - 1
                if array.size(vpBoxes) >= 470
                    break
                float tV = array.get(rV, r)
                int wTot = tV > 0 and rMax > 0 ? int(math.round(inpVpWidth * tV / rMax)) : 0
                if wTot > 0
                    float yB    = array.get(rLo, r)
                    float ySpan = array.get(rHi, r) - yB
                    float yT    = yB + ySpan * 0.85
                    float pc    = yB + ySpan / 2.0
                    bool  isPoc = array.get(rP, r)
                    maxRight := math.max(maxRight, vpLeft + wTot)
                    bool inVa = not na(vah) and not na(vaLow) and pc >= vaLow and pc <= vah
                    int  fade = inVa ? 48 : 72
                    if vpMono
                        // MONOCHROME: one box per row, hue = identity never
                        // direction — amber POC row, blue value-area rows, grey
                        // out-of-value tails. POC row is a denser amber FILL.
                        color rowCol = isPoc ? C_POC : inVa ? C_VA : C_NODE
                        int   rowFd  = isPoc ? 25 : fade
                        array.push(vpBoxes, box.new(vpLeft, yT, vpLeft + wTot, yB, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = na, bgcolor = color.new(rowCol, rowFd), force_overlay = true))
                    else
                        // DELTA SPLIT: green inferred-buy / red inferred-sell, with
                        // the POC row marked by an amber OUTLINE so the split keeps
                        // its colour.
                        float bV = array.get(rB, r)
                        int   wB = tV > 0 ? int(math.round(wTot * bV / tV)) : 0
                        if wB > 0
                            array.push(vpBoxes, box.new(vpLeft, yT, vpLeft + wB, yB, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = na, bgcolor = color.new(C_BULL, fade), force_overlay = true))
                        if wTot - wB > 0
                            array.push(vpBoxes, box.new(vpLeft + wB, yT, vpLeft + wTot, yB, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = na, bgcolor = color.new(C_BEAR, fade), force_overlay = true))
                        if isPoc
                            array.push(vpBoxes, box.new(vpLeft, yT, vpLeft + wTot, yB, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = color.new(C_POC, 0), border_width = 1, bgcolor = na, force_overlay = true))
                    if vpNodesOn and r > 0 and r < nR - 1 and not isPoc and tV > 0
                        float vL = array.get(rV, r - 1)
                        float vR = array.get(rV, r + 1)
                        bool  peak   = tV > vL and tV > vR
                        bool  trough = tV < vL and tV < vR
                        // Node marks sit just LEFT of the histogram (vpLeft-2..vpLeft-1),
                        // so they travel with the profile's offset and keep a one-bar gap.
                        // HVN dense violet / LVN pale violet — disjoint from BOTH the
                        // monochrome grey tails and the delta-split green/red, so the
                        // colour reads in either style. Form still separates them:
                        // full-height block = node, centred tick = vacuum.
                        if tV >= hvnTh and peak
                            array.push(vpBoxes, box.new(vpLeft - 2, yT, vpLeft - 1, yB, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = na, bgcolor = color.new(C_HVN, 40), force_overlay = true))
                        else if tV <= lvnTh and trough
                            array.push(vpBoxes, box.new(vpLeft - 2, pc - vpBin * 0.08, vpLeft - 1, pc + vpBin * 0.08, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = na, bgcolor = color.new(C_LVN, 40), force_overlay = true))
            if maxRight > vpLeft
                tagX := maxRight + VP_GUTTER

// ── right-edge tags for the live levels ───────────────────────────────────────
var label vpLPoc  = na
var label vpLVah  = na
var label vpLVal  = na
var label vpLVwap = na
var label vpLIbh  = na
var label vpLIbl  = na
if barstate.islast
    array.clear(tagRows)
    vpLPoc  := f_vpTag(vpLPoc,  poc,    "POC",  C_POC,    vpPocOn)
    vpLVah  := f_vpTag(vpLVah,  vah,    "VAH",  C_VA,     vpVaOn)
    vpLVal  := f_vpTag(vpLVal,  vaLow,  "VAL",  C_VA,     vpVaOn)
    vpLVwap := f_vpTag(vpLVwap, vpVwap, "VWAP", C_VWAP,   vpVwapOn)
    vpLIbh  := f_vpTag(vpLIbh,  vpIbh,  "IBH",  C_VIOLET, vpIbOn)
    vpLIbl  := f_vpTag(vpLIbl,  vpIbl,  "IBL",  C_VIOLET, vpIbOn)

// ── managed level lines · live tier, IB, prior session, naked POCs ────────────
var line  vpLnPoc  = na
var line  vpLnVah  = na
var line  vpLnVal  = na
var box   vpVaBand = na
var line  vpLnIbh  = na
var line  vpLnIbl  = na
var line  vpPlnPoc = na
var line  vpPlnVah = na
var line  vpPlnVal = na
var label vpLPp    = na
var label vpLPvh   = na
var label vpLPvl   = na
var array<line> vpNpLines = array.new<line>()
if barstate.islast
    line.delete(vpLnPoc)
    line.delete(vpLnVah)
    line.delete(vpLnVal)
    box.delete(vpVaBand)
    line.delete(vpLnIbh)
    line.delete(vpLnIbl)
    line.delete(vpPlnPoc)
    line.delete(vpPlnVah)
    line.delete(vpPlnVal)
    label.delete(vpLPp)
    label.delete(vpLPvh)
    label.delete(vpLPvl)
    for lnN in vpNpLines
        line.delete(lnN)
    array.clear(vpNpLines)
    // Session-anchored. Pine refuses drawings placed more than 10,000 bars into
    // history; every x in this block is bounded by VP_XBACK well inside that. The
    // old bound of 400 cited max_bars_back, which does not govern drawings, and
    // cut session lines short on 24-hour 1-minute charts (1,440 bars a session).
    int xSess = math.max(math.max(not na(vpSessStart) ? vpSessStart : 0, bar_index - VP_XBACK), 0)
    if vpPocOn and not na(poc)
        vpLnPoc := line.new(xSess, poc, tagX, poc, color = color.new(C_POC, 0), width = 2, force_overlay = true)
    if vpVaOn and not na(vah) and not na(vaLow)
        vpVaBand := box.new(xSess, vah, bar_index, vaLow, border_color = na, bgcolor = color.new(C_VA, 93), force_overlay = true)
        vpLnVah  := line.new(xSess, vah,   tagX, vah,   color = color.new(C_VA, 20), width = 1, force_overlay = true)
        vpLnVal  := line.new(xSess, vaLow, tagX, vaLow, color = color.new(C_VA, 20), width = 1, force_overlay = true)
    if vpIbOn and not na(vpIbh)
        vpLnIbh := line.new(xSess, vpIbh, tagX, vpIbh, color = color.new(C_VIOLET, 20), width = 1, style = line.style_dashed, force_overlay = true)
        vpLnIbl := line.new(xSess, vpIbl, tagX, vpIbl, color = color.new(C_VIOLET, 20), width = 1, style = line.style_dashed, force_overlay = true)
    if vpPrevOn and not na(prevPoc)
        int xA = math.max(0, bar_index - 30)
        vpPlnPoc := line.new(xA, prevPoc, tagX, prevPoc, color = color.new(C_POC, 55), width = 1, style = line.style_dotted, force_overlay = true)
        vpPlnVah := line.new(xA, prevVah, tagX, prevVah, color = color.new(C_VA, 55),  width = 1, style = line.style_dotted, force_overlay = true)
        vpPlnVal := line.new(xA, prevVal, tagX, prevVal, color = color.new(C_VA, 55),  width = 1, style = line.style_dotted, force_overlay = true)
        vpLPp  := f_vpTag(vpLPp,  prevPoc, "pPOC", C_POC, vpPrevOn)
        vpLPvh := f_vpTag(vpLPvh, prevVah, "pVAH", C_VA,  vpPrevOn)
        vpLPvl := f_vpTag(vpLPvl, prevVal, "pVAL", C_VA,  vpPrevOn)
    if vpNakedOn and array.size(vpNpY) > 0
        int xMinN = math.max(0, bar_index - math.min(inpVpNakedAge, VP_XBACK))
        int nN    = array.size(vpNpY)
        for i = 0 to nN - 1
            float ny = array.get(vpNpY, i)
            int   x1 = math.max(xMinN, array.get(vpNpX, i))
            array.push(vpNpLines, line.new(x1, ny, bar_index, ny, color = color.new(C_POC, 30), width = 1, style = line.style_dotted, force_overlay = true))

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  9 · PRESENTATION STATE
//
//  One channel, one question. The histogram carries the direction of the push
//  (hue), its phase (lightness) and whether it is real or an amplified quiet
//  regime (saturation). The trace carries where price stands (hue), how far
//  from fair (intensity) and whether the value reading is coherent
//  (saturation). The tapes carry the same two questions across the ladder;
//  the markers carry verdicts; the background carries the standing
//  declaration. Where two layers are one thesis - the value tape and the
//  trace - they share the grading.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_mix(color a, color b, float t) =>
    color.rgb(color.r(a) + (color.r(b) - color.r(a)) * t,
              color.g(a) + (color.g(b) - color.g(a)) * t,
              color.b(a) + (color.b(b) - color.b(a)) * t, color.t(a))

// Conviction's velocity, and the noise floor under it: a move smaller than a
// tenth of its own bar-to-bar σ counts as flat.
float dOsc    = osc - nz(osc[1], osc)
float dOscSd  = ta.stdev(dOsc, inpNorm)
float slopeDb = 0.10 * nz(dOscSd, 0.0)

// Histogram phase: which side of zero, growing or shrinking, and whether
// conviction is still moving with it.
bool  hPrev    = not na(hist[1])
float hMag     = math.abs(hist)
bool  hAbove   = hist >= 0.0
bool  hExpand  = hPrev and hMag > math.abs(hist[1])
bool  trWith   = hAbove ? dOsc > slopeDb : dOsc < -slopeDb
bool  useSlope = inpHLogic == "Expansion + slope"
bool  hImp     = hMag >= thr
bool  hHi      = ready and hImp

// QUIET REGIME. The raw share's σ in the bottom fifth of its own long history:
// the tanh scaling is amplifying a small imbalance into a large reading.
int   Q_LEN    = inpNorm * 4
float rawSdPr  = ta.percentrank(nz(rawSd, 0.0), Q_LEN)
bool  quietReg = inpQuiet and sdOK and not na(rawSdPr) and rawSdPr <= 20.0

// The standing declaration, shown when asked. No tint while the stack is paused.
bool  tintLong  = inpStateBg and stackOk and decl ==  1
bool  tintShort = inpStateBg and stackOk and decl == -1

// Every conviction-triggered marker drawn while the regime is quiet is drawn
// faded, as the columns are. Value events (the arm marks) are not.
f_q(color c, int t) => color.new(c, quietReg ? math.min(100, t + 45) : t)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  INFORMATION PANEL
//
//  Headline: where price stands · what the push is doing - the pane's two
//  questions in one line, coloured by the value side. Then the signal stack,
//  the value model behind the trace, and conviction's own detail. Panel text
//  is C_SOFT: C_INK is glyph ink on filled labels, the panel's own background.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
f_num(float v, string fmt) => na(v) ? "—" : str.tostring(v, fmt)

f_row(table t, int rw, string k, string v, color col, string tip) =>
    table.cell(t, 0, rw, k, text_color = C_SOFT, text_halign = text.align_left,  text_size = size.small, tooltip = tip)
    table.cell(t, 1, rw, v, text_color = col,    text_halign = text.align_right, text_size = size.small, tooltip = tip)

f_sep(table t, int rw, string k) =>
    table.cell(t, 0, rw, k, text_color = C_NEUT, text_halign = text.align_left, text_size = size.tiny)
    table.cell(t, 1, rw, "", text_size = size.tiny)

bool tblFull = inpTbl == "Detailed"
bool pfRows  = tblFull and vpCtxOn
var table tb = na
if inpPanel and barstate.islast
    if na(tb)
        tb := table.new(position.middle_right, 2, tblFull ? (pfRows ? 24 : 21) : 7, bgcolor = C_PANEL,
             frame_color = C_GRID, frame_width = 1, border_width = 0)

    // ── 0 · headline: value word · push word ──────────────────────────────────
    string valTxt = not modelBuilt ? "VALUE MODEL BUILDING" :
         unified_osc >=  osc_entry ? "RICH past θ" :
         unified_osc >   0.0       ? "rich" :
         unified_osc <= -osc_entry ? "CHEAP past θ" :
         unified_osc <   0.0       ? "cheap" : "fair"
    string pushTxt = (hAbove ? "push ↑ " : "push ↓ ") +
         (not useSlope ? (hExpand ? "pressing" : "fading") :
          hExpand ? (hImp ? "impulse" : "pressing") : trWith ? "decelerating" : "turning")
    string head = not ready ? "WARMING UP" : not modelBuilt ? valTxt : valTxt + " · " + pushTxt
    color  hc   = not ready or not modelBuilt ? C_GOLD : unified_osc >= 0.0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR
    table.cell(tb, 0, 0, head, text_color = hc, text_halign = text.align_left, text_size = size.small,
         tooltip = "WHERE PRICE STANDS · WHAT THE PUSH IS DOING - the pane's two questions in one line.\n\nVALUE is the trace: rich or cheap against fair value on this chart, and whether it is past θ. PUSH is the histogram: ↑ when conviction is above its signal line, then its phase - impulse (past the ±k·σ gate), pressing, decelerating, turning.\n\nColoured by the VALUE side: green rich, red cheap. Same hue as the columns = the push is carrying price further from fair; split hue = it is working price back.\n\nGold while the engine warms up or the value model is still building.")
    table.cell(tb, 1, 0, modelBuilt ? f_num(unified_osc, "+#;-#") : "", text_color = hc, text_halign = text.align_right, text_size = size.small)

    // ── SIGNALS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    f_sep(tb, 1, "SIGNALS")
    string dTxt = not stackOk ? "paused · " + stackWhy :
         armedBuy   ? "△ armed · " + str.tostring(bar_index - armBuyBar + 1) + "/" + str.tostring(inpConfirm) :
         armedSell  ? "▽ armed · " + str.tostring(bar_index - armSellBar + 1) + "/" + str.tostring(inpConfirm) :
         decl ==  1 ? "▲ BUY · " + str.tostring(bar_index - declBar) + " bars" :
         decl == -1 ? "▼ SELL · " + str.tostring(bar_index - declBar) + " bars" : "none yet"
    color dCol = not stackOk or armedBuy or armedSell ? C_GOLD : decl == 1 ? C_BULL : decl == -1 ? C_BEAR : C_SOFT
    f_row(tb, 2, " declaration", dTxt, dCol,
         "The standing ▲▼ declaration and how many bars it has stood - a statement about the market at that bar, with no exit.\n\nARMED (gold) means the trace has crossed back through θ - the hollow △▽ on the rail - and the other layers have n of the confirmation window left to agree. Gold because an arm is a setup without evidence yet.\n\nPAUSED names the layer that cannot judge; nothing pauses silently.")

    string vcTxt = not modelBuilt ? "building · " + str.tostring(math.min(anchN, svNeed)) + "/" + str.tostring(svNeed) :
         f_num(unified_osc, "+#;-#") + " vs ±" + f_num(osc_entry, "#") + (svTradable ? "" : " · basket settling")
    color vcCol = not modelBuilt or not svTradable ? C_GOLD :
         unified_osc >= osc_entry ? C_BULL : unified_osc <= -osc_entry ? C_BEAR : C_SOFT
    f_row(tb, 3, " value · chart", vcTxt, vcCol,
         "The TRACE: Samanvaya's unified value reading on this chart, against θ. Past θ it is coloured by side - green rich, red cheap; inside θ it is neutral ink.\n\nBASKET SETTLING (gold): the macro basket rotated in the last 21 bars (the ⟳ on the zero line). The trace is a different series after a rotation, and no ▲▼ can arm until it settles.")

    int    agN    = agreeRich >= agreeCheap ? agreeRich : agreeCheap
    string agSide = agN == 0 ? "past θ" : agreeRich >= agreeCheap ? "rich" : "cheap"
    string lgTxt  = not modelBuilt ? "—" :
         "RV " + f_num(rv_z, "+0.0;-0.0") + " · br " + f_num(breadth_z, "+0.0;-0.0") + " · " +
         str.tostring(agN) + "/5 " + agSide + (legsSplit ? " · split" : "")
    f_row(tb, 4, " value · legs", lgTxt, legsSplit ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
         "The two halves of the trace, in z:\n  RV  the macro-hedged relative-value spread, scored over five timescales\n  br  breadth - seven price-only views of the chart's own momentum and flow\n\nn/5 is how many of the RV leg's five timescales independently sit past θ, and on which side. Five of five and three of five can share an average and mean very different things.\n\nSPLIT (gold): the legs point opposite ways, each by more than half a σ. The legs correlate about 0.7, so a split is uncommon and informative - the trace dims toward grey while it lasts.")

    f_row(tb, 5, " MTF · conv · value",
         (mtfCOk ? f_num(ladAgree, "+#;-#") : "—") + " · " + (mtfVOk ? f_num(tpBias, "+#;-#") : "—"), C_SOFT,
         "The two tapes as numbers: MTF conviction (+90 tape) and MTF value (−90 tape).\n\nConviction: who controls across its ladder - with Ladder down (default) FASTER than the chart's own conviction, the inside of each bar. Value: rich (+) or cheap (−) across its ladder - with Ladder up (default) SLOWER, the frames that contain the chart.\n\n— while a tape warms, is off, or has no frames here.")

    string impWord = not ready ? "warming" : not useSlope ? (hExpand ? "pressing" : "fading") :
         hExpand ? (hImp ? "IMPULSE" : "pressing") : trWith ? "decelerating" : "turning"
    f_row(tb, 6, " impulse",
         f_num(hist, "+0.0;-0.0") + " vs ±" + f_num(thr, "0.0") + " · " + impWord,
         hHi ? (hAbove ? C_BULL : C_BEAR) : C_SOFT,
         "The native histogram - conviction minus its signal line - against the ± k·σ impulse gate the ◆ must cross, and its phase. IMPULSE is past the gate; the same column is drawn near-solid.\n\nNative units. The pane draws the columns σ-pinned so a 3σ column reaches θ; this row is the engine's own number.")

    if tblFull
        // ── VALUE · the model behind the trace ────────────────────────────────
        f_sep(tb, 7, "VALUE · " + (anchorTF == "W" ? "weekly" : "daily") + " clock")

        string hsTxt = not modelBuilt ? "building · " + str.tostring(math.min(anchN, svNeed)) + "/" + str.tostring(svNeed) :
             hedgeMode == "Off"  ? "off · price only" :
             hedgeMode == "Full" ? "100% · forced" :
             f_num(hedgeW * 100.0, "#") + "% · " + (hedgeW < 0.10 ? "idle" : hedgeW < 0.35 ? "partial" : "engaged")
        f_row(tb, 8, " hedge strength", hsTxt, not modelBuilt or hedgeW < 0.10 ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
             "How much of the macro hedge the RV leg applies - its measured out-of-sample R² over the last 104 anchor periods, clipped to 0…1 (Auto), or forced.\n\nIDLE (gold, under 10%): the hedge has not been earning its keep, so the RV leg is close to the chart's own cumulative return - the trace is then mostly a price-only reading, and value restates momentum more than it adds to it.\n\nPARTIAL under 35%, ENGAGED above.")

        string o1 = "(marginal)"
        string o2 = "(partial, given ①)"
        string o3 = "(partial, given ① and ②)"
        for k = 0 to 2
            int    ix   = k == 0 ? idx1 : k == 1 ? idx2 : idx3
            float  rho  = array.get(S1, ix)
            float  wt   = k == 0 ? w1 : k == 1 ? w2 : w3
            bool   live = kEff >= k + 1
            float  qq   = k == 0 ? q1 : k == 1 ? q2 : q3
            float  fl   = k == 0 ? floor1 : k == 1 ? floor2 : floor3
            bool   weak = live and qq < fl
            string gl   = k == 0 ? "①" : k == 1 ? "②" : "③"
            string od   = k == 0 ? o1 : k == 1 ? o2 : o3
            string vTx  = "ρ " + f_num(rho, "+0.00;-0.00") + (live ? " · β* " + f_num(wt, "+0.00;-0.00") : " · out")
            color  vCl  = not live ? color.new(C_SOFT, 55) : weak ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT
            string tip  = "Selection score |ρ| " + f_num(qq, "0.000") + " " + od + " against an admission floor of " +
                 f_num(fl, "0.000") + " on " + str.tostring(nObs) + " anchor observations. " +
                 (not live ? "EXCLUDED - its weight is held at zero." :
                  weak ? "RETAINED BELOW THE FLOOR (gold): a spread needs an anchor driver, so ① is always kept, but this |ρ| is not distinguishable from noise on this sample." :
                  "APPLIED.") +
                 "\n\nρ is its plain correlation with the target, in neutral ink: a negative ρ means it moves inversely, not that anything is bearish. β* is its weight per 1σ of the factor, applied at " +
                 f_num(hedgeW * 100.0, "#") + "% of full strength."
            f_row(tb, 9 + k, " " + gl + " " + array.get(DRV_NAMES, ix), vTx, vCl, tip)

        string dtTxt = str.tostring(drvLagged) + " read a bar late · kept ≤" + f_num(drvKeptH, "0.0") + "h"
        f_row(tb, 12, " driver timing", dtTxt, drvKeptH > 0.0 ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
             "DRIVER TIMING. A driver whose bar closes more than a third of a chart bar after the chart's is read at its previous close - the value known when the chart closed. This row counts how many were read late on this bar, and the largest lateness KEPT, in hours: the most the last bar can still revise by.\n\nGold while any kept lateness is above zero: the live bar's value reading can still move after the chart has closed.")

        // ── DETAIL · conviction's own reading ─────────────────────────────────
        f_sep(tb, 13, "DETAIL")
        string ctrl = osc >=  inpZ2 ? "buyers · extreme" : osc >=  inpZ1 ? "buyers · stretched" : osc >= 0.0 ? "buyers in control" :
                      osc <= -inpZ2 ? "sellers · extreme" : osc <= -inpZ1 ? "sellers · stretched" : "sellers in control"
        f_row(tb, 14, " conviction · control", ready ? f_num(osc, "+#;-#") + " · " + ctrl : "warming",
             not ready ? C_GOLD : osc >= 0.0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR,
             "Chart conviction - the engine behind the histogram, no longer drawn - and who controls this chart: its side of zero, stretched past the inner zone, extreme past the outer. The conviction tape is the same question across the ladder.\n\nNishchaya v3 draws this line, bit-identical.")
        f_row(tb, 15, " raw share · σ", f_num(raw, "+0.0;-0.0") + " · σ " + f_num(rawSd, "0.0") + (quietReg ? " · QUIET" : ""),
             quietReg ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
             "The share before scaling, and the σ it is scaled by. QUIET (gold): that σ is in the bottom fifth of its own long history, so a small imbalance is being amplified - the columns desaturate while it lasts.")
        f_row(tb, 16, " this bar · conviction", f_num(conv * 100.0, "+#;-#") + "% of range",
             conv >= 0.0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR,
             "c = ΔC / TR for this bar alone: how much of its travel became net progress.")
        f_row(tb, 17, " participation", f_num(w, "0.00") + "× · " + wSrc, w >= inpCap ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
             "This bar's participation weight and the branch that ACTUALLY produced it. Gold when it is capped.")
        f_row(tb, 18, " effort → result",
             (na(effShare) ? "—" : f_num(100.0 * effShare, "#") + "%") + " · pct " + f_num(effPct, "#") + (effAbs ? " · ABSORBED" : ""),
             effAbs ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
             "The share of the window's participation that became directional displacement, and where it sits in its own history. ABSORBED (gold, bottom fifth): heavy effort, little result - the exhaustion ▲▼ require somewhere in the dislocation window.")
        f_row(tb, 19, " layer overlap", "C " + f_num(rhoC, "+0.00;-0.00") + " · V " + f_num(rhoV, "+0.00;-0.00"), C_SOFT,
             "Correlation of each tape with chart conviction over the normalization window. Near 1, the tape restates the chart; near 0, it is independent evidence. Shown, not used.")
        f_row(tb, 20, " rungs live", str.tostring(zCnt) + " conv · " + str.tostring(tpCnt) + " value" +
             (multiUp and not na(recErr) ? " · rec " + f_num(recErr, "0.0") + "pt" : ""), C_SOFT,
             "How many rungs each tape is averaging on this bar, the chart included. REC is the reconstruction error of the higher frames, in points, at their last close.")

        if pfRows
            f_sep(tb, 21, "PROFILE")
            f_row(tb, 22, " profile mode",
                 (vpVolOk ? "volume" : vpTpoAuto ? "TPO · no volume" : "TPO") + " · " + (vpIntraOk ? "intrabar " + vpLtf : "bar range"),
                 vpTpoAuto ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
                 "What the profile is ACTUALLY built from. TPO · NO VOLUME (gold): this symbol reports no usable volume, so the profile fell back to time at price.")
            string locTxt = na(poc) ? "—" : (close > nz(vah, close) ? "above value" : close < nz(vaLow, close) ? "below value" : "inside value") +
                 " · POC " + f_num(poc, format.mintick)
            bool outVal = not na(vah) and not na(vaLow) and (close > vah or close < vaLow)
            f_row(tb, 23, " location", locTxt, outVal ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT,
                 "Where price sits against this session's value area. Outside it (gold) is a price the market has not yet accepted.")

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  OPEN INTEREST · WHAT KIND OF PARTICIPATION         (price chart, display only)
//
//  Volume says how much traded. Open interest says whether that trade OPENED
//  positions or CLOSED them - the difference between a move that is being
//  funded and one that ends by itself:
//
//      price ↑  OI ↑   LONG BUILD       new buyers - the move is being funded
//      price ↓  OI ↑   SHORT BUILD      new sellers - the move is being funded
//      price ↑  OI ↓   SHORT COVERING   self-limiting: it ends when the shorts do
//      price ↓  OI ↓   LONG UNWINDING   self-limiting: it ends when the longs do
//
//  Read over a window of OI periods, not a bar: each period is classified,
//  weighted by the size of its OI change, and the window's shares decide the
//  character. On an intraday chart the OI period is the day - published open
//  interest is daily - and the live day reads provisionally until it closes.
//
//  STRUCTURAL JUMPS. A continuous contract rolls; on the roll the series jumps
//  because the contract changed, not because anyone traded. A change beyond
//  OI_JUMP σ of its own history is marked structural and left out of the
//  window, and the table says so in gold.
//
//  THE GOLD CAST. When the push the histogram shows is being made by EXITS -
//  covering into a rally, unwinding into a fall - the columns keep their hue
//  but are cast toward gold: read with caution, the flow is self-limiting.
//  Nothing in the engine or the signals reads open interest.
//
//  The table sits on the PRICE chart (force_overlay), in its own corner, so it
//  never collides with the pane's panel. Text is C_SOFT, never C_INK.
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var string G_OI = "10 · Open interest · price chart"
bool   inpOi     = input.bool(true, "Open interest readout", group=G_OI, tooltip="A small table on the PRICE chart: what kind of participation the window carried - long build, short build, short covering or long unwinding - how much of it was exits, how price and OI moved together, and whether the book is crowded.\n\nNeeds a symbol TradingView publishes open interest for (listed futures, via its _OI companion). Anything else reads 'no OI' and the gold cast stands down.\n\nDisplay only. Stands down entirely in the Core view.", display=display.none)
int    inpOiLen  = input.int(10, "    Window (OI periods)", minval=3, maxval=100, group=G_OI, tooltip="How many OI periods - days on an intraday or daily chart - the character is read over.", display=display.none)
string inpOiPos  = input.string("Bottom right", "    Table position", options=["Top right", "Bottom right", "Bottom left", "Top left"], group=G_OI, display=display.none)
bool   inpOiCast = input.bool(true, "    Gold cast on self-limiting flow", group=G_OI, tooltip="Casts the histogram's columns toward gold while the push they show is being made by exits: an up-push made mostly by short covering, a down-push mostly by long unwinding. The hue is kept - the direction is still the direction - it is the caution that is added.", display=display.none)

float OI_JUMP  = 4.0        // σ of |ΔOI| beyond which a change is structural (a roll)
int   OI_SDLEN = 100        // OI periods that σ is measured over
float OI_EXIT  = 0.60       // exits' share of the window's |ΔOI| for the cast
float OI_CROWD = 90.0       // OI percentile rank that marks a crowded book
int   OI_RANK  = 250        // OI periods that rank is measured over

bool   oiOn     = inpOi and not coreView
// the companion OI series TradingView publishes for listed futures
string oiTicker = syminfo.prefix + ":" + syminfo.ticker + "_OI"
string oiTf     = timeframe.isintraday ? "D" : ""

// Evaluated in the OI period's own context: price comes from the chart symbol
// at that timeframe, open interest from its companion.
f_oiState() =>
    float o    = request.security(oiTicker, "", close, ignore_invalid_symbol = true)
    float dO   = na(o) or na(o[1]) ? na : o - o[1]
    float dP   = close - nz(close[1], close)
    float aO   = math.abs(nz(dO))
    float sdO  = ta.stdev(nz(dO), OI_SDLEN)
    bool  jump = not na(dO) and not na(sdO) and sdO > 0.0 and aO > OI_JUMP * sdO
    float useO = jump or na(dO) ? 0.0 : aO
    float lb   = math.sum(dP > 0.0 and nz(dO) > 0.0 ? useO : 0.0, inpOiLen)
    float sb   = math.sum(dP < 0.0 and nz(dO) > 0.0 ? useO : 0.0, inpOiLen)
    float sc   = math.sum(dP > 0.0 and nz(dO) < 0.0 ? useO : 0.0, inpOiLen)
    float lu   = math.sum(dP < 0.0 and nz(dO) < 0.0 ? useO : 0.0, inpOiLen)
    float rho  = ta.correlation(dP, nz(dO), inpOiLen)
    float rk   = ta.percentrank(o, OI_RANK)
    float jmp  = math.sum(jump ? 1.0 : 0.0, inpOiLen)
    [o, dO, lb, sb, sc, lu, rho, rk, jmp]

float oiLvl = na
float oiD   = na
float oiLB  = 0.0
float oiSB  = 0.0
float oiSC  = 0.0
float oiLU  = 0.0
float oiRho = na
float oiRk  = na
float oiJmp = 0.0
if oiOn
    [o_, d_, lb_, sb_, sc_, lu_, rh_, rk_, jm_] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, oiTf, f_oiState())
    oiLvl := o_
    oiD   := d_
    oiLB  := nz(lb_)
    oiSB  := nz(sb_)
    oiSC  := nz(sc_)
    oiLU  := nz(lu_)
    oiRho := rh_
    oiRk  := rk_
    oiJmp := nz(jm_)

bool   oiHas    = oiOn and not na(oiLvl)
float  oiTot    = oiLB + oiSB + oiSC + oiLU
float  oiExitSh = oiTot > 0.0 ? (oiSC + oiLU) / oiTot : na
bool   oiRoll   = oiHas and oiJmp > 0.0
bool   oiSelf   = oiHas and not na(oiExitSh) and oiExitSh >= OI_EXIT
bool   oiCrowd  = oiHas and not na(oiRk) and oiRk >= OI_CROWD
string oiChar   = not oiHas ? "no OI for this symbol" : oiTot <= 0.0 ? "flat" :
     oiLB >= math.max(oiSB, oiSC, oiLU) ? "long build" :
     oiSB >= math.max(oiLB, oiSC, oiLU) ? "short build" :
     oiSC >= math.max(oiLB, oiSB, oiLU) ? "short covering" : "long unwinding"
color  oiCharCol = not oiHas or oiTot <= 0.0 ? C_SOFT :
     oiChar == "long build" ? C_BULL : oiChar == "short build" ? C_BEAR : C_GOLD

var table oiTb = na
if oiOn and barstate.islast
    if na(oiTb)
        string p = inpOiPos
        oiTb := table.new(p == "Top right" ? position.top_right : p == "Bottom left" ? position.bottom_left :
             p == "Top left" ? position.top_left : position.bottom_right, 2, 6, bgcolor = C_PANEL,
             frame_color = C_GRID, frame_width = 1, border_width = 0, force_overlay = true)
    table.cell(oiTb, 0, 0, "OPEN INTEREST", text_color = C_NEUT, text_halign = text.align_left, text_size = size.tiny)
    table.cell(oiTb, 1, 0, oiHas ? str.tostring(inpOiLen) + (oiTf == "D" ? " days" : " bars") : "", text_color = C_NEUT, text_halign = text.align_right, text_size = size.tiny)
    table.cell(oiTb, 0, 1, " character", text_color = C_SOFT, text_halign = text.align_left, text_size = size.small,
         tooltip = "The window's dominant quadrant, weighted by the size of each period's OI change. BUILD (green / red) is new money funding the move; COVERING and UNWINDING (gold) are exits - self-limiting flow.")
    table.cell(oiTb, 1, 1, oiChar, text_color = oiCharCol, text_halign = text.align_right, text_size = size.small)
    if oiHas
        table.cell(oiTb, 0, 2, " exits · share", text_color = C_SOFT, text_halign = text.align_left, text_size = size.small,
             tooltip = "Covering plus unwinding, as a share of the window's |ΔOI|. Past 60% the flow is mostly positions closing; the histogram is cast gold when that flow is carrying its push.")
        table.cell(oiTb, 1, 2, f_num(100.0 * oiExitSh, "#") + "%", text_color = oiSelf ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT, text_halign = text.align_right, text_size = size.small)
        table.cell(oiTb, 0, 3, " ρ price · OI", text_color = C_SOFT, text_halign = text.align_left, text_size = size.small,
             tooltip = "Correlation of price change with OI change over the window. Positive: rallies open positions and falls close them - longs are driving. Negative: shorts are driving. Neutral ink; the sign is a reading of who, not of direction.")
        table.cell(oiTb, 1, 3, f_num(oiRho, "+0.00;-0.00") + (na(oiRho) ? "" : oiRho >= 0.0 ? " · longs drive" : " · shorts drive"), text_color = C_SOFT, text_halign = text.align_right, text_size = size.small)
        table.cell(oiTb, 0, 4, " book", text_color = C_SOFT, text_halign = text.align_left, text_size = size.small,
             tooltip = "Where open interest sits in its own range over the last 250 periods. CROWDED (gold) past the 90th percentile: many positions that may have to be closed.")
        table.cell(oiTb, 1, 4, "pct " + f_num(oiRk, "#") + (oiCrowd ? " · crowded" : ""), text_color = oiCrowd ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT, text_halign = text.align_right, text_size = size.small)
        table.cell(oiTb, 0, 5, " structure", text_color = C_SOFT, text_halign = text.align_left, text_size = size.small,
             tooltip = "A structural jump - a contract roll - inside the window, left out of every reading above.")
        table.cell(oiTb, 1, 5, oiRoll ? "jump in window · excluded" : "clean", text_color = oiRoll ? C_GOLD : C_SOFT, text_halign = text.align_right, text_size = size.small)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  PANE
//
//  Declaration order is draw order: the background, the value action zones and
//  rails, the histogram BEHIND, the trace IN FRONT, then the markers and the
//  tapes. A line in front of columns stays whole; columns in front of a line
//  cut it into dashes.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ── the histogram: hue = push, lightness = phase, saturation = real or quiet ──
// Gold cast: the push is being made by exits (open interest), self-limiting.
bool  oiCast  = inpOiCast and oiSelf and ready and (hAbove ? oiSC > oiLU : oiLU > oiSC)
color hHue    = hAbove ? C_BULL : C_BEAR
if quietReg
    hHue := f_mix(hHue, C_NEUT, 0.5)
if oiCast
    hHue := f_mix(hHue, C_GOLD, 0.45)
color cImpulse  = color.new(hHue, 0)
color cPressing = color.new(hHue, 35)
color cDecel    = color.new(hHue, 55)
color cTurning  = color.new(hHue, 78)
color histCol   = not ready ? color.new(C_NEUT, 70) :
     not useSlope ? (hExpand ? cImpulse : cTurning) :
     hExpand ? (hHi ? cImpulse : cPressing) : trWith ? cDecel : cTurning

// ── the trace: hue = side of fair, intensity = distance, saturation = coherence
bool  vSoft = legsSplit or not svTradable
float vAbs  = math.abs(unified_osc)
color vHue  = unified_osc >= 0.0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR
if vSoft
    vHue := f_mix(vHue, C_NEUT, 0.55)
color vLo    = color.from_gradient(vAbs, 0.0, osc_entry, color.new(vHue, 55), color.new(vHue, 30))
color vHi    = color.from_gradient(vAbs, osc_entry, RAIL_OUT, color.new(vHue, 15), color.new(vHue, 0))
color valCol = not inpTraceZone ? color.new(vHue, 10) : vAbs >= osc_entry ? vHi : vLo

// ── the histogram's display scale: σ-pinned, 3σ lands on θ ───────────────────
float hDisp = inpHScale == "σ-pinned · 3σ = θ" and ready and nz(histSd) > 1e-9 ? osc_entry / (3.0 * histSd) : inpHGain

// ── background: the standing declaration (opt-in) ────────────────────────────
bgcolor(tintLong ? color.new(C_BULL, 94) : tintShort ? color.new(C_BEAR, 94) : na, title = "Declaration")

// ── value action zones and rails: the pane's scale belongs to the trace ───────
// θ is constant for a run, so its rails and zones are DRAWINGS extended both
// ways, redrawn on the last bar - they cost nothing from the 64-plot budget,
// where four plots and two fills would have cost up to twelve. The ±70 rails
// and zero are hlines, which cost nothing either and hold the pane's scale.
var line thLnU = na
var line thLnD = na
var box  zoneU = na
var box  zoneD = na
if barstate.islast
    line.delete(thLnU)
    line.delete(thLnD)
    box.delete(zoneU)
    box.delete(zoneD)
    thLnU := line.new(bar_index - 1,  osc_entry, bar_index,  osc_entry, extend = extend.both, color = color.new(C_BEAR, 40), width = 1)
    thLnD := line.new(bar_index - 1, -osc_entry, bar_index, -osc_entry, extend = extend.both, color = color.new(C_BULL, 40), width = 1)
    if inpFill
        zoneU := box.new(bar_index - 1,  RAIL_OUT, bar_index,  osc_entry, border_color = na, extend = extend.both, bgcolor = color.new(C_BEAR, 94))
        zoneD := box.new(bar_index - 1, -osc_entry, bar_index, -RAIL_OUT, border_color = na, extend = extend.both, bgcolor = color.new(C_BULL, 94))
hline( RAIL_OUT, "Outer rail · bearish R H", color.new(C_BEAR, 55), hline.style_dashed, 1)
hline(-RAIL_OUT, "Outer rail · bullish R H", color.new(C_BULL, 55), hline.style_dashed, 1)
hline(0.0, "Zero · fair value", color.new(C_NEUT, 55), hline.style_solid, 1)

// ── the histogram, behind ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
plot(inpHist ? hist * hDisp : na, "Histogram", histCol, 1, plot.style_columns, histbase = 0.0, display = display.pane)
// A literal colour on purpose: a theme-aware one would cost two plots each.
plot(inpThr and inpHist ?  thr * hDisp : na, "Impulse threshold +", color.new(#7D8A93, 45), 1, display = display.pane)
plot(inpThr and inpHist ? -thr * hDisp : na, "Impulse threshold −", color.new(#7D8A93, 45), 1, display = display.pane)

// ── the trace, in front ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Not drawn until the value model is built: a flat line at zero would read as
// a neutral statement about the market.
plot(modelBuilt ? unified_osc : na, "Value trace (Samanvaya)", valCol, 2)

// ── Data Window: the engine's own numbers ────────────────────────────────────
// The signal line is conviction − the native histogram. Effort → result and the
// reconstruction error are in the panel's DETAIL rows.
plot(hist, "Histogram (native)", display = display.data_window)
plot(ready ? osc : na, "Conviction (engine)", display = display.data_window)
plot(multi and ladReady ? ladAgree : na, "MTF conviction", display = display.data_window)
plot(tpReady ? tpBias : na, "MTF value (+ rich / − cheap)", display = display.data_window)

// ── the tapes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Each is one column plot whose base sits TAPE_H below it: a band, not a line.
// Hue is direction, INTENSITY how far it has run, graded on each tape's own
// thresholds; grey while warming. No gold on a tape.
const float TAPE_Y = 90.0
const float TAPE_H = 3.0

bool  showCT = inpTape != "Off" and multi
float agAbs  = math.abs(ladAgree)
color agHue  = ladAgree >= 0.0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR
color agLo   = color.from_gradient(agAbs, 0.0, inpZ1, color.new(agHue, 88), color.new(agHue, 55))
color agHi   = color.from_gradient(agAbs, inpZ1, inpZ2, color.new(agHue, 30), color.new(agHue, 0))
color agCol  = not mtfCOk ? color.new(C_NEUT, 75) : agAbs >= inpZ1 ? agHi : agLo

bool  showVT = inpVLad != "Off" and tpOn
float vaAbs  = math.abs(tpBias)
color vaHue  = tpBias >= 0.0 ? C_BULL : C_BEAR
color vaLo   = color.from_gradient(vaAbs, 0.0, osc_entry, color.new(vaHue, 88), color.new(vaHue, 55))
color vaHi   = color.from_gradient(vaAbs, osc_entry, RAIL_OUT, color.new(vaHue, 30), color.new(vaHue, 0))
color vaCol  = not mtfVOk ? color.new(C_NEUT, 75) : vaAbs >= osc_entry ? vaHi : vaLo

plot(showCT ?  TAPE_Y + TAPE_H : na, "MTF conviction tape", agCol, 1, plot.style_columns, histbase =  TAPE_Y - TAPE_H, display = display.pane)
plot(showVT ? -TAPE_Y + TAPE_H : na, "MTF value tape",      vaCol, 1, plot.style_columns, histbase = -TAPE_Y - TAPE_H, display = display.pane)

// ── right edge: θ tags, tape names, and why the stack is paused ───────────────
var label thULb   = na
var label thDLb   = na
var label tapeCLb = na
var label tapeVLb = na
var label stackLb = na
if barstate.islast
    label.delete(thULb)
    label.delete(thDLb)
    label.delete(tapeCLb)
    label.delete(tapeVLb)
    label.delete(stackLb)
    int xr = bar_index + 2
    string thTxt = "θ " + f_num(entryThresh, "0.0")
    thULb := label.new(xr,  osc_entry, thTxt + " ▼", style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(C_NEUT, 100),
         textcolor = color.new(C_BEAR, 25), size = size.tiny)
    thDLb := label.new(xr, -osc_entry, thTxt + " ▲", style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(C_NEUT, 100),
         textcolor = color.new(C_BULL, 25), size = size.tiny)
    if showCT
        string cTxt = "MTF CONVICTION " + (ladFallback ? "↺ " : "") + (multiUp ? "↑ " : "↓ ") + ladLbl + " · " +
             (mtfCOk ? (ladAgree >= 0.0 ? "buyers " : "sellers ") + f_num(ladAgree, "+#;-#") : "warming")
        tapeCLb := label.new(xr, TAPE_Y, cTxt, style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(C_NEUT, 100),
             textcolor = mtfCOk ? color.new(agHue, 10) : C_NEUT, size = size.tiny)
    if showVT
        string vTxt = "MTF VALUE " + (tpFallback ? "↺ " : "") + (tpMultiUp ? "↑ " : "↓ ") + tpLadLbl + " · " +
             (mtfVOk ? (tpBias >= 0.0 ? "rich " : "cheap ") + f_num(tpBias, "+#;-#") : "warming")
        tapeVLb := label.new(xr, -TAPE_Y, vTxt, style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(C_NEUT, 100),
             textcolor = mtfVOk ? color.new(vaHue, 10) : C_NEUT, size = size.tiny)
    if (inpState or inpCon or inpDiv or inpHid) and not stackOk
        stackLb := label.new(xr, 0.0, "SIGNALS PAUSED · " + stackWhy, style = label.style_label_left,
             color = color.new(C_GOLD, 80), textcolor = C_GOLD, size = size.tiny)

// ── markers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Bearish on the upper rails, bullish on the lower. Threshold rails: R H on the
// outer dashed lines, ▲▼ △▽ ◆ on θ. On the trace: at the trace's reading.
bool  rails = inpPlace == "Threshold rails"
float yDivU = rails ?  RAIL_OUT  : unified_osc
float yDivD = rails ? -RAIL_OUT  : unified_osc
float ySigU = rails ?  osc_entry : unified_osc
float ySigD = rails ? -osc_entry : unified_osc

plotshape(divBear ? yDivU : na, "R · bearish", shape.labeldown, location.absolute, f_q(C_BEAR, 0),  text = "R", textcolor = C_INK, size = size.auto, display = display.pane)
plotshape(divBull ? yDivD : na, "R · bullish", shape.labelup,   location.absolute, f_q(C_BULL, 0),  text = "R", textcolor = C_INK, size = size.auto, display = display.pane)
plotshape(hidBear ? yDivU : na, "H · bearish", shape.labeldown, location.absolute, f_q(C_BEAR, 45), text = "H", textcolor = C_INK, size = size.auto, display = display.pane)
plotshape(hidBull ? yDivD : na, "H · bullish", shape.labelup,   location.absolute, f_q(C_BULL, 45), text = "H", textcolor = C_INK, size = size.auto, display = display.pane)
plotshape(sellSig ? ySigU : na, "▼ SELL", shape.triangledown, location.absolute, f_q(C_BEAR, 0), size = size.auto, display = display.pane)
plotshape(buySig  ? ySigD : na, "▲ BUY",  shape.triangleup,   location.absolute, f_q(C_BULL, 0), size = size.auto, display = display.pane)
// both continuation markers share one plot - the budget
plotshape(conLong ? ySigD : conShort ? ySigU : na, "◆ continuation", shape.diamond, location.absolute,
     conLong ? f_q(C_BULL, 30) : f_q(C_BEAR, 30), size = size.tiny, display = display.pane)

// △ ▽ the arm: value turned. Hidden on the bar its own ▲▼ fires. Fixed colour -
// a value event does not fade in a quiet conviction regime, and it saves plots.
bool armMarkUp = inpArm and inpState and stackOk and armBuyNow  and not buySig
bool armMarkDn = inpArm and inpState and stackOk and armSellNow and not sellSig
plotchar(armMarkDn ? ySigU : na, "Arm · ▽", "▽", location.absolute, color.new(C_BEAR, 15), size = size.small, display = display.pane)
plotchar(armMarkUp ? ySigD : na, "Arm · △", "△", location.absolute, color.new(C_BULL, 15), size = size.small, display = display.pane)

// ○ the lean (opt-in): on the trace where the signal line crossed it
plotchar(inpLean and (leanShort or leanLong) ? unified_osc : na, "Lean · ○", "○", location.absolute,
     leanShort ? color.new(C_BEAR, 20) : color.new(C_BULL, 20), size = size.tiny, display = display.pane)

// ⟳ basket rotation, gold, on the zero line
plotchar(inpRot and confirmed and modelBuilt and rotated ? 0.0 : na, "Basket rotation · ⟳", "⟳", location.absolute,
     color.new(C_GOLD, 15), size = size.tiny, display = display.pane)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ALERTS · closed bars only
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
alertcondition(buySig,  "Pragati ▲ BUY",  "Pragati ▲ BUY declared on {{ticker}} {{interval}} - the trace - Samanvaya's value on this chart - crossed back up through −θ, and cheap-across-the-ladder, effort exhausted and buyers working confirmed it. A declaration, not a trade.")
alertcondition(sellSig, "Pragati ▼ SELL", "Pragati ▼ SELL declared on {{ticker}} {{interval}} - the trace - Samanvaya's value on this chart - crossed back down through +θ, and rich-across-the-ladder, effort exhausted and sellers working confirmed it. A declaration, not a trade.")
alertcondition(conLong,  "Pragati ◆ continuation long",  "Pragati ◆ long on {{ticker}} {{interval}} - buyers held control across the ladder, the pullback left value fair or cheap, and the push resumed past its impulse gate.")
alertcondition(conShort, "Pragati ◆ continuation short", "Pragati ◆ short on {{ticker}} {{interval}} - sellers held control across the ladder, the pullback left value fair or rich, and the push resumed past its impulse gate.")
alertcondition(divBear, "Pragati R bearish divergence", "Pragati R bearish on {{ticker}} {{interval}} - price made a higher high, conviction and the ladder a lower one, at a price rich across horizons.")
alertcondition(divBull, "Pragati R bullish divergence", "Pragati R bullish on {{ticker}} {{interval}} - price made a lower low, conviction and the ladder a higher one, at a price cheap across horizons.")
alertcondition(hidBear, "Pragati H hidden bearish", "Pragati H bearish on {{ticker}} {{interval}} - price made a lower high while conviction made a higher one, with sellers in control across the ladder. Unmeasured edge.")
alertcondition(hidBull, "Pragati H hidden bullish", "Pragati H bullish on {{ticker}} {{interval}} - price made a higher low while conviction made a lower one, with buyers in control across the ladder. Unmeasured edge.")
// The lean's alerts use alert(), which does not count toward the plot budget.
// They fire only while the lean is on, through an 'Any alert() function call'
// alert; leanShort and leanLong are already committed on closed bars.
if inpLean and leanShort
    alert("Pragati ○ on " + syminfo.ticker + " " + timeframe.period + " - the signal line crossed ABOVE the trace: SHORT lean. Measured +0.0273R in Samanvaya, smaller than a daily round-trip cost - a lean, not an entry.", alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)
if inpLean and leanLong
    alert("Pragati ○ on " + syminfo.ticker + " " + timeframe.period + " - the signal line crossed BELOW the trace: LONG lean. Measured +0.0273R in Samanvaya, smaller than a daily round-trip cost - a lean, not an entry.", alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)
